{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '160,320p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API.md && cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md\"","aggregated_output":"- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`, `dot`, `line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, width)`, `disc(g, x, y, r, c)`, `ell`, `poly(g, pts, c)`, `polyDither(g, pts, c, 0..1, r.ix, r.iy)`, `ddisc`, `sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip)` (or `{ flip, rot, scale }`), `glow(g, 0..1)` (GPU glow for what follows).\n- Sprites from strings (for coins, bullets, ships, icons, NES-style enemies): `const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }, scale)`. `.` and space are transparent. Frames: `const walk = E.anim([f1, f2, f3], 8, { pingpong })` (or one sheet: `E.anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 })`), drawn with `r.sprite(x, y, z, walk, { t })`. `r.sprite(..., { rot: angle, scale })` turns and scales in whole pixels (cached in 16 steps a turn; `steps: 32`); `E.recolor(spr, { '#3a8a4a': '#8a3a4a' })` swaps a palette.\n- Font (5x7 proportional with lower case: ASCII, the face buttons, arrows, ★ ♥ ♪ ✓ … ° € £, the accented Latin letters; any other is `?`; `font: 'tiny'` for 3x5): `E.font.text(g, str, x, y, color, { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font })`, `E.font.title(g, str, x, y, { scale, colors, outline, depth, depthColor, align })`, `E.font.width(str, opts)`, `E.font.lineHeight(opts)`, `E.font.wrap(str, maxW, opts)`. Glyphs: ASCII plus `♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • × ♪ ▸ ©`.\n- UI (inside `r.overlay(g => ...)`): `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, gradient, alpha })` (a gradient window like Final Fantasy; `bg` can be `[top, bottom]`, `gradient: false` for flat, `alpha: .6` for a see-through HUD panel), `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, color)`, `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max)` (half hearts allowed), `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size, { zoom: 3, turn: .6, bg, frame, cape: false, weapon: false, tint: '#808080' })` (character close-up; it stays upright while the rig kneels or lies down; `tint` greys out a KO'd hero).\n- Dialog: `const talk = new E.Dialog(game)`; `talk.say(['Line one.', 'Page two.'], { name: 'ELDER', portrait: elderRig, onDone, choices: ['YES', 'NO'], onChoice(i) })` (choices show on the last page: the keys, or a tap or click on one, pick); in update `if (talk.update(dt)) return;` (true while a modal box is open: play waits); in draw `talk.draw(r)`. `portrait` takes a Humanoid (live close-up; `portraitSize: 40`, `portraitOpts: { zoom, turn }`) or a function `(g, x, y, size) => {...}`. Radio chatter during play: `talk.say('Enemy fighters ahead!', { name, portrait, auto: 2.5, modal: false })` turns its pages and closes by itself while the game keeps running. Per line: `place: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom'`, `y` (pixels) and `alpha` (see-through box). `new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, color, nameColor, voice, width, bg, border, vars })` (`voice`: a sound, or `(ch, i, talk) => {}` per letter); `talk.close()`, `talk.open`. Markup in the text: `{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}` a color, `{w=.5}` a pause, `{s=12}slowly{/s}` letters a second, `{shake}..{/shake}`, `{wave}..{/wave}`, `{$name}` a value from `say(text, { vars })` or `talk.vars`.\n- Menu: `const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', { label: 'LOAD', disabled: true }], { x: 'center', y: 100, title, onPick(i, item), onCancel, scale: 2, rows: 6 })` (rows: it scrolls); `menu.update(dt)`; `menu.draw(r)`. Keys, d-pad and sticks move it; a click or tap on an entry picks it (so a phone can leave a title), and the mouse over one moves the cursor. A tap on a dialog box turns its page as confirm does.\n- HTML screens and HUD: `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { scale: 2 } (a TV; or `tv: true`), settings, screens: { pause, help, dev: { rows: ['glyphs'] } } })`; `ui.show('title' | 'results' | 'map' | ..., spec)`; each step `ui.hud({ objective, status, bars, prompt, minimap })` (`minimap: { map, fog, camera: true, markers, onClick(x, y) }`); `ui.toast(title, text)`.\n\n## Sound: `game.audio` (chip synth, no files, starts after the first key press)\n- `sfx(name, { vol, pitch, pan, loop })` → `{ stop(), set({ pan, vol }) }` (a loop sounds on: an engine, rain). Presets: `jump jump2 land step coin pickup key powerup oneup heal hit hurt stomp bump swing whoosh punch kick shoot laser charge explode boom door secret warp die select confirm cancel pause text blip`.\n- Custom: `sfx({ wave: 'square' | 'pulse' | 'pulse12' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise', freq: 440 or 'A4', to: 880, dur: .2, vol: .3, arp: [0, 4, 7], step: .05, vib: [8, .03] })`, or `audio.define('zap', voice)`; `audio.instrument('pluck', (ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) => ...)` is a `wave` too.\n- Music: `music('title' | 'adventure' | 'dungeon' | 'boss' | 'victory')` (built in; any other name warns and plays nothing), or your own `{ bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [{ wave: 'square', vol: .15, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 . . . | ...' }, { wave: 'triangle', notes: '...' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h .' }] }`. Tokens: a note (`C4`, `F#3`, `Bb2`), `-` holds, `.` rests, `|` is ignored; drums `k s h o c t`. `music(null)` stops. `mute()`, `setVolume(0..1)` (or set `audio.musicVolume`, `sfxVolume`, then `audio.applyVolume()`), `duck(on)` (quieter music under your own pause or map screen). Your own Web Audio connects to `audio.bus('sfx' | 'music' | 'voice')`, under the volume and mute.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time, headless too, and the sound follows it. `music(song, { sync: true })` pauses with the game (else it plays on); `audio.pos` → `{ t, step, beat, bar, at, loop, bpm, layers, playing }`; `at(step, fn)`, `onBeat(fn)` (on the update step); `tempo(bpm)`; `layer(name or index, vol, fade)` (a track's `name`, `on: false` starts it silent: the chase's drums in by layers); `music(song, { fade: 1 })` crossfades; `sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` over the music; `sfx(voice, { step })` on a step of the music. `settings.audioOffset` (ms, Settings, Sound: Calibrate) sets `audio.offset`; `audio.calibrate()` → `{ tap(), result, done, stop() }`.\n\n## Levels\n**TileMap** (top-down, isometric, brawler): walls stand on the ground plane.\n- `new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorTex, block: ['water', 'lava'], ao: 5, outside })` (`ao` = width of the contact shadow along wall bases, 0 = none; `outside`: the ground past the map, a color, or `false` for none). `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut: true, cutH, face, roof, beam }` (`cut` lowers walls that block the camera, those `map.isFront(cx, cy, view)` says; a map may replace it; `face`: `'brick'` (default) `'stone' 'rock' 'plank' 'timber' 'plaster' 'hedge' 'none'`; `roof`: `'speckle'` (default) `'plain' 'tiles' 'slab' 'leaves' 'grass'`; `beam` = timber color). `floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view)` returns `[r, g, b]` (or `[r, g, b, glow]`); ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`. A map of more than 128 x 128 cells bakes its floor in squares as the camera comes to them and lets the far ones go (`chunks: true | false` to choose; `map.floorBytes()` what it holds), so a 256 x 256 world map costs what the screen shows.\n- Legend values: a number = wall type, a string = spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn, block }` for both. `block: true` (or the map's `block` list of floor tags) makes deep water, lava or pits stop walkers (jumping bodies pass over); `walkable(cx, cy)`, `block(cx, cy, on)` (a bridge appears). FlowField paths go around blocked cells and follow `block` and `set` at their next `update`.\n- `find(tag)` / `findAll(tag)` give `{ x, y, z, cx, cy }`. Also `cell(cx, cy)`, `set(cx, cy, id)` (open doors), `toCell(x, y)`, `center(cx, cy)`, `heightAt(x, y)`, `floorAt(x, y)`, `groundAt(x, y, r, z)`, `collide(body)`, `los(x0, y0, x1, y1)` (ground only), `bounds(view)` (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, buffer pixels), `drawFloor(r)`, `queueWalls(r)`.\n\n**Heights** (collision, standing, sight, reach and navigation share one rule): a body's `z` is its feet; a cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)` (0 = floor); a body stands on the highest top within `z + 2`. Eyes at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) see a target at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22); a body's own `eye` / `chest` override them (crouching). Same level: `|dz| <= 8`. On a TileMap (`dir` = an angle or `[dx, dy]`): `ray(a, b, { eye, target })` → `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, `sight(a, b)`, `standable(x, y, z, r)`, `ledge(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge }` (climb), `landing(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, d }` (a jump across a gap), `drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`, `arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`, `pick(sx, sy)` → `{ cx, cy, z, face }` (the cell under a screen point: `input.clicked()`, any view and turn). Helpers: `E.sameLevel(a, b)`, `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, dz, sight: map })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]` nearest first, `E.inArc(..., dz)`. `new E.FlowField(map, { climb, drop, jump })` paths over roofs, between levels and across gaps.\n\n**PlatformMap** (side-scrollers; 2.5D in tilted views): tiles on the x/z plane, top row first.\n- `new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types })`. `types[id] = { kind, style, side, top, line, mat }` (`mat`: any material for a solid tile's face). Example: `rows: ['      ?    ', '   ===     ', '@  g   ^^  ', '###########']`, `legend: { '#': 1, '=': 2, '?': 3, '^': 4, '@': 'hero', 'g': 'walker' }`, `types: { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#8a5a32' }, 2: { kind: 'oneway' }, 3: { style: 'bonus', side: '#e8a830' }, 4: { kind: 'hazard' } }`. Kinds: `'solid'` (default), `'oneway'` (jump up through), `'ladder'`, `'slope'` (`dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left; `from` / `to` heights 0..1 for gentle slopes), `'hazard'` (spikes, lava), `'deco'`, `'back'` (background wall). Styles: `'stone'` (`moss`) `'brick' 'wood' 'metal' 'ground'` (`flower`) `'block' 'pipe' 'bonus' 'plank' 'spikes' 'ladder' 'liquid' 'plain'`.\n- Spawns: `find(tag)` gives `{ x, z }` with z = the bottom of that cell (feet). Standing on the ground means z = the top of the floor tiles (`level.groundBelow(x, z)`, which returns `null` over a pit), not z = 0. `cell(cx, cz)` / `set(cx, cz, id)` count cz from the bottom row. `slopeZ(cx, cz, x)`, `touching(body, 'hazard')`, `cellsTouching(body, kind)`, `groundBelow(x, z)`, `solidAt(x, z)`, `move(body, dt, solids)`, `bounds(view)`, `draw(r)` (call first, after `r.sky`), `width`, `height`.\n\n## Movement and physics\n**Platformer** (side-scroller character): `const hero = new E.Platformer({ x, z, w: 8, h: 22, run: 95, jump: 285, gravity: 800, airJumps: 0, wallJump: false, dash: 0 })`.\n- Each update: `hero.update(dt, level, game.input, movingPlatforms)`. For enemies pass `{ x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash }` instead of the input.\n- Read: `onGround`, `jumped`, `landed`, `facing` (1 or -1), `air`, `climbing`, `hitWall`, `hitCeiling`, `bumped` (`{ cx, cz, id }` of a block hit from below). Methods: `knock(dir)`, `rigState(extra)` (feed it to `rig.update`).\n- Built in: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height (let go early for a short hop), slopes and small steps (sticks to the ground going down), ladders (up / down), drop through one-way tiles (down + jump), optional double jump, wall jump and dash.\n- Further moves, each on request: `crouch` (down: half as tall, it crawls), `ledge` (catches a ledge falling past it; up climbs), `wallCling`, `momentum` (slopes, a roll, a charged dash); tile kinds `'water'` (it swims), `'rope'` (up grabs it), `'bars'` (it hangs and travels), grip `friction` (ice .15), `carry` (a conveyor), `bounce` (a spring), `crumble` and `respawn` (`level.update(dt)` each step); `hero.swingFrom(x, z)` (a grapple: a pendulum, jump lets go); draw `level.drawWater(r)` after the actors. State: `crouching`, `hanging`, `clinging`, `swimming`, `onRope`, `onBars`, `swinging`, `rolling`; `rigState()` poses them.\n- Moving platforms: `{ x, z, w, h, vx, vz }` boxes you move yourself and pass as `solids`; riders are carried.\n\n**Body** (top-down / isometric / brawler): `new E.Body({ x, y, r: 5, friction: 0, bounce: 0, gravity: 700 })`. Set `vx`, `vy`, then `body.update(dt, map)`. Also `push(ix, iy, iz)` (knockback), `jump(v)`, and `onGround`, `z`, `groundZ`, `hitWall`, `landed`, `bounced` (hit the ground and bounced: dust, a thud). Walls lower than your feet are walkable, so bodies can jump onto blocks.\n\n**Mover** (third person): `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y })`; `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` (input `'THIRDPERSON'`) or an AI's `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`: it sprints, sneaks, climbs, vaults, leaps gaps, hops down and rolls; `climbAny: true` climbs any wall (jump at it; up the screen is up the wall; jump leaps up, crouch lets go; `mover.wall`, `map.wall(from, dir)`), `glide: true` glides (jump again in the air; `lift`: an updraft), `staminaMax: 100` (climbing, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina`); `rig.update(dt, mover.rigState())`; `mover.play('takedown', { other })`, your own `E.ACTIONS`. Stealth: `E.sees(guard, hero, { map })` → `{ seen, exposure }`, `E.hears`, `new E.Suspicion()` (`update(dt, seen, hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'`), `r.sightCone(guard, { map })`, `r.marker(x, y, z, { label })`.\n\n**Attack** (melee timing for swords, punches, kicks): `const slash = new E.Attack('slash')` (a move from `E.MOVES`, optionally with overrides: `new E.Attack('thrust', { reach: 10 })`) or a spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, wind, active, recover }`. `if (input.buffered('attack') && slash.start()) input.consume('attack')`; `slash.update(dt)` (returns the phase that just began); `slash.hits(enemies, e => E.inArc(hero, facing, e, 22, 1.4), e => {...})` hits each target once per swing during `'active'` (moves wait until `hitAt: .35` of the swing, so hit-stop freezes the strike, not the wind-up); `rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: slash.state })`. Also `busy`, `active`, `cancel()`. **Combo**: `const combo = new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'], { window: .3 })` (names, specs or Attacks); `combo.press()` starts or chains the next hit; `update`, `hits`, `state`, `step` work the same. **Knockback**: `E.knockback(attacker, target, 160, up)` pushes a Body (or anything with vx / vy) away.\n\n**Bullets**: `const shots = new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'ground' | 'side' })`; `shots.fire({ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r: 2, team: 'player', color, life, dmg, pierce, sprite })`; `shots.burst(base, E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed))`; `shots.update(dt, map)`; `shots.hit(targets, (b, t) => {...}, 'player')`; `shots.draw(r)`; `shots.clear(team)`. Patterns: `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`, `aim(a, b, speed)`, `aimSide(a, b, speed)`, `spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`, `ring(n, speed, offset)`. In side view the second number of a pattern velocity is vz.\n\n**Helpers**: `E.dist(a, b)`, `E.angleTo(a, b)`, `E.arc(a, b, gravity, { apex | time | speed })` → `{ vx, vy, vz, t }` (a throw that lands at b: a lob, a grenade), `E.overlap(a, b)` (circles x, y, r), `E.overlapBox(a, b)` (side boxes x center, z feet, w, h), `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` (melee cone; `dz` limits the height difference), `E.rand(a, b)`, `E.randInt(a, b)`, `E.pick(list)`, `E.chance(p)`, `E.prune(list, fn)`, `E.store.get(key, fallback)` / `set(key, value)` (save data; slots: `game.saves`), `new E.SpatialHash(32)` (`build(list)`, `near(x, y, r, fn)`), `new E.FlowField(map)` (`update(tx, ty)` or `update([[x, y], ...])`: the nearest; `dir(x, y, tx, ty)` walks around walls and `map.solid` shapes), math `clamp lerp approach ease.* angDiff approachAng smoothDamp rng(seed) noise2 hex shade mix`; `E.version` (the release) and `E.versionLabel()` ('v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07': show it on a title or a debug screen).\n\n## Characters\n**Humanoid** (knights, heroes, soldiers, zombies): `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'chibi' (default) | 'heroic' (tall) | 'bulky' (brawlers, bosses) | 'skeleton' (tint with colors.bone), weapon: 'sword' (the default) | 'gun' | 'staff' | null (fists) | a game's own (`E.Humanoid.weapon(id, { length, rest, draw(w) })`; `.hat(id, draw)`, `.pose(id, (J, w) => ...)`; `draw: { under, over }`; state `tilt`, `roll`), outfit: 'shirt' | 'tunic' | 'robe' | 'coat', hair: 'short' | 'spiky' | 'long' | 'ponytail' | 'bald', hat: 'cap' | 'pointed' | 'helmet' | 'band' | 'crown' (or { style, color }), sleeves: 'short' | 'long' | 'none', armor, hood (in colors.hair), cape: { len: 6, width: 5, seg: 2.5 }, eyeGlow, hunch, lean, style: 'hd' (default) | 'classic' (stick figures for huge crowds), colors: { skin, hair, cloth, coat, pants, boot, belt, trim, glove, cape, capeIn, metal, plate, hilt, eye, iris } })` (`metal` colors the weapon, `plate` the armor, defaulting to `metal`).\n- `rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, aim, attack })`. `facing` is an angle (side view: 0 = right, `Math.PI` = left). `air: true` tucks the legs for jumps; `point: true` holds a gun forward; `aim` tilts it (radians, + = up: `aim: .8` shoots diagonally up, `Math.PI / 2` straight up).\n- **Moves** (`E.MOVES`, use them instead of hand-made specs; every one has anticipation, a committed strike with lunge / step / lean / twist, follow-through and a clean return to the stance, on every build and in every view):\n  - blades and clubs: `slash backslash overhead rising thrust spin plunge twohand`;\n  - fists: `jab` (left hand) `cross hook uppercut haymaker elbow`;\n  - kicks: `kick roundhouse sweep flyingkick knee axekick`;\n  - other: `cast` (both hands) `throw bash claw`.\n  Give each character its own set (a knight: `overhead`, `thrust`, `spin`; a mage: `cast`; a monk: `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse`; a boss: `haymaker`, `sweep`). `E.move('uppercut', .5)` is the attack state of any move at any moment (previews, cutscenes).\n- `attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }`, spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, r0, r1, hand, rel, blade, spin, kick, plane, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`. Angles are relative to facing (+ = right hand side). `rel: true`: heights relative to the shoulder and reach scaled by arm length (kick heights as a share of hip height). `r0`/`r1`: reach that extends (thrusts, jabs). `hand: 'L' | 'both'`. In side views horizontal swings turn into the screen plane (a Castlevania chop) by themselves; `plane: 'side' | 'ground'` (or the rig option `swingPlane`) chooses. `lunge` steps into the strike (body motion in local units, clamped to -4..5; to move the character itself, add speed to your body), `hop` lifts the body, `crouch` dips in the wind-up, `hold` is how long the end pose holds (share of recover). You time the phases.\n- In steep ground views (threequarter) rigs and Blobs are drawn from a lower, sprite-like angle so faces show, like SNES RPG sprites (`charView: false` turns it off for one rig, `E.style.charPitch = false` for all).\n- Faces (eyes with catch-lights, brows, mouth), a cheated 3/4 turn toward the camera in side views (`cheat: .5`, 0 turns it off) and hair layers are automatic. `face: { eyes: 'big' (anime / Mana style), bangs: 0..1 }`.\n- Poses: `rig.update(dt, { ..., pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' })` (they blend in and out), `'block'` (weapon upright across the body), `'die'` (a stagger, then the body topples), `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'` (faces and portraits), `run: 0..1` (run in place), or `down: 0..1` to lie a defeated body flat. `stance: 'guard'` keeps fists up while walking (brawlers; the free hand guards the chin during punches), `stance: 'ready'` holds the weapon forward. `climb: true` with `vz` climbs a ladder hand over hand (`Platformer.rigState()` passes both). Traversal: `crouch` (a sneaking gait), `sprint` (a lean), `hang` with `hangZ` (hands on an edge that high above the feet), `flip: 0..1` (a roll), `land: k` (a heavy landing); `mover.rigState()` sets them. Every wind-up starts from the rig's current pose, so chained hits and hand changes never snap. Idle rigs breathe and shift their weight by themselves.\n- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)` inside `r.actor`, `rig.drawSmear(r)` (a glowing trail along the real blade, fist or foot path, in every view), `rig.debug(r)` (skeleton), `rig.kick(v)` (squash and stretch), `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()` (world points to spawn bullets from), `rig.worldOffset(p)` (where a point in the rig's own frame, such as a joint in `rig.J`, sits from its feet: to draw or spawn at any joint), `rig.lastView` (the view it last drew with), `rig.solvePose()` (sets `rig.J` from its state each step: wrap it on one rig to move joints).\n**Blob** (slimes, rabbites, bats, imps, round monsters): `new E.Blob({ R: 6, colors: { dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil }, ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true, feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' })`, `update(dt, { squash: -0.4..0.4, look: [dx, dy], squint, walk: 0..1, flap: 0..1 (wing speed; 0 folds them), hang: true (roost upside down) })`, `kick(v)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`. Mix the parts for a bestiary: `wings` + `mouth: 'fangs'` is a bat, `ears: 'rabbit'` + `feet` a Mana rabbite, `horns` + `tail` an imp.\n\n## Effects and lighting\n- Particles: `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` (fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, ring, shake, sound), `fire(x, y, z, n, { size })`, `smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color })`, `glints(x, y, z, n, color)`, `dust(x, y, z, n)`, `sparks(x, y, z, n, angle)`, `bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`, `ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur)`, `text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true })` (bouncing damage numbers), `add({ kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'fire' | 'smoke' | 'glint', ... })`. `explosion(x, y, z, size, { debris, freeze: false, shake: 0 })` for chains of small blasts. Side-scrollers: `particles.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z)` so sparks and debris bounce on platforms. Screen flash: `game.flash('#ffffff', .12, strength 0..1)`.\n- Lighting (step 9; every view, side-scrollers included, no WebGPU needed): `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color: '#ffa050' })` every draw. Dark areas get a smooth cool tint, lights add their own color, and emissive items (flames, sparks, bullets, `emissive: true`) stay bright. `lights.darkness` (0..1) and `lights.tint` tune it; after setting `lights.dark = [r, g, b]`, `levels` or `glow`, call `lights.refresh()`. Props with flames can light the scene: `r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { light: true })`. Lamps, street lamps, lanterns and crystals glow on their own (`haloR`, `haloColor`, `halo: false`); `cone: true` adds a light cone under a street lamp.\n- Hits: `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage: 12, textScale: 2, textZ: 22 })` does hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks, a bouncing damage number (`bounce: false` floats it) and a sound in one call; `particles.impact(x, y, z, size, color)` for the star alone.\n- GPU lighting (optional, ground views with a TileMap): `const gpu = game.enableGPU({ map })`; mark glowing things with `px.glow(g, 1)`; add `lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` and `lights.heat(x, y, z, w)`. Where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `game.lights.ambient` (`gpu.fallback = false`: unlit).\n\n## Genre playbook (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1)\n| Genre (examples) | View | Build it with | Copy slice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Platformer (Mario, Mega Man, Sonic, Castlevania) | `side` (+ `brawler` for 2.5D) | PlatformMap, Platformer, Humanoid, sprites, Bullets `'side'` | PLATFORMER |\n| Metroidvania (Metroid, Symphony of the Night) | `side` | an `E.World` of PlatformMaps (the rooms module: doors at open edges, a slide, the explored map, gates opened by abilities, saves), a Platformer given `airJumps` and `dash` as they are found, its further moves, an arm cannon aimed eight ways (`point`, `aim`, `rig.tip()`, Bullets) | METROIDVANIA |\n| Action adventure (Zelda, Secret of Mana) | `threequarter` or `topdown` | TileMap rows, `cam.room`, Body, Humanoid + `E.Attack` sword, Dialog, `E.ui.hearts` | ADVENTURE |\n| Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword) | none: rooms painted in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) | `E.Click` (the click module): rooms with walk areas and holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots, the nine verbs, Sierra's icons or a verb coin, an inventory, puzzles as rules; talk through `E.Story` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad) | none | `E.Story` nodes and choices over a painted backdrop, `rig.drawPortrait` at size, `story.log` (the backlog), `story.auto`, `story.skip`; `game.saves` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Action RPG / dungeon (Diablo, Landstalker) | `iso` | TileMap, FlowField, Body (jump onto blocks), GPU lighting | ADVENTURE |\n| Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage, TMNT) | `brawler` | TileMap street strip, Body, Humanoid (`weapon: null`) + `E.Combo` punches and `kick: true` kicks, `E.knockback`, waves that lock the screen; a second player drops in (`game.allowJoin`, `cam.frame` on both) | BRAWLER |\n| Fighting (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers) | `side` | two `E.Fighter`s in an `E.Match` (the fight module): moves with frame data, guards, throws, specials by motion (`'236'`) or charge, a meter, projectiles; `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training | FIGHTING |\n| Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius, R-Type) | `overhead` (vertical, and horizontal: world x scrolls) | sprites, Bullets, `E.pattern`, a timeline of waves; horizontal: rock edges as functions of x, a power bar, options on the ship's past path, `E.Path` loops; `E.Arcade` for the continue and the table | SHOOTER (both stages) |\n| Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet, Devil's Crush) | a steep table view of its own (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`) | `E.Physics`: chains for the walls, `flipper`, `bumper`, `slingshot`, `kicker`, a ramp on a layer of its own, the ball a `bullet`; `input.zones` flip on a phone; `E.Arcade` for the score and table. Breakout and Asteroids with real collisions are its recipes | PINBALL |\n| Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth) | `side` at a whole scale (`new E.View(id, label, 0, 0, 1, 1)`) | `E.Terrain` (`carve` the blasts, `walk` the soldiers, `ray` the shots), grenades as `E.Physics` bodies on `land.collider()`, `E.Turns`; a drag aims (`steer = 'drag'`, `drag()`, `stickOn`); a CPU tries its shots in simulation | ARTILLERY |\n| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n| Real-time strategy (Warcraft II, StarCraft, Dune II) | `iso` | `E.Strategy` (the strategy module): kinds as data, selection by click, box and group, orders, gold, building, training, fog, an AI's build order and waves; `cam.free`, `{ pick: unit }` and `r.pick`, buildings as `E.SHAPES`, E.Shell's minimap; bodies drawn `every: 3` | RTS |\n| Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity) | `iso` | paths as floor tags walked on an `E.Grid`, agents with `E.needs` picking by `E.choose`, `E.Clock` days and light, `game.timeScale` 0 to 4, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`; rides as shapes stepped through angles (`params`, `facing`) | SIM |\n| 3D platformer or adventure remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash) | `iso` / `threequarter` / `brawler` | TileMap blocks of different heights + Body `jump()` (z is real height); in real 3D: `E.World3D` (the 3D engine's manual), `camera.lock` | ADVENTURE; 3D ACTION, 3D PLATFORMER |\n| Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark) | the 3D engine's `'zones'` camera | `camera.zones` (fixed cameras by room), `camera.steer` (tank controls), `camera.lock` to aim, `world.through` doors, `world.flashlight`, fog; `E.RPG` inventory, `game.saves` | 3D HORROR |\n| First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) | the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes | cells of 2 x 2 tiles stepped and turned a quarter at a time (tweened), `E.Grid` paths for the monsters, `E.Turns` `'atb'` (real time) or `'speed'` (turns), `E.Fog` for the automap, `world.thing('door')` gates and `world.unlock`, `rig.drawPortrait` for the party | 3D CRAWLER |\n| Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania, Space Channel 5) | any (a highway on the overlay) | `E.Rhythm` (the rhythm module): a chart of lane strings in parts on a song's steps, judged in ms of the music clock, combo, life, grade, holds, calls a teacher plays and you answer, `rh.draw` (`arrows: true`); the songbook's songs (`E.Songbook`); lanes as `input.zones` on a phone; `audio.onBeat` moves the dancer | RHYTHM |\n| Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam) | `threequarter` / `topdown` | the ball an `E.Body` (`bounce`; curl: turn its velocity across its path), passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` for a lob, a shot's meter, formations by where the ball is, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` | SPORTS |\n| Third-person stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu) | `iso` / `threequarter` | TileMap roofs, Mover, `E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, FlowField `jump` | STEALTH |\n| Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero) | Mode 7 (the mode7 module, over any view) | `E.Mode7` over a TileMap track (floor tags: grass, sand, boost), `E.Car` karts (`kart: true`: hop, drift, boost) in an `E.Race` round an `E.Path` (laps, checkpoints, drivers, items), rigs in karts as billboards, `game.splitScreen(2)` for two | KART |\n| 3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.track` (banked, walls, pads, `gaps` for a shortcut), `world.vehicle` karts raced by `E.Race`, split screen through `world.cameras` | 3D RACING |\n| Rail shooters (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.flyer` on rails or free in bounds, rolls (`f.rolling`) turn shots aside, `f.seek` for a charged lock, `Dialog`'s radio | 3D FLIGHT |\n| Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Road Rash) | the pseudo-3D road (the road module) | `E.Road` segments (curves, hills, tunnels), scenery and traffic placed on it, `curveAt` throwing the car outward, a clock and checkpoints | ROAD |\n| Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA) | `topdown` / `iso` | `E.Car` on a TileMap (its floor tags slow it, its walls bounce it), `E.Race` for laps and drivers, cars drawn with `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points | KART |\n| First-person shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007, Apex Legends) | the 3D engine's first person (or third) | a level of text rows or a terrain, `world.ray` hitscan, recoil and reloads, doors (`world.thing('door')`), raiders on `world.flow()` | 3D SHOOTER |\n| Bomberman, Frogger, Sokoban, Gauntlet, Robotron; Sonic, Prince of Persia; light guns | as the recipe says | `AI_GUIDE.md`'s Genre recipes: each on the kit closest to it | (a recipe) |\n\n**Sizes:** heroes 40-60 px tall at 320x240 (`size: 1.2-1.4`), bosses 1.8-2.4. Small things (coins, bullets, ships, pickups) are string sprites designed at 8-16 px, drawn at scale 2 if they are the player's ship. Keep hitboxes smaller than the art.\n\n**Feel numbers** (world units per second, tile = 16): walk 70-90, run 95-140, jump 250-320 with gravity 800, fall cap 330, dash 200-260, bullets 250-320 (player) and 70-120 (enemy), enemy walk 25-45. Hit feedback: `game.hitFx` (Effects).\n\n## More: animation and examples outside this card\nThe repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; any file at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`) holds more to take from. Its files are large: search them and take only what the game needs. Real 3D (`E.World3D`: first person, racing, flight) is there: `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`.\n- **Motion clips**: 325 curated and 2,548 motion-capture takes that play on any Humanoid: find one in `src/mocap/catalogs/`, cut a small set with `tools/anim-set.mjs`, then `Mocap.drive(rig, lib)` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Where to find more; `docs/MOCAP.md`).\n- **Objects and materials** (full engine): `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a still object from any view and facing, `map.solid(...)` makes it solid; `E.MATERIALS` dresses floors (`floorMat`), walls (`face`, `roof`) and platform tiles (`mat`). `docs/ASSETS.md` is the card: the format, the library, writing one and checking it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`.\n- **Worked examples**: Emberdeep (`src/emberdeep/`), a whole game on this engine: 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies, 3 heroes and 30 skills, registered with `def(kind, id, spec)` (one search lists them); adapt them, they lean on the game's own helpers. `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body.\n- **Modules** (`engine/modules/<name>.js`; `modules/` in the handoff package): general systems in files of their own, each a `<script>` after the engine, its header its manual (read it there, or its section of the index). A kit carries the modules its slice names (`@modules` in its header); the full engine (`dist/my-3d2dge.js`; `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) carries them all.\n  - `grid`: square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges over heights and past allies, fields of view, `E.Fog`, groups, shapes of effect, threat maps, `E.GridMover`, `E.Turns` (speed, charge time, phases, ATB). ROGUELIKE, PUZZLE, TACTICS, ARCADE, ARTILLERY, RTS, SIM, 3D CRAWLER.\n  - `gen`: generated maps from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a TileMap reads. ROGUELIKE, ARCADE.\n  - `fight` (the full engine only): one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter` (frame data, hurt boxes from the rig's joints, guards, throws, cancels, juggles, specials by motion or charge, a meter, projectiles), `E.Match` (rounds, KO, the HUD, training), `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`. FIGHTING.\n  - `rooms`: a world of rooms on a grid of screens (`E.World`): walking between them at open edges, the explored map (`drawMap`, or `shellMap()` for `E.Shell`), gates that abilities or keys open, saves in `E.store`. METROIDVANIA.\n  - `mode7`: the SNES's Mode 7 (`E.Mode7`): a camera over a flat floor (a TileMap's, a function's or a canvas), a turning sky, billboards far to near (rigs, sprites, shapes, your drawing), fog, `project` and `toFloor`, split screens. KART.\n  - `road`: the pseudo-3D road (`E.Road`): segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance and hidden past a crest, the sky's parallax on curves. ROAD.\n  - `drive`: cars on the ground plane (`E.Car`: grip and slide, a kart's hop and drift boost, floor tags, walls and cars it bounces off) and races (`E.Race`: checkpoints along an `E.Path`, laps, places, drivers that pass and rubber-band, items, the HUD; the 3D engine's `world.vehicle` too). KART.\n  - `story`: dialogue as data (`E.Story`): lines with speakers and portraits, choices with conditions, flags and values, gotos, the game's events, a quest log, balloons over heads, a backlog, auto and skip; its state for a save. RPG, POINT-AND-CLICK, TACTICS.\n  - `rpg`: a role-playing game's rules (`E.RPG`): items, bags (slots or a grid of sizes), members with stats, levels and gear, status effects, a party (items used, rewards shared), shops, damage, random encounters by zone, list and card windows. RPG, 3D HORROR.\n  - `strategy` (the full engine only; needs `grid`): `E.Strategy` for real-time strategy (selection by click, box and group; queued orders; formations; production, resources, placement; fog, the minimap; an AI's build order and waves) and for sims `E.Clock` (days, the light), `E.needs` and `E.choose`, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`. RTS, SIM.\n  - `click` (the full engine only): point-and-click adventures (`E.Click`): painted rooms, walk areas with holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots with verbs, SCUMM's nine verbs, Sierra's icons, a verb coin or two buttons, an inventory, rules as data, exits, `story`'s talk; taps on a phone. POINT-AND-CLICK.\n  - `physics`: 2D rigid bodies (`E.Physics`): circles, boxes, polygons, segments, chains and arcs; bounce, friction, sensors, layers, sleep; fast balls swept, never passing through; the same world at any refresh rate; flippers, bumpers, slingshots, kickers. PINBALL, ARTILLERY.\n  - `terrain`: destructible ground in the side plane (`E.Terrain`): carved and added by circles and polygons, the ground below a point and its normal, rays for shots, walkers on slopes, a collider for `physics`. ARTILLERY.\n  - `songs`: the songbook (`E.Songbook`): songs by mood (field, town, battle, race, horror, chase, dance ...) and jingles (stage clear, game over, level up); `get(id)`, `find(mood)`. RHYTHM.\n  - `rhythm` (the full engine only): `E.Rhythm`: charts on a song's steps, judgments in ms of the music clock, combo, score, a life gauge, grades, holds, call and response, a note highway in any direction. RHYTHM.\n  - `arcade`: the coin-op's frame: scores that float up, extra lives, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode (a demo, the table, the title). ARCADE, SHOOTER, PINBALL.\n- **Everything running**: https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs\n\n## Before you hand the game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge` (between the markers in a single file; `game/game.js` in the handoff package). Only your game: no leftover starter menu or unused slices. No network, no libraries.\n- Title screen and game-over screen as scenes; `pausable: true` on gameplay scenes. No pause, settings, rebinding or full-screen code of your own (they're standard); prompts from `game.input.label(action)`.\n- The camera follows the player (`game.focus` every update) and shows the level; the HUD uses screen pixels, or `E.Shell`'s HTML for a modern game (the STEALTH kit; the handoff package's template).\n- Sound effects on jump, hit, pickup and death; music per scene.\n- The look target list above: backdrop, textured tiles, props, sized and dressed HD characters, effects, title font, lights. Compare your screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug, not with NES games.\n- If you can run commands: `node tools/check.mjs game/index.html` (your page; add `#scene` to jump to a scene). It presses start, plays, cycles the views, prints how much of the screen is filled and how many colors it uses, adds look notes (flat areas, checkerboard dithering, low contrast, a thin palette), and fails on any error. Act on every look note. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html#scene --steps \"wait:1500 press:Enter wait:800 rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records an animation frame by frame into one contact sheet: check every attack with it (a swing must wind up, strike, and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps).\n- Animation variety: every character has its own moves from `E.MOVES` (not one shared swing), enemies telegraph with a visible wind-up, and reactions play out (hurt, knockback, `down`, victory `cheer`).\n# Objects and materials as data\n\nThe full engine draws still objects (barrels, tables, trees, wells) from a few lines of data, in every view and facing,\nwith the house shading and outline, and gives every surface (floors, wall faces, wall tops, platform tiles, the objects'\nown faces) a material from one open registry. An object costs one to a few hundred tokens of data (the core's median is\n125); you check it headless with one command. Read this card whole before you write one. (The agent edition keeps the\nregistries and ignores the calls: `E.addAssets` returns empty lists, the others null.)\n\n## Use one\n\n```js\n// in draw(r): feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor; its front toward facing\nr.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing: Math.PI / 2 });\n// once, before the updates: bodies stop at it, a body coming down lands on it, one wholly below passes under\nmap.solid('table', 120, 80, 0, { facing: 0 });\n// a sprite of it, for an inventory or a HUD (r.sprite, px.sprite)\nconst icon = E.shapeSprite('chest', { view: 'iso' });\n```\n\n- **r.shape options:** facing (radians, where the front points: 0 east, π/2 south, as written), scale, mats ({ slot:\n  material }), flip (mirrors it x to -x, collision too), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (default true),\n  light (adds its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true).\n- **map.solid** takes the same id and placement, so one object can feed both calls. Bodies span z to z + h (E.Body's h, 32).\n- **In 3D** the 3D engine draws the same entries, materials and boxes: `world.shape(id, x, y, z, o)` (its header).\n- **Steep views** (top-down) draw shapes from the characters' lower angle, as rigs are drawn. The light comes from the\n  viewer's upper left in every view.\n- **Glow and fire:** a material with glow: 1 ignores the light and stays bright in the dark; an entry's flames ([[x, y, z,\n  w, h]]) draw the engine's animated flame (r.shape only: a sprite has none). Anything else that moves stays a prop\n  (r.prop) or your own code.\n- **Dressing a generated level:** a scene is a few entries as [id, across, forward] from an anchor (a wall spot, forward\n  into the room); place it whole or not at all, each part on free floor, after the level's own random picks and from a\n  seeded stream of its own, so the level stays as its seed builds it (Emberdeep's `src/emberdeep/53-scenes.js`).\n\n## The core library\n\n**Shapes** (sized for a 26-unit hero; generators take params): barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed,\nbookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, well, sign, lamppost, anvil; fence { len: 16 }, pine { h: 41, tiers: 4 },\ntree { h: 40, seed: 1 }, bush { r: 7 }, grass { h: 6, n: 6 }, rock { r: 7.5, seed: 2 }, crystal { h: 18, seed: 1 }.\n`r.shape('pine', x, y, 0, { params: { h: 30 } })`; `E.SHAPES.barrel.about` says what each is.\n\n**Materials:** woods oak walnut birch crate plank planks wood; stones stone cobble marble granite sandstone slate rock\nbrick flagstone ruin; metals iron steel gold copper bronze metal rusted; clay glazed porcelain; cloth linen burlap wool velvet leather;\nplants bark needles leaves shrub moss straw hedge grass; ground dirt sand snow mud; water ice crystal flame bone shingle;\nwalls timber plaster; tops speckle plain tiles slab; tiles block bonus ground pipe checker.\n\n## Materials on surfaces\n\n```js\nE.MATERIALS.mossy = { color: '#6a7a5a', pattern: 'stones', size: 6 };   // or the shorthand '#6a7a5a stones size=6'\nconst types = { 1: { h: 32, top: '#6a6280', side: '#4a4360', face: 'cobble', roof: 'slate' } };   // TileMap walls\nconst map = new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorMat: 'flagstone', floorMats: { water: 'water' } });   // floors\nconst level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types: { 1: { kind: 'solid', style: 'block', mat: 'mossy' } } });\n```\n\nfloorMats is keyed by the floor tags of the legend ('~': { floor: 'water' }); either stands in for floorTex.\n\nA material is `{ color, pattern, ...the pattern's numbers, ramp, glow, over }`. Its five tones come from `E.tones(color)`\n(or its own `ramp` of five, dark to light); the light picks a band, the pattern moves it a step, so a material works on\nevery surface and in every palette and recolors with one hex. An unknown name warns with the list of names.\nOverlays, the last on top, each in its own color's tones: `over: [['moss', '#5f7a3a', { up: .6 }], ['cracks']]`; moss on\nwhat looks up, cracks (the material a step darker), dirt near the ground (h 6), rust; each takes amount and size.\n\n| Pattern | Numbers | What |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| none | | the light alone |\n| planks | bw 3, len 14, dir | boards with joints; staves on round parts; dir: 'v' turns them |\n| crate | frame 2, bw 3 | a framed, braced side; planks on its lid |\n| bricks | bw 6, bh 3, mortar | running bond |\n| stones | size 5 | irregular stones (Voronoi), dark joints, a lit rim |\n| tiles | size 4 | square tiles, grout |\n| speck, noise | amount .08; scale 4 | specks; soft patches |\n| foliage | size 3.5 | leaf clumps lit on top |\n| bark | | fissures; rings on a cut end |\n| metal | rivet | a shine where lit; rivets |\n| cloth | fold .9 | weave and folds |\n| glaze | | a glossy shine |\n| thatch, shingles | course; bw | straw courses; roof tiles |\n\nYour own pattern: `E.PATTERNS.waves = (s, m) => Math.sin(s.u / (m.size || 3)) > .8 ? 1 : 0` (s.u, s.v: world units\nacross and up the surface; s.lid, s.curved, s.lit, s.W, s.H; return a step, -1 to +2). A material may also paint one kind\nof surface its own way, per pixel: `face(wx, z, tones, type, cx, cy, fi, nh, h)` or `roof(wx, wy, tones, type)` returning\na color or null, `floor(x, y, tag, view)` returning [r, g, b].\n\n## Write one\n\nUnits: 16 = 1 m = one tile; x east, y south, z up. The origin is the middle of the footprint on the ground; write it\nfacing south (+y). Use whole or half units: a pixel at game size is about 0.7 units.\n\n```js\nE.SHAPES.stool = { about: 'round three-legged stool', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { w: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['cyl','w',[0,0,8],5,1.5], ['ring',3,3,['box','l',[0,0,0],[1.5,1.5,8],{rot:[0,-8,0]}]]] };\n```\n\nAn entry: `about`, `tags`, `mats` (slot to material; a slot name that is not in mats is a material itself), and `parts`, or\n`make(params)` returning parts with `params` its defaults. Optional: `scale` (times the placement's), `foot: [x0, y0,\nx1, y1]` (the footprint for collision, and for sorting when it stands raised; the bounds by default), `lights: [[x, y,\nz, color, radius]]`, `flames: [[x, y, z, w, h]]`.\n\n| Part | Form | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| box | `['box', m, at, [w, d, h], { rot }]` | at: the bottom middle |\n| cyl | `['cyl', m, at, r, h, { r2, seg, sq }]` | r2: top radius (0: a cone); sq squashes y; seg: 6 for a hexagon |\n| lathe | `['lathe', m, at, [[r, z], ...], { seg }]` | a profile turned about z, bottom to top; [0, z] closes an end |\n| ball | `['ball', m, at, r, { sz }]` | at: its centre; sz below 1 flattens it |\n| prism | `['prism', m, at, [[a, b], ...], h, { axis }]` | an outline pushed h: up z (outline x, y), or centred along y (outline x, z) or x (outline y, z) |\n| wedge | `['wedge', m, at, [w, d, h]]` | h tall at the back, down to the floor at the front |\n| rock | `['rock', m, at, r, { sz, seed, rough }]` | a lumpy, faceted ball; below the floor is not drawn |\n| use | `['use', id, at, { rot, scale, mats, ...params }]` | another shape inside this one |\n| mirror | `['mirror', 'x' \\| 'y' \\| 'xy', part]` | the part and its mirror copies |\n| ring, row | `['ring', n, radius, part]`, `['row', n, [dx, dy, dz], part]` | n copies round z (the part moved out to radius); n copies in a line, centred |\n| group | `['group', at, rot, [parts]]` | parts moved and turned together |\n\nEvery part takes `{ rot }`: a yaw in degrees, or [x, y, z] degrees. Write arrays densely (`[0,0,8]`, not `[0, 0, 8]`):\nspaces cost a third more tokens.\n\n### Worked entries\n\n```js\nbarrel: { about: 'oak barrel, two iron hoops', tags: 'container wood', mats: { w: 'oak', h: 'iron' },\n  parts: [['lathe','w',[0,0,0],[[0,0],[5,0],[6,3],[6.5,8],[6,13],[5,16],[0,16]]], ['row',2,[0,0,9],['cyl','h',[0,0,7.5],6.5,1.5]]] },\ncrate: { about: 'wooden crate, framed and braced', tags: 'container wood', mats: { c: 'crate' }, parts: [['box','c',[0,0,0],[15,15,14]]] },\ntable: { about: 'plank table, four legs', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { t: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['box','t',[0,0,10],[24,14,2]], ['mirror','xy',['box','l',[10,5,0],[2,2,10]]]] },\nchest: { about: 'oak chest, round lid, iron bands', tags: 'container treasure', mats: { w: 'oak', b: 'iron', l: 'gold' },\n  parts: [['box','w',[0,0,0],[16,10,8]], ['prism','w',[0,0,8],[[-5,0],[5,0],[4,3],[0,5],[-4,3]],16,{axis:'x'}],\n    ['mirror','x',['box','b',[5.5,0,0],[1.5,11,8.5]]], ['box','l',[0,5,5],[3,1,3]]] },\nlamppost: { about: 'iron street lamp, glowing head', tags: 'town light', mats: { m: 'iron', g: 'flame' }, lights: [[0,0,40,'#ffd070',70]],\n  parts: [['cyl','m',[0,0,0],2.5,3], ['cyl','m',[0,0,3],1,34], ['box','g',[0,0,37],[4,4,5]], ['cyl','m',[0,0,42],3.5,3,{r2:0,seg:4}]] },\nfence: { about: 'rail fence, len long (16)', tags: 'village farm', mats: { w: 'oak' }, params: { len: 16 },\n  make: p => [['mirror','x',['box','w',[p.len / 2 - 1,0,0],[2,2,12]]], ['row',2,[0,0,5],['box','w',[0,0,6],[p.len - 2,1,1.5]]]] },\n```\n\nA generator seeds its noise from its params (`E.rng(seed)`), never `Math.random`, so it draws the same pixels everywhere.\n\n## Make one: the recipe\n\n1. **Read this card whole.**\n2. **Find** the nearest entry: `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"tavern table\"` searches the core library and the store's sets\n   (`src/assets/sets/`, about a thousand objects, materials and sprites converted from CC0 libraries, each with where it\n   came from) and `--show set:id` prints one; the Asset Lab (`/asset-lab`) shows them all.\n3. **Copy** it and change its names, parts and materials, or write a new one from the parts above. Give the cue a player\n   knows it by parts of its own (a skep's coils as stacked rings, an anvil's horn, a barrow's open tub dark inside), and\n   keep small parts plain ('none', 'glaze'): a busy pattern on a part a few pixels wide reads as speckle.\n4. **Look:** `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` (a file that sets `E.SHAPES.stool`, or a store set; a core id\n   alone works too: `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs barrel`). It draws `check-output/asset-sheet/stool.png`: every view and\n   four facings, beside the hero, on a light and a dark floor. It lints what reads badly: too small from above, a part\n   under a pixel, too few tones, low contrast with the floor, its tokens. Read the picture, fix, repeat.\n5. **Use** it: `E.SHAPES.stool = {...}` and `r.shape('stool', ...)`; or a set file\n   (`(window.ASSETS = window.ASSETS || {}).MINE = { set: 'MINE', format: 1, materials, shapes }`) and\n   `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS.MINE)`, which names its entries 'MINE:stool' so they never collide, and returns the names.\n   A store set loads the same way (its script, then `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`);\n   `node tools/asset-set.mjs <sets> --ids a,b --name mine --out mine.js` cuts the few a game uses, with what they need\n   (Emberdeep's set dressing loads `src/assets/sets/emberdeep.js`, cut so).\n\n## Limits\n\n- Still objects. Lids that open, swinging signs and held weapons are code (or props) for now.\n- A shape sorts by one point, as props do; a long one (a bed, a cart) may cross a body walking right beside it.\n- Collision is the footprint's box, turned with the shape, and the bounds' height: a table is solid under its top too.\n- Bigger than about 240 pixels on screen, a shape skips the actors' effects (flash, x-ray).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/platformer.js && rg -n 'Platformer|PlatformMap|Action|Humanoid|Shell|Settings|settings|pause|rig.pose' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/platformer.js && rg -n 'Platformer|PlatformMap|Action|Humanoid|Shell|Settings|settings|pause|rig.pose' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md\"","aggregated_output":"// The PLATFORMER kit's game (a side-scroller: a night approach to a castle in five zones, a sword, a dash, candles, a 2.5D view; Castlevania, Mega Man X, Klonoa), readable: the starter's shell (src/starter/00-shell.js), then\n// the slice from line 159 (src/starter/20-platformer.js: its header says what it plays and how), then the start (90-start.js), as the\n// single-file kit holds them between GAME START and GAME END. platformer.html plays it; node kits/test.mjs platformer runs it headless.\n/* ============================== GAME START ==============================\n * MY-3D2DGE STARTER: a title menu and vertical slices, one per genre (a genre kit file holds one; the file with every\n * slice adds an animation lab). Each slice is a complete small game, written to be copied and changed: its own header\n * (below this shell) says what it plays, its keys and touch controls, its deep links and how it works.\n * FULL SCREEN, ANY SHAPE: res: 'ps1', expand: true. 320 x 240 is the least the screen shows; a wider or taller window\n * shows more world (r.W x r.H grows), so every HUD anchors to r.W and r.H, never to 320 and 240, and every level fills\n * what a wide screen or an upright phone shows. Prompts name the player's own keys: prompt('attack', 'Sword').\n * To make your own game: change the slice closest to your genre into yours, with its own title, play and game-over\n * scenes, and delete this menu and the slices you do not use; this shell (the game, its scenes, prompt, best and the\n * proof) stays unless you replace it. Deep links: #<slice> starts a slice, #<slice>/<part> jumps into a part.\n * ======================================================================== */\n(() => {\n'use strict';\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E;\n\n/* ---- SHELL: one game, several scenes. Each slice switches view, keys and resolution on entry ----\n * expand: the picture fills the window, any shape, with no bars: at least 320 x 240 (a slice's res), more on the long side */\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d' });\nconst P = game.particles, L = game.lights, A = game.audio;\nconst scenes = {};\nlet gpu = null;                               // WebGPU lighting, used by the adventure slice only\nconst best = (k, v) => { const old = E.store.get('best:' + k, 0); if (v > old) E.store.set('best:' + k, v); return Math.max(old, v); };\n// a prompt in the player's own controls, after rebinding: 'J Sword' on a keyboard, 'X Sword' on a pad, 'ATTACK Sword' (the\n// button's name) on a touch screen, where the buttons sit over the bottom corner: touch() says so, to move a prompt off it\nconst prompt = (a, word) => game.input.label(a) + ' ' + word, touch = () => game.input.lastDevice === 'touch';\n// each slice: its scene's id, its title entry, a line about it, and its family (the title groups them under it)\nconst SLICES = [\n  { id: 'adventure', label: 'ADVENTURE', about: 'TOP-DOWN ACTION ADVENTURE', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'pointclick', label: 'POINT-AND-CLICK', about: 'RELIGHT THE LIGHTHOUSE ON GULL ROCK', family: 'ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'platformer', label: 'PLATFORMER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING PLATFORMER', family: 'PLATFORM' },\n  { id: 'metroidvania', label: 'METROIDVANIA', about: 'A WORLD OF ROOMS, ITS MAP, ITS GATES', family: 'PLATFORM' },\n  { id: 'brawler', label: 'BRAWLER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING BEAT-EM-UP', family: 'FIGHTING' },\n  { id: 'fighting', label: 'FIGHTING', about: 'ONE ON ONE, BEST OF THREE', family: 'FIGHTING' },\n  { id: 'shooter', label: 'SHOOTER', about: 'VERTICAL SHOOT-EM-UP', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'arcade', label: 'ARCADE', about: 'A CABINET OF THREE CLASSICS', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'pinball', label: 'PINBALL', about: 'ONE TABLE: RAMP, MULTIBALL, JACKPOT', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'kart', label: 'KART', about: 'MODE 7 KARTS, THREE LAPS', family: 'RACING' },\n  { id: 'road', label: 'ROAD', about: 'A COAST ROAD AGAINST THE CLOCK', family: 'RACING' },\n  { id: 'puzzle', label: 'PUZZLE', about: 'FALLING BLOCKS AND FALLING PAIRS', family: 'PUZZLE' },\n  { id: 'rhythm', label: 'RHYTHM', about: 'STEP UP! FOUR LANES, TWO SONGS', family: 'MUSIC' },\n  { id: 'rpg', label: 'RPG', about: 'A JOURNEY: TOWN, WORLD, CAVE, BATTLES', family: 'RPG' },\n  { id: 'roguelike', label: 'ROGUELIKE', about: 'A CRYPT OF TEN GENERATED FLOORS', family: 'RPG' },\n  { id: 'tactics', label: 'TACTICS', about: 'TAKE THE HILL FORT, TURN BY TURN', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'rts', label: 'RTS', about: 'GATHER GOLD, RAISE AN ARMY, BURN THEIR CAMP', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'artillery', label: 'ARTILLERY', about: 'TWO TEAMS OF THREE ON AN ISLAND THAT BLOWS APART', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'sim', label: 'SIM', about: 'BUILD A THEME PARK, KEEP THEM SMILING', family: 'SIMULATION' },\n  { id: 'stealth', label: 'STEALTH', about: 'ROOFTOP STEALTH AND PARKOUR', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'sports', label: 'SPORTS', about: 'TWO AGAINST TWO IN THE CAGE', family: 'SPORTS' },\n  { id: 'animlab', label: 'ANIMATION LAB', about: 'EVERY MOVE, SKIN AND VIEW', family: 'TOOLS' }\n];\n/* the camera: each slice's scene says how the player may zoom it, camera: { zoom: [min, max] | false, start, tall }: the\n   engine zooms it with the mouse wheel, - and =, a pinch on a phone and Settings, Camera zoom (the player's own, kept, on\n   top of the slice's start: tall on a phone held upright). Keys every slice shares besides: V changes the camera view,\n   [ and ] turn it (camera.rotate), 0 resets it (the player's zoom too), G toggles GPU lighting, M mutes */\nconst camOpt = () => (game.scene && game.scene.camera) || {};\naddEventListener('keydown', e => {\n  if (e.repeat) return;\n  if (e.code === 'KeyV') { game.nextView(); game.note(game.view.label.toUpperCase() + ' VIEW'); }\n  else if (e.code === 'Digit0') { game.settings.set('zoom', 1); game.resetCamera(); game.note('CAMERA RESET'); }   // (resetCamera draws it at once)\n  else if ((e.code === 'BracketLeft' || e.code === 'BracketRight') && camOpt().rotate) { game.rotateView(e.code === 'BracketLeft' ? -45 : 45); game.note('TURN ' + game.yaw); }\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyM') A.mute();\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyG' && gpu) gpu.enabled = !gpu.enabled;\n});\n\n/* ---- TITLE: a lineup of HD heroes on a torch-lit castle ledge under a dusk sky. The ledge is wide enough for a wide\n * screen and sits on the screen's bottom edge whatever its height; the logo and menu hang from the top ---- */\nconst MID = 480, stage = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ['S'.repeat(60), 'S'.repeat(60)], legend: { S: 1 },\n  types: { 1: { style: 'stone', side: '#6a5a78', moss: '#4a6a3a' } }, edges: 'open' });\nconst titleBg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night');\nconst HEROES = [   // six heroes, each dressed with the HD rig options\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', armor: true, hair: 'short', cape: { len: 6, width: 6, seg: 2.6 }, colors: { cloth: '#3a5ab0', cape: '#b02a3a', capeIn: '#5a1020', hair: '#e0b050', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, outfit: 'tunic', hair: 'spiky', hat: { style: 'pointed', color: '#3aa050' }, colors: { cloth: '#3aa050', pants: '#e8dcc0', hair: '#e0b040', trim: '#8a5a2a' } }) },\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'bulky', weapon: null, hat: { style: 'band', color: '#e03a3a' }, colors: { cloth: '#f0f0f0', pants: '#2f5fd0' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#d83a2a', pants: '#2f5fd0', glove: '#f0f0f0' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'staff', outfit: 'robe', hair: 'ponytail', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#6a3ab0', hair: '#c8402a', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', hood: true, outfit: 'coat', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#e8e4dc', coat: '#d8d2c8', trim: '#b02a2a', belt: '#b02a2a', pants: '#4a4a5a', boot: '#3a2e2a' } }) }   // the stealth slice's hooded assassin\n];\nHEROES.forEach((h, i) => { h.x = MID + (i - 2.5) * 35; });\n// the corner's buttons (menu, full screen, the page's ? API) in game pixels: about 170 x 60 CSS pixels at the top right\nconst corner = () => { const S = game.screen, k = S.dpr / S.S; return [170 * k, 60 * k]; };\n// where a HUD's top-right corner ends: left of the corner's menu button while it stays there (a touch screen), else the edge\nconst cornerX = () => { const ca = touch() && game.cornerArea(); return ca && ca.y0 < 40 ? Math.floor(ca.x0) - 4 : game.W; };\n/* ---- THE PROOF (tools/kits-test.mjs runs each slice's twice and compares): proof(id, n, script, state, fresh) enters the\n * slice afresh on a game clock at 0, with Math.random seeded, runs n fixed steps of 1/120 s with script(i, key) pressing\n * keys (key.hold('right'), key.up('right'), key.tap('attack')), and gives a hash of what state() returns: the numbers that\n * say how the run went. fresh(), when given, runs first (seeded): it puts back what the slice keeps between visits. A\n * slice's run(n) is one (__game.scenes.adventure.run(600)): the same number every time ---- */\nconst proof = (id, n, script, state, fresh) => {\n  const was = Math.random, I = game.input, held = new Set();\n  const key = { hold: a => { held.add(a); I.hold(a); }, up: a => { held.delete(a); I.hold(a, false); }, tap: a => I.tap(a) };\n  Math.random = E.rng(7);\n  try {\n    if (fresh) fresh();\n    game.time = 0; game.timeScale = 1; game.go(id, null, { fade: 0 }); game.advance(1 / 120);\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { script(i, key); game.advance(1 / 120); }\n    const s = JSON.stringify(state()); let h = 2166136261;\n    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) h = Math.imul(h ^ s.charCodeAt(i), 16777619);\n    return h >>> 0;\n  } finally { for (const a of held) I.hold(a, false); Math.random = was; }\n};\n// a proof's walk: every `every` steps it lets go of the directions and holds the next of `dirs`\nconst walk = (i, key, dirs, every) => { if (i % every) return; for (const d of dirs) key.up(d); key.hold(dirs[(i / every) % dirs.length]); };\n// START picks too (the touch START, a pad's Start), as Enter does: any player's\nconst pickOnStart = m => { const P = game.players; if (P.some(I => I.pressed('start'))) { P.forEach(I => I.consumeAll()); m.onPick(m.i, m.items[m.i]); } };\n/** the title's menu for the slices in this file: grouped by family when there are several (FamilyMenu, in 05-families.js:\n *  the reference editions carry it, a genre kit of one slice doesn't), else a plain E.Menu */\nconst menuOf = (slices, o) => new Set(slices.map(s => s.family)).size > 1 ? new FamilyMenu(slices, o) : new E.Menu(game, slices.map(s => s.label), o);\nlet titleMenu = null;   // (made at the start, once the file's slices are known)\nscenes.title = {\n  view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'DEFAULT', res: 'ps1',\n  enter() { A.music('title'); game.resetCamera(); game.follow(null); game.cam.room = null; game.cam.bounds = null; if (gpu) gpu.enabled = false; L.enabled = false; },\n  update(dt) {\n    if (!titleMenu.update(dt)) pickOnStart(titleMenu);\n    game.focus(MID, 0, 32 + (game.H / 2 - 42) / -game.view.bz);   // the ledge's top 42 px above the screen's bottom edge\n    const t = game.time;\n    HEROES.forEach((h, i) => {\n      const u = (t * .45 + i * .37) % 3, swing = u < .5 ? { spec: { a0: 1.4, a1: -1.6, z0: 12, z1: 8, reach: 7.5 }, phase: u < .15 ? 'wind' : 'active', u: u < .15 ? u / .15 : (u - .15) / .35 } : null;\n      h.rig.update(dt, { x: h.x, y: 0, z: 32, facing: h.face, vx: 0, vy: 0, attack: swing, point: i === 3 && u < .6 });\n    });\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    const [cw, ch] = corner(), top = r.W / 2 + 132 > r.W - cw ? Math.round(ch) : 0;   // the logo drops below the corner's buttons where it would reach them (an upright phone)\n    titleMenu.y = 76 + top;\n    titleBg.draw(r);\n    stage.draw(r);\n    for (const x of [MID - 100, MID + 100]) r.prop('torch', x, 0, 32, { size: 1.3, light: false });\n    for (const x of [MID - 53, MID + 53]) r.prop('banner', x, -8, 64, { size: 1.1, color: '#8a1a2a' });\n    for (const h of HEROES) r.actor(h.x, 0, 32, (g, ox, oy) => h.rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n    r.overlay(g => {\n      E.font.title(g, 'my-3D2dge', r.W / 2, 12 + top, { align: 'center', scale: 4, colors: ['#fffbe8', '#ffe08a', '#ffb347', '#e0662a'], depth: 3 });\n      E.font.text(g, 'My \"3D\" 2D Game Engine', r.W / 2, 48 + top, '#e8e0ff', { align: 'center', gradient: ['#ffffff', '#c8b8f0'], shadow: '#140c1c', outline: false });\n      E.font.text(g, SLICES[titleMenu.i].about, r.W / 2, r.H - 14, '#ffe7a8', { align: 'center', shadow: '#000', outline: false });\n    });\n    titleMenu.draw(r);\n    const dev = game.input.lastDevice, how = dev === 'touch' ? 'Tap a game to play it' : dev === 'gamepad' ? 'D-pad + ' + game.input.label('confirm') + '   ' + game.input.label('start') + ' menu'\n      : 'Arrows + Enter or click   V view   - = zoom   M mute';\n    r.text(how, r.W / 2, r.H - 26, '#b8b0d8', { align: 'center', shadow: '#000', outline: false });\n  }\n};\n\n/* ---- GAME OVER: shared by every slice ---- */\nconst overMenu = new E.Menu(game, ['Retry', 'Title'], { y: 118, onPick: i => game.go(i === 0 ? overMenu.from : 'title') });\nconst overBg = new E.Backdrop({ sky: ['#12040c', '#3a0c1c', '#6a1a28'], moon: { x: .5, y: .3, r: 16, color: '#e8a0a0' }, stars: 30,\n  layers: [{ kind: 'mountains', color: '#2a0c16', y: .72, height: .3, parallax: 0 }, { kind: 'fog', color: '#8a2a3a', y: .9, height: .12, parallax: 0, drift: 4 }] });\nscenes.over = {\n  view: 'side', input: 'DEFAULT', res: 'ps1',\n  enter(d) { overMenu.from = d.from; overMenu.i = 0; A.music(null); A.sfx('die'); game.follow(null); game.focus(0, 0, 0); if (gpu) gpu.enabled = false; L.enabled = false; },\n  update(dt) { if (!overMenu.update(dt)) pickOnStart(overMenu); },\n  back() { game.go('title'); return true; },   // a phone's back gesture: to the title, not off the page\n  draw(r) {   // centred on any screen\n    const y = Math.round(r.H / 2) - 60; overMenu.y = y + 58;\n    overBg.draw(r); r.overlay(g => E.font.title(g, 'GAME OVER', r.W / 2, y, { align: 'center', scale: 4, colors: ['#ffe0e0', '#ff7a6a', '#c8202a'], depthColor: '#3a0810' })); overMenu.draw(r);\n  }\n};\n\n/* =====================================================================================\n * SLICE 2  PLATFORMER  (side-scroller in the style of Castlevania: SotN, Mega Man X and Klonoa)\n * A night approach to a castle in five zones (graveyard, bailey, lava moat, rampart above the clouds, gate hall). Space jumps,\n * C dashes, X attacks (the move set is in reset()). Cut down skeletons, bats and knights, break candles for gold and hearts,\n * reach the gate. TOUCH: the stick, JUMP, ATTACK, DASH. V switches to a 2.5D view, - / = zoom. How the look is built:\n *   1. SKIES   three E.Backdrops cross-faded by zone, a painted moon, a castle on its crag, soft mist, a sea of clouds\n *   2. LAYERS  two E.PlatformMaps: BACK (small-brick walls, never solid) and ROWS (big stone blocks you stand on)\n *   3. DRESS   engine props in two depth rows (against the wall, on the path in front), baked decals, an earth cut\n *   4. BLEND   PS1-style additive and half-transparent drawing, never dithered screen-door patterns\n *   5. LIGHT   each zone has a colored darkness; every flame punches a smooth hole in it and adds a colored glow\n *   6. CAST    engine rigs with E.MOVES move sets (a caped hero, rising skeletons, a knight), a hand-drawn bat\n *   7. HITS    game.hitFx (numbers stack in rows), a short tint and a flinch; 'die' topples, skeletons fall apart into bones\n *   8. ANY SCREEN  wider shows more of the level; taller (a phone held upright) lifts it onto a band of SOIL, clear of the\n *              touch buttons, and hangs the skies from the level's foot (FOOT) instead of the screen's\n * ===================================================================================== */\nconst PLAT = (() => {\n  const T = 16, MAXHP = 80, BONE = '#e4dcc4';\n  // 2. LAYERS. PLAY LAYER: a 16x16 tile per character, top row first; letters are spawn points (LEGEND).\n  const ROWS = [\n    '                                                                                                            ',\n    '                                                                                                            ',\n    '                                                                    k                                       ',\n    '                                                      b       c c c                                         ',\n    '                               k                b          HSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS                                ',\n    '             c                c c                          H                           k c c     k          ',\n    '           c                oooooo       c c      c        H          v                                     ',\n    '            k    k                      ====   c     c     H                                  g       F     ',\n    '           M  v       k              SS                    H k                      SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS',\n    '          MM             SS          SS                    H                       SDDDDGDDDDDDGDDDDDDDDGDDD',\n    ' @      sMMM    s        SS   g      SS   s                H    g             s   SSDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDD',\n    '####################SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS     m     SSSSSSSSSSSS^^^SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS',\n    '####################SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSLLLLLLLLLLLSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS'\n  ];\n  const LEGEND = { '#': 1, S: 2, D: 13, M: 14, G: 13, o: 5, '=': 6, H: 7, '^': 8, L: 9,\n    '@': 'hero', c: 'coin', k: 'candle', g: 'knight', s: 'skeleton', b: 'bat', v: 'roost', m: 'lift', F: 'gate' };   // v: a bat roosting upside down\n  // styles shade with tones of `side`; `top` colors tops in 2.5D\n  const TYPES = { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#4a3a48', top: '#4a7a62', flower: '#c8b8f0' }, 2: { style: 'stone', side: '#8a8298', top: '#5e5a72', moss: '#56704e' },   // soil, castle blocks\n    13: { style: 'stone', side: '#655d78' }, 14: { style: 'stone', side: '#6c7a76', top: '#46604c', moss: '#4f7a4a' },                                    // foundation, mossy ruin\n    5: { kind: 'oneway', style: 'plank', side: '#948aa4' }, 6: { kind: 'oneway', style: 'plank', side: '#8a5a3a' }, 7: { kind: 'ladder', side: '#a07850' },   // balcony, scaffold\n    8: { kind: 'hazard', style: 'spikes', side: '#b8c0d8' }, 9: { kind: 'hazard', style: 'liquid', side: '#c8401a' } };                                  // lava: see lavaDraw()\n  // BACK LAYER, aligned with ROWS: walls behind the play plane (never solid); small bricks behind big blocks is a cheap depth\n  // cue. Letters place engine props (see PROP) or baked decals (W A t g).\n  const BACK = [\n    '                                                        v###                ################################',\n    '                                                        ####                --------------------------------',\n    '                    # # # # # # # # # # # # #           ####                #vq#########h##########h#######Q',\n    '                    -v----------------------v-          ##### # # # # # # # ################N#######N#######',\n    '                    #########N#########N######          ####                ################################',\n    '                    ########FFFFFF############          ####----------------################################',\n    '                    ####W#########W########W##          #W##w  ||  j  ||   e#########T##W####T##W##T#####T##',\n    '                    --------------------------          ####   ||     ||    ####R#########C#######U#######U#',\n    '          eV        ##########################          ####   P|     P|    ################################',\n    '                    ##T#####T#######T####T####          #T##   ||     ||    ################################',\n    'lutgpgicg  xKtigpfflI##########C##A##########I          ####   ||     ||    #Z#A############################',\n    '                                                                                                            ',\n    '                                                                                                            '\n  ];\n  // FRONT: small, darker props on the graveyard path in front of the characters\n  const FRONT = ' , k,m   ,o  ,  k,m ', FP = { ',': { name: 'grass', size: 1.3, color: '#1e3430' }, k: { name: 'skull', size: .8, color: '#8a8478', dx: -5 },\n    m: { name: 'mushroom', size: .45, color: '#4a1a3a' }, o: { name: 'rock', size: .9, color: '#2e2a3a' } };\n  const on = (spawn, tile = 1) => ({ tile, spawn }), back = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: BACK, types: { 1: { kind: 'back', style: 'brick', side: '#6e6890', course: 6 },\n    2: { kind: 'back', style: 'stone', side: '#7e789c' }, 3: { kind: 'back', style: 'stone', side: '#9a94b8' } }, legend: { '#': 1, '-': 2, '|': 3, W: on('glass'), A: on('arch'), v: on('vines'),\n    T: on('torch'), N: on('banner'), C: on('candelabra'), I: on('pillar'), Z: on('brazier'), P: on('torch', 3), R: on('ruin'), F: on('rail'), U: on('statue'), h: on('chandelier'), q: on('web'), Q: on('webR'),\n    t: 'tree', g: 'grave', f: 'fence', ',': 'grass', l: 'lamp', x: 'bones', p: 'ruin', k: 'skull', m: 'mushroom', i: 'offering', V: 'vines', w: 'web', e: 'webR', j: 'lantern',\n    c: 'cross', u: 'urn', K: 'tomb' } });\n  // shapes from the asset store (docs/ASSETS.md): a stone cross, an urn and a sarcophagus, cut from kenney-graveyard into\n  // the page's 'platformer' set (src/assets/sets/platformer.js); r.shape options per BACK tag, y as PROP's\n  const STONES = E.addAssets((window.ASSETS || {}).platformer || { set: 'platformer', shapes: {} }).shapes.length ? {\n    cross: { id: 'platformer:cross', y: -4, scale: 1.1, facing: Math.PI / 2, mats: { a: '#5a566e speck' } },\n    urn: { id: 'platformer:urn-round', y: -2, scale: 1.3, facing: Math.PI / 2 },\n    tomb: { id: 'platformer:crypt-a', y: -5, scale: .9, facing: 0, mats: { a: '#605c74 speck' } } } : {};\n  // 3. DRESS: r.prop options per BACK tag (y < 0: against the wall; top: hangs from its cell; name: a variant).\n  const PROP = { torch: { y: -7 }, banner: { y: -8, color: '#6a1830', emblem: '#d8a040' }, candelabra: { y: -4 }, pillar: { y: -6, color: '#8a84a0' }, ruin: { name: 'pillar', broken: true, y: -5, color: '#7c7892' },\n    statue: { y: -6, color: '#8e8aa4' }, brazier: { y: -4, size: 1.2 }, fence: { y: -3, color: '#34304a' }, grass: { y: -2, color: '#3e6a58' }, lamp: { y: -2, halo: false }, bones: { y: -2, color: '#b8b0a8' },\n    skull: { y: -1, color: '#d8d0c0' }, offering: { name: 'candle', y: -3, size: .5 }, mushroom: { y: -1, color: '#6a3a8a', size: .6 }, vines: { y: -1, top: 1, color: '#3e7048' }, web: { name: 'cobweb', y: -7, top: 1, color: '#a8a6c8' },\n    webR: { name: 'cobweb', y: -7, top: 1, flip: true, color: '#a8a6c8' }, chandelier: { y: -4, top: 1 }, rail: { name: 'fence', y: 3, color: '#7a7290' }, lantern: { y: -3, top: 1, color: '#e0902a' } };\n  for (const k in PROP) if (PROP[k].top) PROP[k].anchor = 'top';\n  const FLAME = { torch: 24, candelabra: 30, brazier: 20, lamp: 29, offering: 6, chandelier: -8, lantern: -8 };   // flame heights, for the light pass\n  // 1. SKIES: one Backdrop per mood, far layers pale and blue; close sky stops (ramp) hide the gradient dither. The castle,\n  // clouds and moon are drawn in sky().\n  const ramp = (...cs) => cs.slice(1).flatMap((c, i) => [0, .2, .4, .6, .8].map(k => E.mix(cs[i], c, k))).concat(cs.slice(-1)), BG = [\n    new E.Backdrop({ sky: ramp('#050820', '#0c1a3e', '#1a3460', '#2e5078'), stars: 90, layers: [{ kind: 'clouds', color: '#24345e', y: .42, height: .34, parallax: .01, drift: 2, nebula: true },\n      { kind: 'mountains', color: '#34507c', y: .6, height: .24, parallax: .03 }] }),\n    new E.Backdrop({ sky: ramp('#0c040c', '#2a0a18', '#5c1420', '#a8361c'), stars: 16, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains', color: '#4a1a24', y: .6, height: .28, parallax: .03, seed: 5 }] }),\n    new E.Backdrop({ sky: ramp('#02030f', '#0a0f34', '#1e2860', '#4a4a88'), stars: 140, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains', color: '#5a6aa8', y: .74, height: .3, parallax: .02, snow: '#d8e0ff', seed: 12 }] })];\n  // 5. LIGHT, part 1: ZONES set the sky, the darkness (color, strength) and the rim light, blended over 90 units\n  const ZONES = [{ x: 0, bg: 0, amb: '#060a28', dark: .42, rim: '#a8c4ff' }, { x: 330, bg: 0, amb: '#12061a', dark: .6, rim: '#ffc890' }, { x: 720, bg: 1, amb: '#2a0404', dark: .32, rim: '#ffa060' },\n    { x: 930, bg: 2, amb: '#060826', dark: .38, rim: '#b8c4ff' }, { x: 1250, bg: 2, amb: '#140610', dark: .62, rim: '#ffd8a0' }];\n  const zoneAt = x => ZONES.slice(1).reduce((z, Z) => { const u = clamp((x - Z.x + 45) / 90, 0, 1); return u ? { amb: E.mix(z.amb, Z.amb, u), dark: lerp(z.dark, Z.dark, u), rim: u > .5 ? Z.rim : z.rim,\n    bg: z.bg.map((w, i) => lerp(w, i === Z.bg ? 1 : 0, u)) } : z; }, Object.assign({}, ZONES[0], { bg: [1, 0, 0] }));\n  // 4. BLENDING: PS1 translucency is additive ('lighter') or half (globalAlpha), never a dither. bake(): draw once, stamp often.\n  const blend = (g, mode, a, fn) => { g.globalCompositeOperation = mode; g.globalAlpha = a; fn(); g.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over'; g.globalAlpha = 1; };\n  const cache = new Map(), bake = (key, w, h, fn) => { if (!cache.has(key)) { const c = E.mkCanvas(w, h); fn(E.ctx2d(c)); cache.set(key, c); } return cache.get(key); };\n  // soft sprites with per-pixel alpha (stacked discs would band)\n  const soft = (key, w, h, c, fn) => bake(key, w, h, g => { const im = g.createImageData(w, h), rgb = E.hex(c); for (let i = 0; i < w * h; i++) im.data.set([...rgb, 255 * fn(i % w, i / w | 0)], i * 4); g.putImageData(im, 0, 0); });\n  const halo = (R, c) => soft('h' + R + c, 2 * R + 1, 2 * R + 1, c, (x, y) => Math.max(0, 1 - Math.hypot(x - R, y - R) / R) ** 2);   // a light: quadratic falloff\n  // mist: a seamless 320 px band, billowy on top, thickest low\n  const fog = c => soft('fog' + c, 320, 32, c, (x, y) => { const u = x / 320 * E.TAU, top = 12 - 7 * Math.sin(u * 3) - 4 * Math.sin(u * 7 + 1); return y < top ? 0 : Math.sin((y - top) / (32 - top) * Math.PI) ** 1.5 * .7; });\n  // 3. DECALS. Stained glass reads by its lead lines: a red and gold rose, blue lancets with a diamond lattice.\n  const glass = () => bake('glass', 23, 46, g => { for (let y = 0; y < 46; y++) for (let x = 0; x < 23; x++) {\n    const dx = x - 11, dy = y - 11, d = Math.hypot(dx, dy), e = y < 11 ? d : Math.abs(dx), a = (Math.atan2(dy, dx) / (Math.PI / 4) + 8) % 8, h = E.hash2((x + y) / 5 | 0, (x - y + 99) / 5 | 0), K = '#140c18';\n    if (e <= 11.5) px.dot(g, x, y, e > 9.3 || y > 42 ? (dx < -1 ? '#a8a2bc' : dx > 1 ? '#4c4660' : '#7a7490') : d < 6.6 ? (d > 5.6 || a % 1 < .2 ? K : d < 1.8 ? '#fff4c8' : a & 1 ? '#e8b040' : '#c8303c')\n      : y < 19 ? '#2a3a98' : !dx || y === 19 || (x + y) % 5 === 0 || (x - y + 99) % 5 === 0 ? K : h > .86 ? '#9a3ac8' : h < .1 ? '#3aa070' : h < .2 ? '#e8b040' : y < 30 ? '#5a82e8' : '#3a58c0'); } });\n  // a deep arched recess: a dark interior, a ring of wedge stones, a pale keystone\n  const arch = (w, h) => bake('arch' + w, w, h, g => { const R = w / 2; for (let y = 0; y < h; y++) for (let x = 0; x < w; x++) {\n    const dx = x + .5 - R, dy = y + .5 - R, d = y < R ? Math.hypot(dx, dy) : Math.abs(dx), seg = y < R ? Math.atan2(dy, dx) * 5 : y / 7, j = seg - Math.floor(seg); if (d > R) continue;\n    px.dot(g, x, y, d > R - 5 ? (j < .14 || d > R - .8 ? '#1e1628' : y < 5 && Math.abs(dx) < 3 ? '#c0bad4' : Math.floor(seg) % 2 ? '#8a84a0' : '#6e6886') : y < R + 6 ? '#07040c' : y < h * .7 ? '#0e0818' : '#170e22'); } });\n  // the graveyard soil in cross-section: wavy strata, buried stones, roots\n  const earth = () => bake('earth', 160, 48, g => { const rnd = E.rng(4), S = ['#4a3848', '#3c2e3e', '#463646', '#30242f'];\n    for (let x = 0; x < 160; x++) for (let k = 0, y = 0; k < 4; k++) { const h = [6, 11, 12, 30][k] + Math.round(2 * Math.sin((x / 160 * (k + 2) + k * .3) * E.TAU)); px.rect(g, x, y, 1, h, S[k]); px.dot(g, x, y + h - 1, '#241a28'); y += h; }\n    for (let i = 0; i < 16; i++) { const x = 8 + rnd() * 144, y = 10 + rnd() * 34, w = 2.5 + rnd() * 3.5, t = E.tones(['#8a8098', '#6e6680', '#7a6a60'][i % 3]);\n      px.ell(g, x, y, w + 1, w * .7 + 1, '#1e1622'); px.ell(g, x, y, w, w * .7, t.sh); px.ell(g, x - .6, y - .6, w - .8, w * .7 - .8, t.base); px.line(g, x - w * .5, y - w * .6, x + w * .2, y - w * .7, t.lt); }\n    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) { let x = 4 + rnd() * 152; const n = 5 + rnd() * 16; for (let y = 0; y < n; y++) { x += (rnd() - .5) * 1.5; px.dot(g, x, y, '#1a1220'); if (y % 3 === 1) px.dot(g, x + 1, y, '#5a4450'); } }\n });\n  // foreground weeds and rubble, scrolling faster than the camera (depth, as in Klonoa)\n  const fore = () => bake('fore', 480, 36, g => { const rnd = E.rng(11); for (let x = 0; x < 480; x += 2 + rnd() * 5) px.line(g, x, 36, x + (rnd() - .5) * 6, 32 - rnd() * 14, '#04050c', rnd() < .3 ? 2 : 1);\n    for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) { const x = rnd() * 480, w = 8 + rnd() * 18; px.ell(g, x, 34, w, 5 + rnd() * 6, '#04050c'); px.ell(g, x - w * .3, 30, w * .4, 1.5, '#1a1e30'); } px.rect(g, 0, 33, 480, 3, '#04050c'); });\n  // a row of cumulus (seamless): shadowed discs, the same discs lit and nudged up-left\n  const CLOUD = ['#8088c0', '#545c96', '#30366a'], clouds = k => bake('cl' + k, 600, 72, g => { const t = E.tones(CLOUD[k]), rnd = E.rng(k + 3), puffs = [];\n    for (let x = 0; x < 600; x += 6 + rnd() * 8) puffs.push([x, 26 + Math.sin(x / 600 * E.TAU * 3 + k) * 7 + rnd() * 5, 7 + rnd() * 10]);\n    for (const [c, dx, dy, f] of [[t.deep, 0, 2, 1], [t.sh, 0, 0, 1], [t.base, -2, -3, .78], [t.lt, -4, -6, .38]]) for (const [x, y, rad] of puffs) for (const o of [-600, 0, 600]) px.disc(g, x + o + dx, y + dy, rad * f, c);\n    px.rect(g, 0, 34, 600, 38, t.deep); });\n  // dead trees: seeded recursive branches with a moonlit edge; also the far treeline\n  const branches = (g, rnd, x, y, a, len, w, c, e) => { const x1 = x + Math.cos(a) * len, y1 = y + Math.sin(a) * len; px.line(g, x, y, x1, y1, c, Math.max(1, Math.round(w)));\n    if (w > 1.4) px.line(g, x + w / 2, y, x1 + w / 3, y1, e); if (len > 4) for (const s of [-1, 1]) branches(g, rnd, x1, y1, a + s * (.3 + rnd() * .45) + (rnd() - .5) * .2, len * (.62 + rnd() * .16), w * .6, c, e); };\n  const tree = seed => bake('tree' + seed, 100, 120, g => { branches(g, E.rng(seed), 50, 120, -1.57 + (seed % 3 - 1) * .12, 36, 7, '#0a0e1a', '#3a4c72'); px.poly(g, [[38, 120], [62, 120], [52, 106], [48, 106]], '#0a0e1a'); });\n  const treeline = () => bake('tl', 320, 90, g => { const rnd = E.rng(5); for (let i = 0; i < 11; i++) branches(g, rnd, 8 + i * 30 + rnd() * 10, 90, -1.57 + (rnd() - .5) * .3, 14 + rnd() * 12, 4, '#0c1830', '#1c2e50'); px.rect(g, 0, 84, 320, 6, '#0c1830'); });\n  // the far castle on its crag: conical roofs, lit windows, a moonlit rim\n  const castle = (c, e, win) => bake('castle' + c, 190, 170, g => { const rnd = E.rng(3), hi = E.mix(e, '#ffffff', .3);\n    px.poly(g, [[0, 170], [26, 120], [50, 104], [66, 112], [82, 98], [150, 100], [168, 120], [190, 170]], c); px.line(g, 150, 100, 190, 170, e, 2); px.rect(g, 30, 84, 130, 20, c); for (let x = 30; x < 160; x += 4) px.rect(g, x, 81, 2, 3, c);\n    for (const [x, w, top, roof] of [[40, 12, 70, 14], [62, 10, 56, 12], [78, 16, 40, 22], [100, 20, 30, 30], [126, 12, 50, 16], [144, 10, 64, 12]]) {\n      px.rect(g, x, top, w, 104 - top, c); px.rect(g, x + w - 3, top, 3, 104 - top, e); px.rect(g, x + w - 1, top, 1, 104 - top, hi); px.poly(g, [[x - 2, top], [x + w + 2, top], [x + w / 2, top - roof]], c); px.line(g, x + w / 2, top - roof, x + w + 1, top, hi);\n      for (let y = top + 5; y < 96; y += 7) if (rnd() < .45) px.rect(g, x + 2 + Math.floor(rnd() * (w - 5)), y, 2, 3, win); } });\n  const grave = v => bake('grave' + v, 18, 27, g => { const t = E.tones(v ? '#6e6c86' : '#848498'); px.rect(g, 2, 8, 14, 19, t.sh); px.disc(g, 9, 8, 7, t.sh); px.rect(g, 3, 8, 11, 18, t.base); px.disc(g, 8, 8, 5.6, t.base);   // tombstone with a cross\n    px.rect(g, 12, 5, 2, 20, t.lt); px.rect(g, 7, 7, 2, 10, t.deep); px.rect(g, 5, 9, 6, 2, t.deep); px.rect(g, 0, 24, 18, 3, '#243e30'); px.rect(g, 2, 23, 5, 1, '#3e6a48'); });\n  // the dialog portrait: a hand-placed E.sprite like SotN's close-ups\n  const VALE = E.sprite([\n    '.................DDDDDDDDD',\n    '.............DDDDDDDCAADDDDD',\n    '........DDDDDDDDDDDAABCCBBDDDDD',\n    '........DDDDDDCCAAACCBCCCCBBBDDDD',\n    '.......DDDDDDDAACCCCCCBCCCCBCBBDD',\n    '.......DDDDDAADBCCCCCCBCCCCCBCCDD',\n    '......DDDDAADCDCBCCCCCCBCCCCBCCCDD',\n    '......DDDDDDDCBCCCCCCCDBCCCCCBCCCD',\n    '......DDDDDDDCBCCCCDCDcCCCCDcBCCCD',\n    '.....DDDDDDDDDBCCCCDCDcCCCCDcCCCDD',\n    '.....DDDDEDDCDBCCCCDCDccCCDEEECCDDD',\n    '....DDDDDEDDCDBCCCCDCDccCCDcccCCDDD',\n    '....DDDDDEDDCDBCCCCDDdcccDcccccDcDD',\n    '....DDDDDEDDCDCBCCCDDdcccDcccccDcD',\n    '....DDDDDEDDCDCBCCDCDccckkkkkkkccD',\n    '....DDDDEDDCDDEBCCDCDcccccwAiwcdcc',\n    '....DDDDEDDCDDEBCCDDdcccccwiiwccdc',\n    '....DDDDEDDCDDEBCCDDdcccccccccccdc',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDEBCCDddccccccbbccccd',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDECCCDddccccccccccccdc',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDECCCDdddcccccccccccca',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDEDCCDdddcccccccccccccc',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDEDCCDdddccccccccccec',\n    '.....DDDEDDCDDEDCCDdddcccccccccccc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDCDdddddccccccccccc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDCDdddddccccccccccc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDCDddddddccccmmmmc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDDDddddddcccccdccc',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEDDDDDddddddcccccce',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEyDDDDddddddcccceer',\n    '.....DDEDDCDDEryyDDdddddddccerrrr',\n    '.....DDEDDCDvErrryddddeeeeeerrrrr',\n    '....DDvvvvvvvrrrrryydddddyrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrryydyyrrrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrrrryrrrrrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrr',\n    'vvvvvvvvvvvvrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrr'], { a: '#fff0e0', b: '#fce0c8', c: '#f0c8a8', d: '#d49a88', e: '#9a6070', A: '#ffffff', B: '#eceaf8', C: '#cfcbe6', D: '#9a94c0', E: '#5a5288',\n    v: '#2a2256', r: '#8a1a30', y: '#f0c860', k: '#2a1020', w: '#f8eef0', i: '#a01830', m: '#b05868' });\n  // 6. CAST, part 1: the bat, drawn by hand. f.beat: wing phase, f.open: spread (0..1). Upside down on the roost and dead, it\n  // rolls upright in the screen plane when it drops (never edge-on); f.tilt cocks it before a dive.\n  function bat(g, x, y, f) {\n    const s = Math.sin(f.beat) * f.open, sp = .3 + .7 * f.open, c = E.tones('#4a2a5a'), a = (f.roost || f.dead ? Math.PI : Math.PI * clamp((f.drop + .05) / .15, 0, 1)) + f.tilt; y -= 9;\n    if (a) { g.save(); g.translate(x, y); g.rotate(a); g.translate(-x, -y); }\n    for (const d of [-1, 1]) {\n      const P = (a, b) => [x + d * a * sp, y + b], w = P(7, -3 - s * 5), tips = [P(15, -s * 6), P(11, 5 - s * 3), P(5, 6)];\n      px.poly(g, [P(2, -2), w, tips[0], P(11, 1 - s * 4), tips[1], P(7, 3 - s), tips[2], P(2, 3)], c.sh);   // membrane, scalloped between the fingers\n      for (const p of tips) px.line(g, w[0], w[1], p[0], p[1], c.lt);\n      px.line(g, x + d * 2, y - 1, w[0], w[1], c.hi, 2); px.dot(g, w[0], w[1] - 1, '#e8dcf0');   // arm and thumb claw\n      px.poly(g, [[x + d * 1, y - 4], [x + d * 3, y - 10], [x + d * 3, y - 3]], c.deep);   // ear\n    }\n    px.ell(g, x, y + 1, 3, 4.5, c.deep); px.ell(g, x - .5, y + 1.5, 2, 3, c.base); px.dot(g, x - 1, y + 2, c.lt);\n    px.disc(g, x, y - 3, 2.6, c.sh); px.dot(g, x - 1, y - 4, c.lt); for (const d of [-1, 1]) { px.dot(g, x + d, y - 3, '#ff4a2a'); px.dot(g, x + d, y - 1, '#ffffff'); }\n    if (a) g.restore();\n  }\n  // 6. CAST, part 2: E.MOVES move sets. HIT per hero move: box [forward, behind, low, high], damage x, knockback, launch.\n  const HIT = { slash: [27, 6, -6, 52, 1, 90, 50], backslash: [27, 6, -6, 52, 1, 90, 70], overhead: [31, 4, -6, 60, 1.5, 170, 90],\n    rising: [26, 4, 8, 80, 1.2, 120, 240], thrust: [38, 0, 6, 40, 1.3, 180, 40], spin: [28, 28, -4, 50, 1.4, 150, 110], plunge: [12, 12, -18, 16, 1.2, 40, 0] };\n  const CHARGE = .45;                                  // seconds of holding X for the spin\n  // Enemies: their own moves, slow enough to dodge (a long wind-up, a glint on the raised blade). heavy: never staggered.\n  // LUNGE: a thrust draws the blade back (the telegraph), then lunges on the hit.\n  const LUNGE = { r0: -1.5, a0: .15, lunge: 4, active: .1 };\n  const FOES = {\n    // a knight: bulky, plate over a violet tabard; a slow two-hand cleave, a lunging thrust\n    knight: { hp: 90, w: 14, h: 34, pts: 300, walk: 20, chase: 34, dmg: 16, heavy: true, make: () => ({ moves: [new E.Attack('twohand', { range: 32, wind: .5, active: .16, recover: .5 }),\n      new E.Attack('thrust', { range: 44, wind: .42, recover: .45, ...LUNGE })], rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'bulky',\n      armor: true, sleeves: 'long', outfit: 'tunic', hat: { style: 'helmet', color: '#8a92b0' }, eyeGlow: '#ff5a30', bladeLen: 14,\n      colors: { skin: '#1a1422', cloth: '#5a2a78', pants: '#767c98', boot: '#3e3e52', metal: '#9aa2bc', glove: '#767c98', belt: '#8a6a3a', trim: '#d8b050' } }) }) },\n    // a skeleton: a heap of bones (down: 1) until you come near; it grabs its blade and rises through 'kneel'\n    skeleton: { hp: 30, w: 12, h: 30, pts: 200, walk: 22, chase: 30, dmg: 10, make: () => ({ sleep: 1, rise: 0, moves: [new E.Attack('overhead', { range: 22, wind: .4, active: .15, recover: .45 }),\n      new E.Attack('thrust', { range: 32, wind: .34, recover: .4, ...LUNGE })],\n      rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.2, build: 'skeleton', weapon: null, cheat: .85, headR: 2.8, hunch: .4, bladeLen: 10, colors: { bone: BONE, cloth: '#1c1424', belt: '#6a5a44', metal: '#a89a80', hilt: '#5a4430' } }) }) },\n    bat: { hp: 12, w: 16, h: 12, pts: 150, make: () => ({ open: 1, beat: 0, cool: 1, drop: -1, tilt: 0 }) }\n  };\n  const DEBRIS = { knight: ['#c0c8e0', '#5a2a78', '#e0d4b8'], skeleton: [BONE, '#b0a68c', '#fff4dc'], bat: ['#4a2a5a', '#8a6a9a', '#1a0c22'] };\n  const S = { shadow: '#05030a', outline: false }, SC = Object.assign({ align: 'center' }, S);   // HUD text styles\n  const talk = new E.Dialog(game, { bg: ['#2a2450', '#0e0a1e'], border: '#e0d0a8' }), say = text => talk.say(text, { name: 'VALE', auto: 2.5,\n    portrait: (g, x, y) => { E.ui.box(g, x - 2, y - 2, 44, 44, { bg: ['#4a1a3a', '#0c0612'], border: '#e0d0a8' }); px.sprite(g, VALE, x, y + 2); if (game.real % 4 < .12) px.rect(g, x + 26, y + 17, 4, 2, '#f0c8a8'); } });   // + a blink\n  const fx = [], lights = [], shafts = [], addFx = (kind, x, z, o) => fx.push(Object.assign({ kind, x, z, t: 0, max: .3, size: 1, c: '#ffffff', vx: 0, vz: 0, dir: 1 }, o));\n  let level, hero, rig, combo, MV, atk, charge, idle, land, foes, coins, candles, drops, lift, lives, score, gold, hp, inv, cheer, clear, spawn, gate, lava, age, hint, darkCv, rimWas;\n  // 8. ANY SCREEN: soil = the band of earth under the level on a screen taller than it (0 on a wide one); FOOT = the level's\n  // foot on screen when the camera rests on the ground (the screen's bottom edge, or the top of the soil): the skies hang from it\n  let soil = 0, FOOT = 240;\n  const soilFor = (b, H) => { const lh = b.y1 - b.y0; return H > lh ? Math.min(160, Math.round((H - lh) * .55)) : 0; };\n\n  function reset(full) {\n    if (full) {\n      level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ROWS, legend: LEGEND, types: TYPES }); lives = 3; score = 0; gold = 0; clear = false; spawn = level.find('hero'); gate = level.find('gate'); drops = []; fx.length = 0;\n      coins = level.findAll('coin').map(c => ({ x: c.x, z: c.z + 3, w: 8, h: 10 }));\n      candles = level.findAll('candle').map(c => ({ kind: 'candle', x: c.x, z: c.z, w: 10, h: 20 }));\n      foes = level.spawns.filter(s => FOES[s.tag] || s.tag === 'roost').map(s => { const kind = FOES[s.tag] ? s.tag : 'bat';   // a roost is a bat asleep, wings folded\n        return Object.assign({ kind, x: s.x, z: s.z, vx: 0, vz: 0, t: Math.random() * 9, flash: 0, stun: 0, cool: 0, dir: -1 }, FOES[kind], FOES[kind].make(), s.tag === 'roost' && { roost: true, open: 0 }); });\n      const m = level.find('lift'), cols = []; lift = { x0: m.x, x: m.x, z: m.z + 10, w: 44, h: 6, vx: 0, vz: 0, t: 0 };\n      for (let cx = 0; cx < level.w; cx++) if (level.cell(cx, 0) === 9) cols.push(cx * T); lava = { x0: cols[0], x1: cols[cols.length - 1] + T, z: T };\n    }\n    hero = new E.Platformer({ x: spawn.x, z: spawn.z, w: 10, h: 30, run: 105, jump: 300, dash: 250, dashTime: .2 });\n    // The hero: 'heroic' build, cheat: .7 turns him to the camera; a readable palette on dark stages.\n    rig = rig || new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', outfit: 'coat', hair: { style: 'long', len: 4 }, sleeves: 'long', stride: 6, lift: 4.6, swing: 5.5, lean: .06, bladeLen: 13, cheat: .7, face: { eyes: 'big', bangs: .2 }, cape: { len: 5, width: 6, seg: 2.6 },\n      colors: { skin: '#f4d2b0', hair: '#e4e0f4', cloth: '#b02840', coat: '#861a30', pants: '#d8ccb8', boot: '#3a2638', belt: '#3a2020', trim: '#f0c860', glove: '#f4f0e8', metal: '#dce6f4', hilt: '#f0c050',\n        cape: '#3a2e70', capeIn: '#5a6ad8', eye: '#2a1840' } });\n    // the hero's moves, tuned for the side view: X chains a chest-high chop > a backhand around the body from behind the head\n    // (plane: 'ground', the torso turns) > a stepping, crouching overhead; up + X rising; down + X in the air a plunge (a tuck,\n    // then the blade driven below the feet); X in a dash thrust; hold X: spin\n    combo = new E.Combo([new E.Attack('slash', { a0: 1.35, a1: -.45 }), new E.Attack('backslash', { plane: 'ground', a0: -2.6, a1: .6, z0: 5, z1: -9, twist: 2 }),\n      new E.Attack('overhead', { lunge: 3.5, crouch: .7, a1: -.9 })], { window: .3 });\n    const spin = new E.Attack('spin', { a0: .3 });      // side views: a windmill loop in front that ends blade forward\n    MV = { rising: new E.Attack('rising', { a1: 1.45, recover: .42 }), plunge: new E.Attack('plunge', { hand: 'both', lean: .45, a0: 1.3, r0: 4, r1: 9.5, wind: .1, active: .28 }), thrust: new E.Attack('thrust'), spin,\n      charge: { spec: { ...spin.spec, plane: 'side', a0: 2.4 }, phase: 'wind' } };   // charging holds the blade raised behind the head; on release it chops into the spin\n    atk = null; charge = idle = land = 0; hp = MAXHP; inv = full ? 0 : 1.5; cheer = 0;   // inv: blink after a respawn\n    game.follow(hero, { z: 34, lead: 24 });            // the camera tracks the hero, looking ahead\n  }\n  // 7. HITS. A kill: an additive glow, then the engine explosion with debris in the victim's colors\n  function burst(x, z, s, colors) { addFx('glow', x, z, { max: .25, size: 22 * s, c: '#ff9a40' }); P.explosion(x, 0, z, s, { debris: colors, flash: false }); }\n  function crumble(f) {                                // the end of a foe: a burst; a skeleton falls apart into a skull and bones\n    f.corpse = 0; if (f.kind !== 'skeleton') return burst(f.x, f.z + (f.rig ? 6 : f.h / 2), f.rig ? .9 : .6, DEBRIS[f.kind]);\n    P.dust(f.x, 0, f.z + 2, 12, { color: '#8a8070' }); A.sfx('bump', { pitch: .6 });\n    for (let i = 0; i < 7; i++) addFx('blade', f.x + E.rand(-9, 9), f.z + 4, { max: 1.5, vx: E.rand(-50, 50), vz: E.rand(40, 130), a: E.rand(0, 6), spin: E.rand(-14, 14), L: i ? E.rand(4, 8) : 0, c: BONE });\n  }\n  // a fallen fighter drops the sword (weapon: null): it tumbles, bounces and lies flat (see update)\n  function disarm(rg, dir, max) {\n    const h = rg.hand(), t = rg.tip(); rg.o.weapon = null;\n    addFx('blade', (h[0] + t[0]) / 2, (h[2] + t[2]) / 2, { max, vx: dir * 50, vz: 110, a: Math.atan2(t[2] - h[2], t[0] - h[0]), spin: dir * -12, L: rg.o.bladeLen * rg.o.size, c: rg.C.metal, h: rg.C.hilt });\n  }\n  function hurt(dir, n = 10) {\n    if (inv > 0 || clear || hero.ko) return; hp -= n; inv = 1.2; hero.knock(dir); A.sfx('hurt'); game.flash('#ff2030', .12, .25); stop();   // knocked back (the hurt pose)\n    game.hitFx(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24, { power: 1.2, angle: dir > 0 ? 0 : Math.PI, color: '#ff8080', sound: false, damage: n, textZ: 32, textScale: 2, textColor: '#ff6a6a' });\n    if (hp <= 0) die();\n  }\n  const stop = () => { if (atk) atk.cancel(); charge = 0; };   // a hit interrupts the hero's move\n  function die() {                                     // knocked out: pose 'down', then respawn or game over\n    if (hero.ko) return; hp = 0; lives--; hero.ko = 1.6; stop(); disarm(rig, -hero.facing, 1.6); A.sfx('die'); game.flash('#ffffff', .5, .7); game.shake(4);\n    addFx('glow', hero.x, hero.z + 16, { max: .5, size: 30, c: '#ff4060' }); P.sparks(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 16, 14); P.bits(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 20, 8, ['#b02840', '#e4e0f4', '#f0c860']);\n  }\n  // one hit: game.hitFx (hit-stop, shake, star, sparks, sound, a number), a slash flare, chips and a flinch (f.flash),\n  // knocked back (and up) by the move's HIT speeds\n  function damage(f, n, dir, crit, k) {\n    f.hp -= n; f.flash = .3; f.vx = dir * (k ? k[5] : 60) * (f.heavy ? .3 : 1); const z = f.z + f.h * .55;\n    if (!f.heavy) { f.stun = .4; f.vz = k ? k[6] : 60; f.swoop = null; if (f.atk) f.atk.cancel(); }\n    f.pop = game.time - f.popT < .8 ? (f.pop + 1) % 3 : 0; f.popT = game.time;   // quick hits stack their numbers in rows\n    game.hitFx(f.x - dir * 3, 0, z, { power: crit ? 2 : 1, angle: dir > 0 ? 0 : Math.PI, color: crit ? '#ffd040' : '#ffe8a0', damage: n, textZ: 26 + f.pop * 16, textScale: 2, textColor: crit ? '#ffd040' : '#ffffff' });\n    addFx('cut', f.x, z, { max: .14, dir, c: crit ? '#ffe066' : '#c8f0ff' }); P.bits(f.x, 0, z, 4, DEBRIS[f.kind]);\n    if (f.hp > 0) return;\n    f.dead = true; f.corpse = f.rig ? 1.2 : .5; score += f.pts; P.text(f.x, 0, f.z + f.h + 50, '+' + f.pts, '#ffe066');\n    if (f.rig) disarm(f.rig, dir, 1.2);\n  }\n  function snuff(c) {                                  // a candle: a puff of light, chips and loot that drops\n    c.dead = true; addFx('glow', c.x, c.z + 16, { max: .2, size: 16, c: '#ffb050' }); P.sparks(c.x, 0, c.z + 16, 6); P.bits(c.x, 0, c.z + 12, 8, ['#e8dcc0', '#ffb040', '#4a4058']); A.sfx('bump');\n    drops.push({ x: c.x, z: c.z + 8, w: 8, h: 8, vx: 0, vz: 140, heart: Math.random() < .35 });\n  }\n  // facing angle; in the 2.5D view foes turn 3/4 to the camera\n  const face = dir => (dir < 0) * Math.PI + dir * (game.view.pitchDeg > 5) * .5;\n  // is f inside the box [forward, behind, low, high] ahead of a (facing dir)?\n  const within = (a, dir, f, [fw, bk, lo, hi]) => { const dx = (f.x - a.x) * dir; return dx > -bk - f.w / 2 && dx < fw + f.w / 2 && f.z < a.z + hi && f.z + f.h > a.z + lo; };\n  function updateFoe(f, dt) {\n    const dx = hero.x - f.x, dz = hero.z - f.z, near = Math.abs(dx) < 90 && Math.abs(dz) < 30 && !(hero.ko > 0), facing = face(f.dir); f.t += dt; f.flash -= dt; f.stun -= dt; f.cool -= dt;\n    const ov = (hero.w + f.w) / 2 - Math.abs(dx), push = ov > 0 && Math.abs(dz) < f.h && f.onGround ? (Math.sign(dx) || f.dir) * ov * 10 : 0;   // never stand inside the hero: step out\n    if (f.dead) {                                      // carried by the blow, a rig dies (stagger, buckle, topple), a bat tumbles; then they crumble\n      f.corpse -= dt; f.vz -= 700 * dt; f.vx = approach(f.vx, 0, 250 * dt) - push; level.move(f, dt); if (f.corpse <= 0) crumble(f);\n      return f.rig && f.rig.update(dt, { x: f.x, y: 0, z: f.z, vx: 0, vy: 0, facing, pose: 'die' });\n    }\n    if (Math.abs(dx) > 260) return;                    // sleep until near, like 16-bit enemies\n    if (f.kind === 'bat') return fly(f, dt, dx);\n    if (f.sleep) {                                     // a heap of bones until the hero comes near; then it pulls itself up\n      if (Math.abs(dx) < 100 && Math.abs(dz) < 40) { f.sleep = 0; f.rise = .9; f.cool = 1.5; f.rig.o.weapon = 'sword'; f.dir = Math.sign(dx) || -1; P.dust(f.x, 0, f.z + 2, 10, { color: '#8a8070' }); A.sfx('bump', { pitch: .5 }); }\n      return f.rig.update(dt, { x: f.x, y: 0, z: f.z, vx: 0, vy: 0, facing, down: 1 });\n    }\n    if (f.rise > 0) { f.rise -= dt; return f.rig.update(dt, { x: f.x, y: 0, z: f.z, vx: 0, vy: 0, facing, down: clamp((f.rise - .5) * 2.5, 0, 1), pose: f.rise > .2 ? 'kneel' : null }); }   // sit up, kneel, stand\n    // walkers: patrol, close in on a near hero (weapon ready), wait at ledges, telegraph and strike\n    const busy = f.atk && f.atk.busy, free = !busy && f.stun <= 0 && f.onGround;\n    if (near && free) f.dir = Math.sign(dx) || f.dir;\n    const ahead = level.groundBelow(f.x + f.dir * 9, f.z + 1), blocked = f.hitWall === f.dir || (f.onGround && (ahead === null || ahead < f.z - 1));\n    if (blocked && !near) f.dir = -f.dir;              // turn at walls and ledges\n    f.vx = (f.stun > 0 || !f.onGround ? approach(f.vx, 0, (f.onGround ? 500 : 60) * dt) : busy || blocked || (near && Math.abs(dx) < f.moves[0].spec.range - 4) ? 0 : f.dir * (near ? f.chase : f.walk)) - push;   // stunned: slide back; in reach: hold ground\n    const m = near && free && f.cool <= 0 && Math.abs(dz) < 24 && f.moves.find(mv => Math.abs(dx) < mv.spec.range);\n    if (m) { f.atk = m; m.start(); f.cool = .8; }\n    if (f.atk) { if (f.atk.update(dt) === 'active') A.sfx('swing', { vol: .6, pitch: .7 }); f.atk.hits([hero], h => f.atk.u > .5 && within(f, f.dir, h, [f.atk.spec.range + 6, 8, -6, 52]), () => hurt(f.dir, f.dmg)); }   // once the blade is low (or the thrust out)\n    f.vz -= 900 * dt; level.move(f, dt);\n    f.rig.update(dt, { x: f.x, y: 0, z: f.z, vx: f.stun > 0 ? 0 : f.vx, vy: 0, facing: face(f.dir), attack: f.atk && f.atk.state, hurt: f.stun > 0 || f.flash > 0, stance: near ? 'ready' : null });   // every hit flinches\n  }\n  // a bat roosts until the hero passes below, drops, hovers over his head and swoops through him. The telegraph: it rears up\n  // beating hard, then folds its wings and cocks back.\n  function fly(f, dt, dx) {\n    const s = f.swoop, tuck = s && s.u > -.12 && s.u < 0, d = s ? Math.sign(s.x1 - s.x0) : 0;\n    f.open = approach(f.open, f.roost ? 0 : tuck ? .1 : s && s.u > 0 ? .5 : 1, dt * (tuck ? 12 : 4)); f.beat += dt * (f.roost ? 0 : !s ? 15 : s.u < 0 ? 28 : 7);\n    f.tilt = approach(f.tilt, tuck ? -.5 * d : s && s.u > 0 ? .3 * d : 0, dt * 8);   // lean away, then into the dive\n    if (f.roost) { if (Math.abs(dx) < 80 && hero.z < f.z) { f.roost = false; f.drop = .25; f.vz = -90; f.cool = .9; A.sfx('whoosh', { vol: .4, pitch: 1.6 }); } return; }\n    f.drop -= dt;                                    // it falls, unfolding, then rolls upright\n    if (f.stun > 0) { f.x += f.vx * dt; f.z += f.vz * dt; f.vx = approach(f.vx, 0, 300 * dt); f.vz = approach(f.vz, 0, 400 * dt); return; }   // knocked away (or bouncing off)\n    if (s) {\n      s.u += dt / .8; const u = clamp(s.u, 0, 1); f.x = lerp(s.x0, s.x1, u); f.z = s.z0 + (s.u < 0 ? 5 * Math.sin(-s.u / .3 * Math.PI) : 6 * u - Math.sin(u * Math.PI) * s.dip);\n      if (s.u >= 1) { f.swoop = null; f.cool = 1.4; f.vx = Math.sign(s.x1 - s.x0) * 60; } return;\n    }\n    f.vz = approach(f.vz, 0, 150 * dt); f.vx = approach(f.vx, Math.sign(dx) * 48, 110 * dt); f.x += f.vx * dt;\n    f.z = approach(f.z + f.vz * dt, hero.z + 46 + Math.sin(f.t * 3) * 5, 50 * dt);\n    if (f.cool <= 0 && Math.abs(dx) < 40) { f.swoop = { u: -.3, x0: f.x, z0: f.z, x1: hero.x + Math.sign(dx || 1) * 40, dip: f.z - hero.z - 14 }; A.sfx('whoosh', { vol: .5, pitch: 1.3 }); }\n  }\n  // a buffered X picks a move from the context (cutting the last one's follow-through). The plunge holds its stab until it\n  // lands or pogos off a foe. Holding X charges the spin.\n  function fight(inp, dt, diving) {\n    if (inp.buffered('attack') && !(atk && atk.busy && atk.state.phase !== 'recover') && !hero.climbing) {\n      const m = hero.dashing > 0 ? MV.thrust : inp.down('up') ? MV.rising : inp.down('down') && hero.air ? MV.plunge : combo;\n      if (atk && atk !== m) atk.cancel();\n      if (m === combo ? combo.press() : m.start(true)) {\n        inp.consume('attack'); atk = m; charge = 0;\n        if (m === MV.thrust) hero.dashing = Math.max(hero.dashing, .16);   // the thrust rides the dash on\n        if (m === MV.rising && hero.onGround) hero.vz = 170;              // and the rising cut hops\n      }\n    }\n    if (inp.down('attack') && !(atk && atk.busy) && !hero.climbing) { if ((charge += dt) >= CHARGE && charge - dt < CHARGE) A.sfx('charge'); }   // charged: the blade glints (see draw)\n    else { if (charge >= CHARGE && !inp.down('attack')) { atk = MV.spin; MV.spin.start(); } charge = 0; }   // released charged: spin\n    if (atk && !(diving && atk.active && atk.u > .9) && atk.update(dt) === 'active') A.sfx(atk === MV.spin || atk.state.spec.name === 'overhead' ? 'whoosh' : 'swing');\n    if (!atk || !atk.active) return;\n    const name = atk.state.spec.name, k = HIT[name];\n    atk.hits(foes.concat(candles), f => atk.active && atk.state.u > .3 && !f.sleep && !(f.rise > 0) && within(hero, hero.facing, f, k), f => {   // 16-24 damage x the move's, one hit in seven a critical\n      if (f.kind === 'candle') return snuff(f);\n      const crit = Math.random() < .15; damage(f, Math.round(E.randInt(16, 24) * k[4] * (crit ? 2 : 1)), Math.sign(f.x - hero.x) || hero.facing, crit, k);\n      if (name === 'plunge') { hero.vz = 250; atk.cancel(); rig.kick(3); }   // pogo off whatever the plunge stabs\n    });\n  }\n  function update(dt) {\n    for (const f of fx) { f.t += dt; f.x += f.vx * dt; f.z += f.vz * dt;\n      if (f.kind !== 'blade') continue;               // a dropped sword falls, bounces and settles flat\n      const gz = level.groundBelow(f.x, f.z + 8); f.vz -= 700 * dt; f.a += f.spin * dt;\n      if (gz !== null && f.z < gz + 1) { f.z = gz + 1; f.vz = f.vz < -90 ? -f.vz * .3 : 0; f.vx *= .6; f.spin = (Math.round(f.a / Math.PI) * Math.PI - f.a) * 10; }\n    }\n    E.prune(fx, f => f.t >= f.max);\n    const inp = game.input, ko = hero.ko > 0; inv -= dt; cheer -= dt; land -= dt; age += dt; if (!talk.open && age > 2) hint -= dt;\n    talk.update(dt);\n    if (age > 1.8 && age - dt <= 1.8 && !clear) say('The dead will not rest tonight. I must reach the gate.');\n    lift.t += dt; const nx = lift.x0 + Math.sin(lift.t * .8) * 64; lift.vx = (nx - lift.x) / dt; lift.x = nx;   // the lift glides over the moat and carries riders\n    if (ko) { hero.ko -= dt; if (!level.touching(hero, 'hazard')) hero.update(dt, level, { x: 0 }, [lift]); if (hero.ko <= 0) return lives > 0 ? reset(false) : game.go('over', { from: 'platformer' }); }\n    else {\n      const diving = atk === MV.plunge && atk.busy && atk.phase !== 'recover' && hero.air;\n      if (diving) hero.vz = Math.min(hero.vz, -300);\n      hero.update(dt, level, clear ? { x: gate.x > hero.x + 2 ? .4 : 0 } : inp, [lift]);\n      if (hero.jumped) { A.sfx('jump'); rig.kick(3); }                                 // stretch on take-off, squash and dust on landing\n      if (hero.landed) { A.sfx(diving ? 'stomp' : 'land'); rig.kick(diving ? -8 : -5); for (const s of [-1, 1]) addFx('smoke', hero.x + s * 6, hero.z + 2, { vx: s * (diving ? 70 : 30), vz: 6, max: .35, size: 2.5 }); }\n      if (hero.landed && diving) { game.shake(3); land = .3; P.dust(hero.x, 0, hero.z, 14, { speed: 70 }); P.sparks(hero.x + hero.facing * 4, 0, hero.z, 6); }   // the dive's impact\n      if (!clear) fight(inp, dt, diving);\n      for (const c of coins) if (!c.got && E.overlapBox(hero, c)) { c.got = true; gold++; score += 100; A.sfx('coin'); P.glints(c.x, 0, c.z + 5, 4, '#fff6c0', 10); }\n    }\n    for (const d of drops) {                           // candle loot falls and is picked up with a cheer (a heart heals 20)\n      d.vz -= 700 * dt; level.move(d, dt);\n      if (d.got || ko || !E.overlapBox(hero, d)) continue; d.got = true; cheer = .7; P.glints(d.x, 0, d.z + 4, 6, '#fff6c0', 12);\n      if (d.heart) { hp = Math.min(MAXHP, hp + 20); A.sfx('heal'); P.text(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 56, 20, '#7aff8a', { scale: 2, bounce: true }); } else { gold += 5; score += 500; A.sfx('powerup'); P.text(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 56, '+5', '#ffe066', { scale: 2 }); }\n    }\n    for (const f of foes) {                            // enemies: stomp from above (bounce) or take a hit\n      if (f.dead && !(f.corpse > 0)) continue; updateFoe(f, dt); if (ko || f.dead || f.sleep || f.rise > 0 || !E.overlapBox(hero, f)) continue;\n      const d = hero.x < f.x ? 1 : -1; if (hero.vz < 0 && hero.z > f.z + f.h * .5) { hero.vz = 230; A.sfx('stomp'); damage(f, 20, d, false); continue; }\n      hurt(-d, f.dmg || 8); if (f.kind === 'bat') Object.assign(f, { swoop: null, stun: .35, cool: 1.4, vx: d * 110, vz: 130 });   // a bat bounces off\n    }\n    if (Math.random() < dt * 9) {                      // ambient life: fireflies, dust, embers and smoke over the lava\n      const x = hero.x + E.rand(-170, 190), lv = x > lava.x0 && x < lava.x1, ff = x < 330;\n      addFx('mote', x, lv ? lava.z + 4 : hero.z + E.rand(0, 110), { vx: E.rand(-8, 8) - (ff ? 0 : 6), vz: lv ? E.rand(25, 45) : E.rand(-6, 8), max: E.rand(1.2, 2.6), size: E.pick([1, 1, 2]), c: lv ? '#ff7a2a' : ff ? '#b8ff70' : '#a898c8' });\n      if (lv && Math.random() < .3) addFx('smoke', x, lava.z + 4, { vz: E.rand(12, 22), vx: -4, max: 2.2, size: E.rand(6, 10) });\n    }\n    if (!clear && !ko && (level.touching(hero, 'hazard') || hero.z < -40)) die();\n    if (!clear && !ko && Math.abs(hero.x - gate.x) < 12 && hero.z >= gate.z - 2) {   // goal: step into the castle gate\n      clear = true; stop(); score += 1000; A.music('victory'); game.flash('#fff4d0', .6, .7); P.glints(gate.x, 0, gate.z + 20, 12, '#fff6c0', 30); game.after(6, () => game.go('title'));\n      say(['The gate is open. Whatever waits inside, I am ready.', 'Score ' + score + '   Best ' + best('platformer', score)]);\n    }\n    // poses: cheer (gate, pickups); down when knocked out; crouch (a tuck) over the plunge and its landing; hips after a long idle,\n    // blade tip down. The sword is held ready, except running and on ladders (weapon: null), where he turns his back to the\n    // camera and climbs hand over hand.\n    const won = clear && hero.onGround && Math.abs(gate.x - hero.x) < 3, busy = atk && atk.busy, cheering = won || (cheer > 0 && hero.onGround && !busy && !charge);\n    idle = hero.onGround && !hero.vx && !busy && !charge ? idle + dt : 0; if (!(hero.ko > 0)) rig.o.weapon = hero.climbing ? null : 'sword';\n    rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack: (atk && atk.state) || (charge > .1 ? { ...MV.charge, u: clamp((charge - .1) / (CHARGE - .1), 0, 1) } : null), ...(hero.climbing && { facing: -Math.PI / 2 }),\n      stance: cheering || hero.climbing || idle > 6 || (hero.onGround && Math.abs(hero.vx) > 40) ? null : 'ready', point: idle > 6, aim: -.5, expr: cheering ? 'smile' : hero.stun > 0 ? 'wince' : null,\n      pose: hero.ko > 0 ? 'down' : cheering ? 'cheer' : land > 0 || (atk === MV.plunge && busy && hero.air) ? 'crouch' : idle > 6 ? 'hips' : null }));   // the plunge squats over its blade\n  }\n  function coin(g, x, y, ph) {                         // spinning coin: its width follows |cos| of a phase\n    const w = Math.abs(Math.cos(ph)) * 5; px.ell(g, x, y, Math.max(1, w), 6, '#a0600c'); px.ell(g, x - .5, y - .5, Math.max(.6, w - 1.2), 4.8, '#f8c030');\n    if (w > 2) { px.rect(g, x - 1, y - 3, 1, 5, '#fff2a8'); px.dot(g, x + 1, y + 3, '#c07a10'); }\n  }\n  // lights for the light pass: a world point, a radius, a color, a strength (flick() makes flames breathe)\n  const lit = (r, x, z, R, c, a, y = 0) => { const [sx, sy] = r.w(x, y, z), p = Math.max(6, Math.round(R * r.view.scale / 6) * 6); if (sx > -p && sx < r.bw + p && sy > -p && sy < r.bh + p) lights.push({ x: Math.round(sx), y: Math.round(sy), R: p, c, a }); };\n  const flick = (x, k = 1) => 1 + .12 * k * Math.sin(game.real * 13 + x) * Math.sin(game.real * 7.3 + x * 3);\n  // ground mist over the graveyard: the fog band at two depths and speeds, behind the play layer and faintly in front\n  const mist = (r, g, Z, a, k, y) => { if (Z.bg[0] < .01) return; const [, sy] = r.w(0, y, 2 * T), o = ((r.ix * k + game.time * 5 * k) % 320 + 320) % 320;\n    blend(g, 'source-over', a * Z.bg[0], () => { for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 320) g.drawImage(fog('#a8b8e8'), x, Math.round(sy) - 24); }); };\n  function sky(r, g, Z) {\n    let first = true;\n    Z.bg.forEach((w, i) => { if (w > .01) { blend(g, 'source-over', first ? 1 : w, () => BG[i].draw(r)); first = false; } });   // each Backdrop draws with the alpha we set\n    const mo = Z.bg[0] + Z.bg[2], mx = Math.round(r.W * .76 - r.ix * .01), my = 44, t = E.tones('#f0ead8');\n    if (mo > .01) {   // the moon: a smooth additive halo, then craters on a lit disc\n      blend(g, 'lighter', .55 * mo, () => g.drawImage(halo(60, '#4a68c8'), mx - 60, my - 60));\n      blend(g, 'source-over', mo, () => { px.disc(g, mx, my, 16, t.sh); px.disc(g, mx - 1, my - 1, 15, t.base); px.disc(g, mx - 4, my - 5, 7, t.lt); for (const [a, b, c] of [[5, 3, 4], [-6, 6, 3], [2, -8, 2], [-2, 10, 2]]) px.disc(g, mx + a, my + b, c, t.sh); });\n    }\n    // the far castle (blue by moonlight, red over the moat), mist at its foot, a faster treeline\n    const cx = Math.round(r.W * .28 - r.ix * .03), cy = Math.round(FOOT * .72 - r.iy * .04);\n    for (const [i, c, e, w] of [[0, '#16223e', '#34507e', '#ffc868'], [1, '#3a0e18', '#8a2a20', '#ff8a3a']]) if (Z.bg[i] > .01) blend(g, 'source-over', Z.bg[i], () => g.drawImage(castle(c, e, w), cx, cy - 170));\n    blend(g, 'source-over', .8, () => { const o = ((r.ix * .05 + game.time * 3) % 320 + 320) % 320; for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 320) g.drawImage(fog(Z.bg[1] > .5 ? '#b8482a' : '#5a7aa8'), x, cy - 30); });\n    if (Z.bg[0] > .01) blend(g, 'source-over', Z.bg[0], () => { const ty = Math.round(FOOT * .9 - 90 - r.iy * .12), o = ((r.ix * .22) % 320 + 320) % 320; for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 320) g.drawImage(treeline(), x, ty); px.rect(g, 0, ty + 90, r.bw, r.bh, '#0c1830'); });\n    if (Z.bg[2] > .01) blend(g, 'source-over', Z.bg[2], () => { for (let k = 0; k < 3; k++) {   // the sea of clouds: three billowy rows, nearer = darker and faster\n      const y = Math.round(FOOT * (.58 + k * .12) - r.iy * .05 * (k + 1)), o = ((r.ix * (.05 + k * .07) + game.time * (2 + k * 2)) % 600 + 600) % 600;\n      for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 600) g.drawImage(clouds(k), x, y); px.rect(g, 0, y + 70, r.bw, r.bh, E.tones(CLOUD[k]).deep); } });\n  }\n  function decor(r, g) {                               // baked decals behind the play layer; engine props are queued\n    for (const s of back.spawns) {\n      const [sx, sy] = r.w(s.x, -8, s.z), x = Math.round(sx), y = Math.round(sy), fl = FLAME[s.tag];\n      if (x < -90 || x > r.bw + 90) continue;\n      if (s.tag === 'glass') { g.drawImage(glass(), x - 11, y - 46); lit(r, s.x, s.z + 18, 40, '#5a7aff', .22, -8); shafts.push([x, y, (s.z - (level.groundBelow(s.x + 30, s.z) || 0)) * r.view.scale + 30]); }   // + a light shaft\n      else if (s.tag === 'arch') { g.drawImage(arch(44, 76), x - 10, y - 76); if (Math.sin(game.real * .7 + s.cx) > .8) blend(g, 'lighter', 1, () => { px.dot(g, x + 9, y - 40, '#ff4030'); px.dot(g, x + 13, y - 40, '#ff4030'); }); }   // eyes in the dark\n      else if (s.tag === 'tree') g.drawImage(tree(s.cx), x - 40, y - 100);\n      else if (s.tag === 'grave') g.drawImage(grave(s.cx % 2), x - 9, y - 26);\n      else if (STONES[s.tag] || !PROP[s.tag]) { const o = STONES[s.tag]; if (o) r.shape(o.id, s.x, o.y, s.z, o); }   // (no set on the page: no stones)\n      else { const o = PROP[s.tag], z = o.top ? s.z + T : s.z; r.prop(o.name || s.tag, s.x, o.y, z, o); if (fl) lit(r, s.x, z + fl, s.tag === 'lamp' ? 44 : s.tag === 'offering' ? 30 : 56, '#ff9a40', .45 * flick(s.x), -6); }\n    }\n    for (let i = 0; i < FRONT.length; i++) { const o = FP[FRONT[i]]; if (o) r.prop(o.name, i * T + 8 + (o.dx || 0), 7, 2 * T, o); }   // the FRONT row, in front of the path\n  }\n  // LAVA: a rolling surface, a bright crest, molten bands darkening with depth, drifting crust, streaks and bubbles that pop.\n  // It also lights the walls (lit).\n  function lavaDraw(r, g) {\n    const [x0, y0] = r.w(lava.x0, 8, lava.z), [x1, y1] = r.w(lava.x1, 8, 0), t = game.time, B = ['#fff4c0', '#ffd050', '#ffa030', '#f07020', '#d04818', '#a02c14', '#6a1810', '#3a0c0c'];\n    for (let x = Math.max(0, Math.round(x0)); x < Math.min(r.bw, Math.round(x1)); x++) {\n      const wx = x + r.ix, top = Math.round(y0 + Math.sin(wx * .09 + t * 2) * 1.5 + Math.sin(wx * .23 - t * 3.1) * .8), n = E.noise2(wx * .07 + t * .25, t * .1);\n      let y = top; for (let k = 0; k < B.length; k++) { const h = k < 7 ? [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6][k] : y1 - y + 2; px.rect(g, x, y, 1, h, B[k]); y += h; }\n      if (n > .6) { px.rect(g, x, top + 1, 1, n > .7 ? 4 : 2, '#2a0a08'); if (n < .62) px.dot(g, x, top + 1, '#ff8a20'); }\n      if ((wx + Math.floor(t * 18)) % 29 < 7) px.dot(g, x, top + 6 + wx % 3, '#ffc040');\n    }\n    for (let k = 0; k < 4; k++) { const u = (t * .6 + k * .29) % 1, bx = lava.x0 + 8 + E.hash2(Math.floor(t * .6 + k * .29), k) * (lava.x1 - lava.x0 - 16), [sx, sy] = r.w(bx, 8, lava.z);\n      if (u < .85) { px.disc(g, sx, sy + 1 - u * 2, 1 + u * 3, '#ff9a30'); px.dot(g, sx - 1, sy - u * 4, '#fff4c0'); } else blend(g, 'lighter', 1, () => px.disc(g, sx, sy - 2, 5, '#ff6a20')); }\n    for (let x = lava.x0 + 8; x < lava.x1; x += 40) lit(r, x, lava.z + 6, 64, '#ff5a1a', .42 * flick(x, 2));\n  }\n  function drawFx(r) {                                 // FX: additive glows, slash flares and motes; half-transparent smoke\n    for (const f of fx) r.queue(f.x, 6, f.z, g => {\n      const u = f.t / f.max, [x, y] = r.w(f.x, 0, f.z), s = r.view.scale;\n      if (f.kind === 'blade') { const c = Math.cos(f.a) * f.L * s / 2, n = Math.sin(f.a) * f.L * s / 2;   // a dropped sword (blade, hilt), a bone or a skull\n        if (f.max - f.t < .3 && (f.t * 20 | 0) % 2) return;   // blinks out\n        if (!f.h) return f.L ? px.line(g, x - c, y + n, x + c, y - n, f.c, 2) : (px.disc(g, x, y - 2, 2.6 * s, f.c), px.rect(g, x - 1, y - 2, 3, 1, '#1a1020'));\n        px.line(g, x - c * .6, y + n * .6, x + c, y - n, f.c); return px.line(g, x - c, y + n, x - c * .6, y + n * .6, f.h, 2); }\n      if (f.kind === 'smoke') return blend(g, 'source-over', .5 * (1 - u), () => { px.disc(g, x, y, f.size * s * (.6 + u), '#2e2a3a'); px.disc(g, x - 1, y - 1, f.size * s * (.35 + u * .6), '#4e4860'); });\n      blend(g, 'lighter', f.kind === 'mote' ? Math.sin(u * Math.PI) : 1 - u, () => {\n        if (f.kind === 'glow') { const R = Math.round(f.size * s / 6) * 6; g.drawImage(halo(R, f.c), x - R, y - R); }\n        else if (f.kind === 'cut') { const L = 22 - u * 8, k = f.dir; px.poly(g, [[x - L * k, y - L * .55], [x + 2 * k, y - 2], [x + L * k, y + L * .55], [x - 2 * k, y + 2]], f.c); px.line(g, x - L * .7 * k, y - L * .38, x + L * .7 * k, y + L * .38, '#ffffff'); }   // SotN's slash flare across the target\n        else px.rect(g, x, y, f.size, f.size, f.c);\n      });\n    }, { bias: 2 });\n  }\n  function draw(r) {\n    const view = r.view, t = game.time, g = r.ctx, Z = zoneAt(hero.x), tilted = view.pitchDeg > 5, b = level.bounds(view);\n    lights.length = shafts.length = 0; soil = soilFor(b, r.H); FOOT = r.H - soil;\n    sky(r, g, Z);                                      // 1. skies (cross-faded), moon, castle, mist, clouds\n    back.draw(r); decor(r, g); mist(r, g, Z, .55, .6, -8);   // 2. castle walls, decals, props on them, the far mist layer\n    level.draw(r); lavaDraw(r, g);                     // 3. the play layer, the lava painted over its tiles, the earth cut\n    if (soil) {   // 8. the SOIL under the level: the earth cut's strata, darkening; above the clouds it gives way to them\n      const top = Math.round(b.y1 - 4 - r.iy), o = ((r.ix % 160) + 160) % 160;\n      blend(g, 'source-over', 1 - Z.bg[2], () => { for (let x = -o; x < r.bw; x += 160) g.drawImage(earth(), x, top); for (let k = 0; k < 4; k++) px.rect(g, 0, top + 48 + k * 10, r.bw, k < 3 ? 10 : r.bh, ['#2a2030', '#211a28', '#18131f', '#100c16'][k]); });\n    }\n    { const [ex, ey] = r.w(0, 8, 2 * T), [x1, eb] = r.w(20 * T, 8, 0), w = (x1 - ex) / 3; if (x1 > 0) for (let k = 0; k < 3; k++) g.drawImage(earth(), Math.round(ex + k * w), Math.round(ey) + 3, Math.round(w), Math.round(eb - ey) - 3); }\n    const shadow = (x, z) => { const gz = tilted && level.groundBelow(x, z); if (gz !== null && gz !== false) r.shadow(x, 0, 6, .35, '#000', gz); };   // contact shadows in the 2.5D view\n    for (const c of candles) if (!c.dead) {            // floating candles on iron cups: slash them for loot\n      r.prop('candle', c.x, -2, c.z + 5, { size: 1.1 }); lit(r, c.x, c.z + 18, 40, '#ffb060', .4 * flick(c.x));\n      r.queue(c.x, -3, c.z + 5, g2 => { const [x, y] = r.w(c.x, -2, c.z + 5); px.poly(g2, [[x - 6, y], [x + 6, y], [x + 2, y + 4], [x - 2, y + 4]], '#3a3040'); px.rect(g2, x - 6, y, 12, 1, '#9a8aa0'); px.rect(g2, x - 1, y + 4, 2, 4, '#2a2230'); px.dot(g2, x, y + 8, '#c8b060'); });\n    }\n    for (const c of coins) if (!c.got) r.queue(c.x, 0, c.z, g2 => { const [x, y] = r.w(c.x, 0, c.z + 5); coin(g2, x, y + Math.round(Math.sin(t * 3 + c.x) * 1.5), t * 4 + c.x * .1); });\n    for (const d of drops) if (!d.got) r.queue(d.x, 1, d.z, g2 => { const [x, y] = r.w(d.x, 0, d.z); if (d.heart) E.ui.hearts(g2, x - 3, y - 7, 1, 1); else coin(g2, x, y - 6, t * 5); });\n    r.queue(lift.x, 0, lift.z, g2 => {                 // the lift: a floating slab with runes\n      const [x, y] = r.w(lift.x, 0, lift.z + lift.h), w = Math.round(lift.w * view.scale), X = Math.round(x - w / 2), st = E.tones('#6a6280'), H = Math.round(lift.h * view.scale) + 3;\n      blend(g2, 'lighter', .35 + .15 * Math.sin(t * 4), () => { px.ell(g2, x, y + H + 6, w * .42, 5, '#1a5aa0'); px.ell(g2, x, y + H + 5, w * .25, 3, '#3ab8ff'); });\n      px.poly(g2, [[X + 3, y + H], [X + w - 3, y + H], [x + 6, y + H + 8], [x - 6, y + H + 8]], st.deep); px.rect(g2, X, y, w, H, st.base); px.rect(g2, X, y, w, 2, st.lt); px.rect(g2, X, y, w, 1, st.hi);\n      px.rect(g2, X, y + H - 2, w, 2, st.deep); px.rect(g2, X + 2, y + 3, w - 4, 1, st.sh); for (const k of [1, 2]) px.rect(g2, X + Math.round(w * k / 3), y + 4, 1, H - 6, st.deep);\n      blend(g2, 'lighter', .6 + .4 * Math.sin(t * 4), () => { for (const k of [-.33, 0, .33]) { px.rect(g2, x + k * w - 1, y + 5, 3, 1, '#3ab8ff'); px.dot(g2, x + k * w, y + 6, '#bfefff'); } });\n    });\n    lit(r, lift.x, lift.z - 6, 34, '#3ab8ff', .3);\n    const [gx, gy] = r.w(gate.x, -8, gate.z); g.drawImage(arch(60, 90), Math.round(gx) - 30, Math.round(gy) - 90);   // the gate: a double door in a carved arch (it swings open on clear)\n    r.prop('door', gate.x, -6, gate.z, { size: 1.55, color: '#5a2e22', open: clear });\n    if (clear) lit(r, gate.x, gate.z + 30, 80, '#ffe0a0', .6);\n    for (const f of foes) { if (f.dead && !(f.corpse > 0)) continue;\n      const o = { flash: f.flash > .24 || (f.dead && f.corpse < .4 && (game.real * 20 | 0) % 2 === 0), rimColor: Z.rim }; shadow(f.x, f.z);   // a hit: a white tint for 3-4 frames\n      if (f.kind === 'bat') { r.actor(f.x, 0, f.z, (g2, ox, oy) => bat(g2, ox, oy, f), o); lit(r, f.x, f.z + 10, 12, '#ff5030', f.swoop && f.swoop.u < 0 ? .9 : .4); }   // the eyes flare before a swoop\n      else { r.actor(f.x, 0, f.z, (g2, ox, oy) => f.rig.draw(g2, ox, oy, view), Object.assign(o, { bias: f.atk && f.atk.busy ? 1 : 0 })); f.rig.drawSmear(r, ['#fff4e0', '#f0d8b0', '#d0986a', '#8a4a3a']);   // striking: over the hero, a rusty trail\n        if (!f.dead && !f.sleep) { const h = f.rig.head(); lit(r, h[0] + f.dir * 2, h[2], 8, '#ff4020', .3); if (f.atk && f.atk.phase === 'wind') { const w = f.rig.hand(); lit(r, w[0], w[2], 20, '#ff6040', .4); } } }   // eye glow; the raised blade glints\n    }\n    {   // the hero: a red flicker when hurt, then a blink; dashing and spinning leave blue ghosts\n      const blink = inv > 0 && !hero.ko && Math.floor(game.real * 16) % 2 === 0, red = hero.stun > 0 && (game.real * 20 | 0) % 2 === 0, w = rig.tip(); shadow(hero.x, hero.z);\n      r.actor(hero.x, 0, hero.z, (g2, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g2, ox, oy, view),\n        { alpha: blink && hero.stun <= 0 ? .45 : 1, flash: red && '#ff3050', flashMix: .4, rimColor: Z.rim, ghost: (hero.dashing > 0 || (atk === MV.spin && atk.active)) && (game.real * 60 | 0) % 3 === 0 ? { color: '#5a8aff', life: .25 } : null });\n      lit(r, hero.x, hero.z + 26, 46, '#ffe8d0', .25); rig.drawSmear(r, ['#ffffff', '#e0f0ff', '#8ab8ff', '#5a6ad8']); if ((atk && atk.active) || charge >= CHARGE) lit(r, w[0], w[2], 30, '#a0d8ff', charge ? .5 * flick(w[0], 3) : .35);   // the blade lights up\n      if (charge >= CHARGE) r.queue(w[0], 9, w[2], g2 => { const [x, y] = r.w(w[0], 0, w[2]), k = 3 + 2 * Math.sin(game.real * 30);   // charged: a star glints on the tip\n        blend(g2, 'lighter', 1, () => { px.line(g2, x - k, y, x + k, y, '#bfe8ff'); px.line(g2, x, y - k, x, y + k, '#bfe8ff'); px.dot(g2, x, y, '#ffffff'); }); });\n    }\n    drawFx(r);\n    r.queue(0, -1.5, 0, g2 => shade(g2, r, Z, Z.dark));   // full darkness: after the walls and wall props, before the characters\n    r.overlay(g2 => { shade(g2, r, Z, Z.dark * .3); glow(g2); mist(r, g2, Z, .3, 1.3, 8);   // a third of it over everything, the colored glows, the near mist\n      for (const [x, y, L] of shafts) blend(g2, 'lighter', .09, () => { const k = L / 126, b = y - 30 + L; px.poly(g2, [[x - 8, y - 30], [x + 8, y - 30], [x + 70 * k, b], [x + 40 * k, b]], '#8098ff'); px.poly(g2, [[x - 3, y - 30], [x + 3, y - 30], [x + 58 * k, b], [x + 50 * k, b]], '#c0c8ff'); });   // shafts end on the floor\n      const fo = ((r.ix * 1.3) % 480 + 480) % 480, fy = soil ? Math.round(b.y1 - 4 - r.iy) : r.bh; if (Z.bg[1] < .99) blend(g2, 'source-over', 1 - Z.bg[1], () => { for (let x = -fo; x < r.bw; x += 480) g2.drawImage(fore(), x, fy - 36); });\n      hud(g2, r); });\n    talk.draw(r);\n  }\n  // 5. LIGHT, part 2: a darkness layer in the zone color with every light punched out, over the walls fully and over everything\n  // at a third, so sprites stay brighter than rooms (as in SotN).\n  function shade(g, r, Z, a) {\n    const W = r.bw, H = r.bh; if (!darkCv || darkCv.width !== W || darkCv.height !== H) darkCv = E.mkCanvas(W, H);\n    if (darkCv.frame !== game.real) {                  // built once per frame, used twice\n      const d = darkCv.getContext('2d'); darkCv.frame = game.real; d.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over'; d.globalAlpha = 1; d.fillStyle = Z.amb; d.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);\n      d.globalCompositeOperation = 'destination-out'; for (const L of lights) { d.globalAlpha = Math.min(1, L.a * 2); d.drawImage(halo(L.R, '#ffffff'), L.x - L.R, L.y - L.R); }\n    }\n    blend(g, 'source-over', a, () => g.drawImage(darkCv, 0, 0));\n  }\n  const glow = g => { for (const L of lights) { const R = Math.max(6, Math.round(L.R * .55 / 6) * 6); blend(g, 'lighter', L.a * .5, () => g.drawImage(halo(R, L.c), L.x - R, L.y - R)); } };\n  // HUD in the SotN manner: a gold HP medallion, an ornate HP bar, lives and gold\n  function hud(g, r) {\n    const gt = E.tones('#d8a848'), w = Math.round(84 * clamp(hp / MAXHP, 0, 1));\n    px.rect(g, 30, 8, 96, 11, '#0c0610'); px.rect(g, 31, 9, 94, 9, gt.sh); px.rect(g, 32, 10, 92, 7, gt.lt); px.rect(g, 33, 11, 90, 5, '#1a0610');\n    px.rect(g, 37, 11, w, 5, '#c8243a'); px.rect(g, 37, 11, w, 1, '#ff8a94'); px.rect(g, 37, 15, w, 1, '#6a0818'); px.poly(g, [[124, 7], [132, 13], [124, 20]], gt.sh); px.poly(g, [[124, 9], [130, 13], [124, 17]], gt.lt);\n    px.disc(g, 19, 19, 18, '#0c0610'); px.disc(g, 19, 19, 17, gt.sh); px.disc(g, 18, 18, 16, gt.lt); px.disc(g, 19, 19, 14, '#2a0a1c'); px.disc(g, 19, 19, 13, '#4a1430'); px.dot(g, 11, 8, gt.hi);\n    E.font.text(g, String(Math.max(0, hp)), 20, 12, '#ffffff', { align: 'center', scale: 2, gradient: ['#ffffff', '#ffb0b8'], shadow: '#10040a', outline: false });\n    E.font.text(g, '♥', 40, 23, '#ff5a6a', S); E.font.text(g, '×' + lives, 48, 23, '#f0e8ff', S); coin(g, 74, 27, 0); E.font.text(g, String(gold), 81, 23, '#ffe066', S);\n    const ribbon = (y, h, a) => blend(g, 'source-over', a, () => { px.rect(g, 0, y, r.W, h, '#05030a'); px.rect(g, 0, y, r.W, 1, '#d8a848'); px.rect(g, 0, y + h - 1, r.W, 1, '#d8a848'); });\n    if (age < 1.8) { const a = clamp((1.8 - age) * 2, 0, 1); ribbon(58, 26, .5 * a); if (a > .3) E.font.title(g, 'Castle Approach', r.W / 2, 64, { scale: 2, align: 'center', colors: ['#ffffff', '#d8c8f0', '#9a7ad0'], depth: 1, depthColor: '#1c1430' }); }\n    else if (hint > 0 && !talk.open) {   // controls after the dialog, in the player's own keys; over the soil when there is some\n      const k = a => game.input.label(a), y = soil ? r.H - soil + 6 : r.H - 17, s = hint > 4 ? [prompt('jump', 'jump'), prompt('attack', 'slash'), prompt('dash', 'dash'), touch() ? '' : 'V 2.5D'].filter(Boolean).join('  •  ')\n        : ['↑' + k('attack') + ' rise', '↓' + k('attack') + ' dive', k('dash') + '+' + k('attack') + ' thrust', 'hold ' + k('attack') + ' spin'].join('  •  '), fit = E.font.width(s) < r.W - 8;\n      ribbon(y, 13, .55 * clamp(hint, 0, 1)); E.font.text(g, s, r.W / 2, y + (fit ? 3 : 4), '#e8e0ff', fit ? SC : Object.assign({ font: 'tiny' }, SC));\n    }\n    if (clear) { ribbon(40, 30, .6); E.font.title(g, 'STAGE CLEAR', r.W / 2, 46, { scale: 2, align: 'center', colors: ['#fffbe8', '#ffd36a', '#e0662a'] }); }   // a translucent ribbon keeps the lit gate visible\n  }\n  return { view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'ps1', pausable: true, touch: ['jump', 'attack', 'dash'], propSize: 1.5, camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], tall: 1.5 },   // props at the hero's scale; the player zooms .75x to 2x (no turning), a phone held upright starts at 1.5x: the level fills more of it\n    enter() { reset(true); age = 0; hint = 8; talk.close(); game.cam.bounds = v => { const b = level.bounds(v); return Object.assign({}, b, { y1: b.y1 + soilFor(b, game.H) }); }; A.music('adventure'); L.enabled = false; if (game.r) { rimWas = game.r.rimAlpha; game.r.rimAlpha = .5; }   // a stronger, zone-colored rim light (headless, no renderer)\n      P.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z + 1); },              // sparks and debris land and bounce on the platform under them\n    exit() { if (game.r) game.r.rimAlpha = rimWas; P.ground = null; }, update, draw,\n    /** the proof: a run to the right through the graveyard, jumping, cutting and dashing; a hash of the run (with a new rig:\n     *  the hero keeps his between visits, and his pose decides where the blade lands) */\n    run: (n = 600) => proof('platformer', n, (i, k) => { if (!i) k.hold('right'); if (i % 70 === 10) k.tap('jump'); if (i % 45 === 30) k.tap('attack'); if (i % 240 === 200) k.tap('dash'); if (i % 150 === 100) k.tap('confirm'); },\n      () => [hero.x, hero.z, hp, lives, score, gold, clear, foes.map(f => [f.x, f.z, f.hp])], () => { rig = null; }) };\n})();\nscenes.platformer = PLAT;\n\n/* ---- START: the title menu, or a slice named in the address (#platformer) ---- */\n// a genre kit file holds one slice: the menu lists only the slices in this file\nfor (let i = SLICES.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) if (!scenes[SLICES[i].id]) SLICES.splice(i, 1);\ntitleMenu = menuOf(SLICES, { y: 76, onPick: i => game.go(SLICES[i].id) });\n// #<slice>/<part> jumps in (#arcade/maze, #shooter/horizontal): the slice's enter(data) gets { part }; a part that is a\n// scene of its own (the slice's parts: { battle: 'rpg-battle' }) starts there\nconst [first, part] = (location.hash || '').slice(1).split('/'), sc = scenes[first], to = sc && part && sc.parts && sc.parts[part];\ngame.start({ scenes, scene: to || (sc ? first : 'title'), data: sc && part ? { part } : undefined });\nwindow.__game = { game, scenes, titleMenu };   // for tests\n})();\n/* =============================== GAME END =============================== */\n63:- 572 Standard features; settings, fullscreen\n69:- 648 Humanoid rig; E.Attack: slash, E.Combo: combo, E.Humanoid: rig\n75:- 861 Platform map; E.PlatformMap: level\n76:- 895 Platformer; E.Platformer: hero\n78:- 956 Actions; E.Action: action\n88:- 1184 21d. Shell; E.Shell: shell\n121:- `E.HEIGHTS: eye, chest, level, step`: the numbers of THE HEIGHT RULES, which collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing all read (API.md, \"Heights\", and the API index's head give the  [... omitted end of long line]\n173:- `E.overlapBox(a, b)`: side-view boxes: { x (center), z (feet), w, h } (the Platformer / PlatformMap convention)\n262:- `game.screen.filter = 'clean'` †: 'clean' | 'scanlines' | 'crt', drawn over the picture as it is presented, never on the buffer, and not under a test harness (navigator.webdriver). game.settings.fil [... omitted end of long line]\n267:- `game.screen.scale = 'auto'`: how a fixed resolution fills the screen: 'sharp' (whole-number scale, every pixel the same size, black bars unless o.expand grows the picture into them), 'fill' (the la [... omitted end of long line]\n279:- `input.bindings` †: the player's bindings over the preset: { action: { keys: [codes], pad: [codes] } } (game.settings.bindings keeps them)\n304:- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the ga [... omitted end of long line]\n312:- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\n318:- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size the [... omitted end of long line]\n321:- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. E [... omitted end of long line]\n330:- `input.invertMoveX`, `input.invertMoveY = false` †: the move stick turned round (the Controller settings)\n340:- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\n345:- `input.rumbleScale = .6` †: vibration strength 0..1 (0 = off; game.settings.rumble sets it)\n384:- `game.lights.screen = false` †: true measures lights on the screen, not the ground (side views and PlatformMaps do it by themselves)\n466:- `new E.Game({ menu })`: the pause, settings and controls screens ({ items, settings, controls, quit } adjusts them, false leaves them out)\n469:- `new E.Game({ autoPause })`: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden or loses focus\n470:- `new E.Game({ live })`: true: a world that never stops for one player (online, a shared screen): a pause opens the menus over it, the scene's update goes on (game.paused says a menu is up) and the m [... omitted end of long line]\n471:- `new E.Game({ id })`: the name the player's settings are saved under (the page's path)\n480:- `game.back()`: a step back, as a phone's back gesture asks for (the engine calls it for one; a back button of the game's own can): the open menu screen goes back one; else the scene's own back() tak [... omitted end of long line]\n486:- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by [... omitted end of long line]\n492:- `game.hook(kind, fn)` †: a function of the game's own on the engine's clock: kind 'input' fn(dt) every step, paused or not, after the input is read (a host's guard, an idle timer); 'step' fn(dt) eve [... omitted end of long line]\n493:- `game.menu(name, o)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, wa [... omitted end of long line]\n498:- `game.pause(reason = 'player')`: pause the game, saying why ('player' | 'focus' | 'controller' | 'menu': pauseReason); returns true if it paused. The player resumes (Escape, Start, Resume); resume() [... omitted end of long line]\n501:- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom) [... omitted end of long line]\n502:- `game.resume()`: take the game out of pause (and close the pause screen)\n508:- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n514:- `game.start({ camera })`: a scene's { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5, wheel: true }: the player zooms the camera within zoom (the wheel, - and =, a pinch, Settings, Camera zoom: theirs, kept, o [... omitted end of long line]\n520:- `game.zoomBy(k)` †: the player zooms the camera by a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther), as the wheel, - and = and a pinch do (a zoom button of the game's own can): within the scene's camera.zoom [... omitted end of long line]\n530:- `game.drawEvery = 1`: 1, or 2 while a fast display (a 120 Hz phone) refreshes quicker than the game can draw (a frame's cost, or refreshes missed: its GPU behind): every other refresh is drawn, even [... omitted end of long line]\n541:- `game.live` †: true (o.live): a pause opens the menus over a world that keeps stepping (an online or shared world can't stop for one player): paused is true and the scene's update goes on, the menu  [... omitted end of long line]\n544:- `game.pauseOverlay = true`: false hides the engine's PAUSED overlay (a game drawing its own pause screen)\n545:- `game.pauseReason = null`: why the game paused: 'player' | 'focus' (the page was hidden) | 'controller' (unplugged) | 'menu', or null\n546:- `game.paused = false`: true while paused (a pausable scene toggles it on 'pause'; steps still run, scenes don't update)\n548:- `game.real = 0`: seconds the game has stepped through, pauses and hit-stop included (blinking HUD, notes)\n554:- `game.settings`: the player's choices (game.settings.volume, game.settings.set('volume', .5), your own from menu.settings), saved and applied (section 10b)\n555:- `game.shell = null` †: the E.Shell over this game (an HTML interface: screens and HUD), or null\n563:- `game.time = 0`: game seconds since start (it stops during pause and hit-stop, and slows with timeScale)\n568:- `game.zoom = 1` †: the camera's zoom now (1 = as the view is): the game's (setZoom, a scene's camera.start) times the player's (settings.zoom: the wheel, a pinch), within the scene's camera.zoom ran [... omitted end of long line]\n574:- `settings.default(key)`: a setting's default value\n575:- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its  [... omitted end of long line]\n576:- `settings.keys(group)`: the keys of a group's settings, in order ('Display', 'Sound', 'Comfort', 'Controller', 'Touch', 'Game'), or every key\n577:- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n578:- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value [... omitted end of long line]\n579:- `settings.spec(key)`: the spec a setting was defined with (its label, type, range, options), or undefined\n580:- `settings.saved`: false when the last save failed (storage blocked or full: the choices last for this visit)\n586:- `fullscreen.toggle()`: full screen on or off, as the player asked (the corner's button, the menu): their choice is kept in settings.fullscreen, so a game starting on a phone goes full screen by itse [... omitted end of long line]\n637:- `saves.show(mode = 'load', done)` †: open the standard save screen over the game (E.Shell's): 'save' (to a slot) or 'load'; done(save) after a load or a save. False without a menu (a headless game,  [... omitted end of long line]\n639:- `saves.canSave = true` †: the pause menu offers Save (a JRPG turns it off away from save points)\n642:- `saves.played = 0` †: seconds played in the game's scenes (paused time and titles left out), saved with each save and set by a load\n648:## 11. Humanoid rig\n656:- `slash.state`: what Humanoid.update wants as `attack` (null when idle)\n670:- `combo.state`: what Humanoid.update wants as `attack` (null when idle)\n676:- `new E.Humanoid(o = {}) → rig`: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build, weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, hood, cape, colors, ... }): a character rig posed from math every step (API.md, \"Charact [... omitted end of long line]\n677:- `E.Humanoid.hat(id, draw)` †: a hat of the game's own: draw(h) with h.at(forward, right, up) a point round the head on screen, h.r its radius there, h.c its color (o.hat.color), h.dk a darker one, h [... omitted end of long line]\n678:- `E.Humanoid.hats` †: the hats a Humanoid may wear, by id: the built-ins (cap, band, crown, helmet, pointed) and a game's own\n679:- `E.Humanoid.pose(id, fn)` †: a held pose of the game's own: fn(J, w, rig) moves the joints (the rig's frame: forward, right, up) toward the pose by w, 0..1, which eases in and out as state.pose name [... omitted end of long line]\n680:- `E.Humanoid.poses` †: the held poses of a game's own, by id (the built-in poses are the rig's own solve: state.pose names either)\n681:- `E.Humanoid.weapon(id, spec)` †: a weapon of the game's own (or a built-in replaced): { length: the tip's distance from the hand (a number, or rig => one; tip() and the swing's trail), rest: [forwar [... omitted end of long line]\n682:- `E.Humanoid.weapons` †: the weapons a Humanoid may hold, by id: the built-ins (sword, gun, staff) and a game's own\n693:- `rig.update(dt, s = {})`: s: { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, attack, air, point } air: true while jumping or falling (legs tuck), point: true to hold the weapon hand forward (aiming a gun) at [... omitted end of long line]\n711:- `rig.poseW`: held poses: 'cheer' (item get, victory), 'cast' (both hands forward), 'guard' (fists up), 'kneel', 'crouch', 'wave', 'hips' (hands on hips), and down: 0..1 (knocked flat). stance: 'guar [... omitted end of long line]\n863:- `new E.PlatformMap(o = {}) → level`: { rows: [...ASCII, top row first...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types: { 1: {...} }, tile: 16, depth: 16 }. Tile kinds: 'solid' (default) | 'oneway' | 'la [... omitted end of long line]\n895:## 16b. Platformer\n897:- `new E.Platformer(o = {}) → hero`: a side-scroller character body with modern, forgiving controls (coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, optional double jump, wall jump, dash, ladders,  [... omitted end of long line]\n900:- `hero.rigState(extra)`: state for Humanoid.update: rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack }))\n956:## 17b. Actions\n958:- `E.ACTIONS: climbUp, climbJump, vault, hopDown, roll, getUp, takedown` †: the actions E.Action plays. Add your own: E.ACTIONS.name = { beats, setup, at }; setup(A) and at(A, beat, u) get the action: [... omitted end of long line]\n959:- `new E.Action(name, o = {}) → action`: new E.Action(name, { body, to, other, dir, dist, top, aerial, reverse, speed: 1 }): play E.ACTIONS[name] on a body (and on o.other for a paired action). It mov [... omitted end of long line]\n987:- `mover.action = null` †: the E.Action playing, or null\n1042:- `shots.update(dt, map)`: move them one step; with a map they stop at walls (a TileMap, or a PlatformMap in side views)\n1070:- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute a [... omitted end of long line]\n1071:- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: thi [... omitted end of long line]\n1073:- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\n1076:- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token pe [... omitted end of long line]\n1093:- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\n1130:- `talk.say(text, o = {})`: open with one string or a list of strings (each starts a new page). opts: { name, onDone, choices, onChoice, portrait: rig (a live close-up of that Humanoid beside the text [... omitted end of long line]\n1149:- `talk.portrait`: the portrait beside the text (a Humanoid, or a function (g, x, y, size)), or null; pSize: its size in pixels; pOpts: its options ({ zoom, turn, bg })\n1155:- `new E.Menu(game, items, o = {}) → menu`: Menu with a cursor (title screens, pause menus, shops, battle commands). const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', 'OPTIONS', { label: 'CONTINUE', disabled: ! [... omitted end of long line]\n1184:## 21d. Shell\n1186:- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HT [... omitted end of long line]\n1187:- `new E.Shell({ theme })`: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font: a family, titleFont), and scale: the whole interface that many times (a number, or a function of the window) f [... omitted end of long line]\n1188:- `new E.Shell({ screens })`: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n1189:- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard [... omitted end of long line]\n1190:- `new E.Shell({ store, meters })`: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n1191:- `new E.Shell({ standard })`: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the settings' groups)\n1192:- `shell.back()` †: what cancel does: the screen's own back(), or close it (back to the screen under it; closing the pause screen resumes)\n1194:- `shell.hide()` †: close every screen (and resume, when the menu paused the game)\n1200:- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n1202:- `shell.screen` †: the name of the open screen ('pause', 'settings', 'controls', ...), or null while the game plays\n1203:- `shell.settings` †: the player's settings: game.settings (the standard ones and the game's own, by key)\n1204:- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (stri [... omitted end of long line]\n1363:- `E.FIGHT_KEYS: up, down, left, right, lp, hp, lk, hk, start, pause, confirm, cancel` †: the fighting preset (a scene's input): the moves and menu keys of E.Input.DEFAULT, light and heavy punch (U, I [... omitted end of long line]\n1387:- `new E.Fighter(game, o = {}) → fighter` †: new E.Fighter(game, { name, x, side: 1 (faces right), rig: Humanoid options (or a Humanoid), life: 1000, walk: 75, back: 60, jump: 390, jumpX: 90, moves, t [... omitted end of long line]\n1399:- `fighter.body` †: its E.Platformer (x, z: its feet; w, h: its push box)\n1418:- `fighter.rig` †: its E.Humanoid (drawn and posed from its state every step)\n1574:- `rhythm.start()` †: play the song from its start (synced: it pauses with the game) and judge from here\n1628:- `world.shellMap()`: the explored map for E.Shell's minimap: game.shell.hud({ minimap: world.shellMap() }) (a cell a square, the save and boss rooms marked, the hero's cell)\n1645:- `rpg.card(g, x, y, m, o = {})`: a member's card: o.portrait (a Humanoid) beside its name, level, HP and MP bars, its stats and effects; → { w, h }\n1718:- `strategy.minimap(colors = {})` †: E.Shell's minimap spec: the map, the fog, the camera's view, the units seen as dots by side, a click moving the camera\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Objects and materials as data\n\nThe full engine draws still objects (barrels, tables, trees, wells) from a few lines of data, in every view and facing,\nwith the house shading and outline, and gives every surface (floors, wall faces, wall tops, platform tiles, the objects'\nown faces) a material from one open registry. An object costs one to a few hundred tokens of data (the core's median is\n125); you check it headless with one command. Read this card whole before you write one. (The agent edition keeps the\nregistries and ignores the calls: `E.addAssets` returns empty lists, the others null.)\n\n## Use one\n\n```js\n// in draw(r): feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor; its front toward facing\nr.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing: Math.PI / 2 });\n// once, before the updates: bodies stop at it, a body coming down lands on it, one wholly below passes under\nmap.solid('table', 120, 80, 0, { facing: 0 });\n// a sprite of it, for an inventory or a HUD (r.sprite, px.sprite)\nconst icon = E.shapeSprite('chest', { view: 'iso' });\n```\n\n- **r.shape options:** facing (radians, where the front points: 0 east, π/2 south, as written), scale, mats ({ slot:\n  material }), flip (mirrors it x to -x, collision too), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (default true),\n  light (adds its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true).\n- **map.solid** takes the same id and placement, so one object can feed both calls. Bodies span z to z + h (E.Body's h, 32).\n- **In 3D** the 3D engine draws the same entries, materials and boxes: `world.shape(id, x, y, z, o)` (its header).\n- **Steep views** (top-down) draw shapes from the characters' lower angle, as rigs are drawn. The light comes from the\n  viewer's upper left in every view.\n- **Glow and fire:** a material with glow: 1 ignores the light and stays bright in the dark; an entry's flames ([[x, y, z,\n  w, h]]) draw the engine's animated flame (r.shape only: a sprite has none). Anything else that moves stays a prop\n  (r.prop) or your own code.\n- **Dressing a generated level:** a scene is a few entries as [id, across, forward] from an anchor (a wall spot, forward\n  into the room); place it whole or not at all, each part on free floor, after the level's own random picks and from a\n  seeded stream of its own, so the level stays as its seed builds it (Emberdeep's `src/emberdeep/53-scenes.js`).\n\n## The core library\n\n**Shapes** (sized for a 26-unit hero; generators take params): barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed,\nbookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, well, sign, lamppost, anvil; fence { len: 16 }, pine { h: 41, tiers: 4 },\ntree { h: 40, seed: 1 }, bush { r: 7 }, grass { h: 6, n: 6 }, rock { r: 7.5, seed: 2 }, crystal { h: 18, seed: 1 }.\n`r.shape('pine', x, y, 0, { params: { h: 30 } })`; `E.SHAPES.barrel.about` says what each is.\n\n**Materials:** woods oak walnut birch crate plank planks wood; stones stone cobble marble granite sandstone slate rock\nbrick flagstone ruin; metals iron steel gold copper bronze metal rusted; clay glazed porcelain; cloth linen burlap wool velvet leather;\nplants bark needles leaves shrub moss straw hedge grass; ground dirt sand snow mud; water ice crystal flame bone shingle;\nwalls timber plaster; tops speckle plain tiles slab; tiles block bonus ground pipe checker.\n\n## Materials on surfaces\n\n```js\nE.MATERIALS.mossy = { color: '#6a7a5a', pattern: 'stones', size: 6 };   // or the shorthand '#6a7a5a stones size=6'\nconst types = { 1: { h: 32, top: '#6a6280', side: '#4a4360', face: 'cobble', roof: 'slate' } };   // TileMap walls\nconst map = new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorMat: 'flagstone', floorMats: { water: 'water' } });   // floors\nconst level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types: { 1: { kind: 'solid', style: 'block', mat: 'mossy' } } });\n```\n\nfloorMats is keyed by the floor tags of the legend ('~': { floor: 'water' }); either stands in for floorTex.\n\nA material is `{ color, pattern, ...the pattern's numbers, ramp, glow, over }`. Its five tones come from `E.tones(color)`\n(or its own `ramp` of five, dark to light); the light picks a band, the pattern moves it a step, so a material works on\nevery surface and in every palette and recolors with one hex. An unknown name warns with the list of names.\nOverlays, the last on top, each in its own color's tones: `over: [['moss', '#5f7a3a', { up: .6 }], ['cracks']]`; moss on\nwhat looks up, cracks (the material a step darker), dirt near the ground (h 6), rust; each takes amount and size.\n\n| Pattern | Numbers | What |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| none | | the light alone |\n| planks | bw 3, len 14, dir | boards with joints; staves on round parts; dir: 'v' turns them |\n| crate | frame 2, bw 3 | a framed, braced side; planks on its lid |\n| bricks | bw 6, bh 3, mortar | running bond |\n| stones | size 5 | irregular stones (Voronoi), dark joints, a lit rim |\n| tiles | size 4 | square tiles, grout |\n| speck, noise | amount .08; scale 4 | specks; soft patches |\n| foliage | size 3.5 | leaf clumps lit on top |\n| bark | | fissures; rings on a cut end |\n| metal | rivet | a shine where lit; rivets |\n| cloth | fold .9 | weave and folds |\n| glaze | | a glossy shine |\n| thatch, shingles | course; bw | straw courses; roof tiles |\n\nYour own pattern: `E.PATTERNS.waves = (s, m) => Math.sin(s.u / (m.size || 3)) > .8 ? 1 : 0` (s.u, s.v: world units\nacross and up the surface; s.lid, s.curved, s.lit, s.W, s.H; return a step, -1 to +2). A material may also paint one kind\nof surface its own way, per pixel: `face(wx, z, tones, type, cx, cy, fi, nh, h)` or `roof(wx, wy, tones, type)` returning\na color or null, `floor(x, y, tag, view)` returning [r, g, b].\n\n## Write one\n\nUnits: 16 = 1 m = one tile; x east, y south, z up. The origin is the middle of the footprint on the ground; write it\nfacing south (+y). Use whole or half units: a pixel at game size is about 0.7 units.\n\n```js\nE.SHAPES.stool = { about: 'round three-legged stool', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { w: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['cyl','w',[0,0,8],5,1.5], ['ring',3,3,['box','l',[0,0,0],[1.5,1.5,8],{rot:[0,-8,0]}]]] };\n```\n\nAn entry: `about`, `tags`, `mats` (slot to material; a slot name that is not in mats is a material itself), and `parts`, or\n`make(params)` returning parts with `params` its defaults. Optional: `scale` (times the placement's), `foot: [x0, y0,\nx1, y1]` (the footprint for collision, and for sorting when it stands raised; the bounds by default), `lights: [[x, y,\nz, color, radius]]`, `flames: [[x, y, z, w, h]]`.\n\n| Part | Form | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| box | `['box', m, at, [w, d, h], { rot }]` | at: the bottom middle |\n| cyl | `['cyl', m, at, r, h, { r2, seg, sq }]` | r2: top radius (0: a cone); sq squashes y; seg: 6 for a hexagon |\n| lathe | `['lathe', m, at, [[r, z], ...], { seg }]` | a profile turned about z, bottom to top; [0, z] closes an end |\n| ball | `['ball', m, at, r, { sz }]` | at: its centre; sz below 1 flattens it |\n| prism | `['prism', m, at, [[a, b], ...], h, { axis }]` | an outline pushed h: up z (outline x, y), or centred along y (outline x, z) or x (outline y, z) |\n| wedge | `['wedge', m, at, [w, d, h]]` | h tall at the back, down to the floor at the front |\n| rock | `['rock', m, at, r, { sz, seed, rough }]` | a lumpy, faceted ball; below the floor is not drawn |\n| use | `['use', id, at, { rot, scale, mats, ...params }]` | another shape inside this one |\n| mirror | `['mirror', 'x' \\| 'y' \\| 'xy', part]` | the part and its mirror copies |\n| ring, row | `['ring', n, radius, part]`, `['row', n, [dx, dy, dz], part]` | n copies round z (the part moved out to radius); n copies in a line, centred |\n| group | `['group', at, rot, [parts]]` | parts moved and turned together |\n\nEvery part takes `{ rot }`: a yaw in degrees, or [x, y, z] degrees. Write arrays densely (`[0,0,8]`, not `[0, 0, 8]`):\nspaces cost a third more tokens.\n\n### Worked entries\n\n```js\nbarrel: { about: 'oak barrel, two iron hoops', tags: 'container wood', mats: { w: 'oak', h: 'iron' },\n  parts: [['lathe','w',[0,0,0],[[0,0],[5,0],[6,3],[6.5,8],[6,13],[5,16],[0,16]]], ['row',2,[0,0,9],['cyl','h',[0,0,7.5],6.5,1.5]]] },\ncrate: { about: 'wooden crate, framed and braced', tags: 'container wood', mats: { c: 'crate' }, parts: [['box','c',[0,0,0],[15,15,14]]] },\ntable: { about: 'plank table, four legs', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { t: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['box','t',[0,0,10],[24,14,2]], ['mirror','xy',['box','l',[10,5,0],[2,2,10]]]] },\nchest: { about: 'oak chest, round lid, iron bands', tags: 'container treasure', mats: { w: 'oak', b: 'iron', l: 'gold' },\n  parts: [['box','w',[0,0,0],[16,10,8]], ['prism','w',[0,0,8],[[-5,0],[5,0],[4,3],[0,5],[-4,3]],16,{axis:'x'}],\n    ['mirror','x',['box','b',[5.5,0,0],[1.5,11,8.5]]], ['box','l',[0,5,5],[3,1,3]]] },\nlamppost: { about: 'iron street lamp, glowing head', tags: 'town light', mats: { m: 'iron', g: 'flame' }, lights: [[0,0,40,'#ffd070',70]],\n  parts: [['cyl','m',[0,0,0],2.5,3], ['cyl','m',[0,0,3],1,34], ['box','g',[0,0,37],[4,4,5]], ['cyl','m',[0,0,42],3.5,3,{r2:0,seg:4}]] },\nfence: { about: 'rail fence, len long (16)', tags: 'village farm', mats: { w: 'oak' }, params: { len: 16 },\n  make: p => [['mirror','x',['box','w',[p.len / 2 - 1,0,0],[2,2,12]]], ['row',2,[0,0,5],['box','w',[0,0,6],[p.len - 2,1,1.5]]]] },\n```\n\nA generator seeds its noise from its params (`E.rng(seed)`), never `Math.random`, so it draws the same pixels everywhere.\n\n## Make one: the recipe\n\n1. **Read this card whole.**\n2. **Find** the nearest entry: `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"tavern table\"` searches the core library and the store's sets\n   (`src/assets/sets/`, about a thousand objects, materials and sprites converted from CC0 libraries, each with where it\n   came from) and `--show set:id` prints one; the Asset Lab (`/asset-lab`) shows them all.\n3. **Copy** it and change its names, parts and materials, or write a new one from the parts above. Give the cue a player\n   knows it by parts of its own (a skep's coils as stacked rings, an anvil's horn, a barrow's open tub dark inside), and\n   keep small parts plain ('none', 'glaze'): a busy pattern on a part a few pixels wide reads as speckle.\n4. **Look:** `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` (a file that sets `E.SHAPES.stool`, or a store set; a core id\n   alone works too: `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs barrel`). It draws `check-output/asset-sheet/stool.png`: every view and\n   four facings, beside the hero, on a light and a dark floor. It lints what reads badly: too small from above, a part\n   under a pixel, too few tones, low contrast with the floor, its tokens. Read the picture, fix, repeat.\n5. **Use** it: `E.SHAPES.stool = {...}` and `r.shape('stool', ...)`; or a set file\n   (`(window.ASSETS = window.ASSETS || {}).MINE = { set: 'MINE', format: 1, materials, shapes }`) and\n   `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS.MINE)`, which names its entries 'MINE:stool' so they never collide, and returns the names.\n   A store set loads the same way (its script, then `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`);\n   `node tools/asset-set.mjs <sets> --ids a,b --name mine --out mine.js` cuts the few a game uses, with what they need\n   (Emberdeep's set dressing loads `src/assets/sets/emberdeep.js`, cut so).\n\n## Limits\n\n- Still objects. Lids that open, swinging signs and held weapons are code (or props) for now.\n- A shape sorts by one point, as props do; a long one (a bed, a cart) may cross a body walking right beside it.\n- Collision is the footprint's box, turned with the shape, and the bounds' height: a table is solid under its top too.\n- Bigger than about 240 pixels on screen, a shape skips the actors' effects (flash, x-ray).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '128,160p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API.md && sed -n '240,312p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API.md && sed -n '1184,1220p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && rg -n 'localPlayer|local.players|player\\\\(|players|Shell|# 10b|# 21d' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '128,160p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API.md && sed -n '240,312p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API.md && sed -n '1184,1220p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && rg -n 'localPlayer|local.players|player\\\\(|players|Shell|# 10b|# 21d' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md\"","aggregated_output":"  `E.all([...])` waits for all; a bare `yield` waits one step. It runs at once to its first `yield`, the same at any refresh rate; `{ keep: true }` outlives the scene; `.cancel()`, `.done`, `.value`.\n- Paths: `const p = new E.Path([[x, y, z], ...], { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })`: `p.length`, `p.at(d)` (`{ x, y, z, angle }` by distance along: moving platforms, dives, rails), `p.nearest(x, y)` (a racer's progress), `p.draw(r)` to see it.\n- Weather: `game.weather = { kind: 'rain' | 'snow' | 'ash' | 'leaves' | 'sand', amount: .8, wind: .3, lightning: .2 }` (null: clear) falls through the world in every view, settles or splashes, flashes and thunders; cosmetic only (its own random stream), and it ends with the scene: set it in `enter`.\n- Wrap-around (Asteroids): `x = E.wrap(x, 0, W)`, `E.wrapDiff(a, b, W)` (the short way across the seam), and `wrap: [W, H]` on `r.sprite` or `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge.\n\n## Standard features (every game has them: don't build your own)\nWith no code: a **pause menu** on pausable scenes (Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Quit to title; keys, mouse, gamepad and touch), **settings** saved per game (`game.settings`: volume, music, effects, mute, reduce motion, shake and flashes, pixel scaling, a screen filter, the frame rate and its readout), **controls** the player rebinds (conflicts said first; dead zone, invert aim and move, button symbols, vibration; touch button side, size, opacity), **full screen** (a phone's first tap, a top-right button, the menu; turning it off is remembered), a **menu button** at the top right (on a touch screen it stays in play: it is the touch START), a **way out** of every menu screen, the **back gesture** stepping back in the game (`game.back()`) before it leaves the page, **pausing** when the page is hidden or the controller unplugs, and a page set up for phones (safe areas, no scrolling, zooming or pull to refresh on the canvas).\n- Adjust: `new E.Game({ menu: { items: [['Restart', fn]], settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true }, { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal' }], controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: false }, quit: 'title' } })`, then read `game.settings.hints`. Leave parts out: `menu: false` (your own pause), `fullscreen: 'button' | false`, `corner: false`, `backGesture: false`, `autoPause: false`, `touch: 'never'` (a phone is told a controller or keyboard is needed). `game.cornerArea()`: where the corner's buttons cover the screen (or null): keep a HUD clear of it.\n- Save slots (`game.saves`): three slots and an autosave, each with its place, level and time played. `game.saves.write = () => ({ data, place, level })` and `read = save => {...}`; then `save(slot)` (0, 1, 2 or `'auto'`), `load(slot)`, `copy(a, b)`, `erase(slot)`, `autosave()`, `list()`, `latest()` (a title's Continue). `menu: { saves: true }` (or `{ slots, version, migrate(data, from) }`) puts Save (while `saves.canSave`: a save point) and Load in the pause menu, and `saves.show('save' | 'load')` opens the screen from your own menu. Blocked storage keeps them for the visit.\n- Prompts: `game.input.label('jump')` is that action's key or button on the player's device, after rebinding ('Space', 'A', '✕'). PlayStation symbols by default: `settings: [{ key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation' }]` (a standard key without a label sets its default). A canvas title's OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')`. From code: `game.settings.set(key, v)`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`. `live: true` (or `game.menu(name, { pause: false })`): menus over a world that keeps stepping (online play).\n- **Boot**: heavy setup in steps behind a boot screen, and a report for any error before play: `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { title, steps: [['Building the city', () => { map = build(); }]] } })` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Boot).\n\n## Input: `game.input`\n- Actions in every preset: `up down left right` (WASD, arrows, d-pad), `start` (Enter, Start), `pause` (Escape, P, Start), `confirm` (Enter, Space, J, Z, pad A), `cancel` (Escape, Backspace, X, K, pad B).\n- DEFAULT adds `attack` (J, X, click), `dash` (Space, Shift, K), `skill` (L, E, right click), `jump` (Z). PLATFORMER: `jump` (Space, Z, K, pad A), `attack` (X, J, click), `dash` (Shift, C, L), `skill`. SHMUP: `fire` (Space, Z, J, click), `bomb` (X, K). THIRDPERSON: `sprint` (Shift), `jump` (Space), `crouch` (C, Ctrl), `interact` (E, F), `attack` (J), `block` (K), `dodge` (L), `use` (Q), `map` (M, Tab).\n- `down(a)`, `pressed(a)`, `released(a)`, `repeat(a, delay, rate)` (menus), `buffered(a, win)` + `consume(a)`, `consumeAll()`, `anyPressed()`, `move()` (screen direction `[x, y]`, length up to 1), `use(preset)`, `touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'])`, `touchSet('confirm', 'TALK', near)` (a context button: relabel it, or hide it while it has nothing to do).\n- Moves: `input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing })` is true on the step a quarter circle forward and punch ends (numpad notation, mirrored when `facing` is -1: `'623'`, `'41236'`); `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down+right', 'attack'], { window: .5 })` any run of inputs (a cheat code); `input.history` the last second of them (`{ t, dir, pressed }`, for an input display).\n- Players (two to four on one machine): `game.players` is `[game.input, ...]`, each an input with this API, its `player` number, `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`) and device. `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => {} })` lets them in: Start on a free gamepad, or 2 on the keyboard (player 2 takes its right half: arrows, `.` `/` `,` and the numpad; player 1 keeps WASD with F G H; a preset's `halves: { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: {...} }` its own); `game.addPlayer({ pad: 0 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })`, `game.removePlayer(p)`. Prompts, the pause (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller and the Controls screen are per player. One screen: `cam.frame(heroes)`. Split: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [h1, h2], layout: 'auto' })` (`'columns'`, `'rows'`, `'grid'`; up to 4) draws the scene once per viewport (`r.viewport` is `{ i, x, y, w, h }`), then `scene.hud(r)` once over the whole screen; `game.viewports[i].follow(t)` or `.focus(x, y)`.\n- `clear()` releases held inputs, pending presses, virtual sticks and touch buttons; focus loss does this automatically. `suppress(a)` blocks an action for the current tick; `suppressCode('Mouse0')` lets a HUD widget consume that mouse button while other devices bound to the action still work.\n- Controller settings: `deadzone` (default `.18`, radial and rescaled), `invertAimY`, `lastDevice` (the index has the rest). The left stick and the touch stick, pushed past halfway, press `up` / `down` / `left` / `right` as the d-pad does (menus, dialog choices, ladders); bind `PadAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` to your own menu actions for the same; the right stick is `PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right`, the wheel `WheelUp`/`WheelDown`; `value(a)`: 0 to 1 (a trigger's travel, a stick's push). Options while Create is held is no press (the AI Hub's chord).\n- Touch settings: `touchEnabled`, `stickSide` (`'left'` or `'right'`), `stickRadius` (CSS pixels), `touchFilter(event)` (return false to reserve a canvas tap for UI). `touchArea()`: where the touch buttons cover the screen (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, or null): keep a prompt clear of it. `clicked()`: where a click or tap on the canvas landed (`[x, y]` in screen pixels) the step after it, else null; `pointer()`: the mouse in screen pixels; `drag()`: how far the stick's finger moved this step (`[dx, dy]`, or null), to steer one to one; `steer = 'drag'`: a finger anywhere steers, no stick drawn (`stickOn`: a finger is on it). `zones = [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }]` (fractions of the canvas): a finger in one holds its act until it lifts (a pinball's halves). Gestures (a mouse or one finger, screen pixels): `box()` → `{ x0, y0, x1, y1, done }` (a left drag; on touch a long press, then a drag), `longPress()`, `doubleTap()`, `pan()` → `[dx, dy]` (a finger's drag on open ground, a middle or right drag), `zoomPoint()`; `feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch, button, id })` plays a pointer in a test. `bindButtons(root)` wires `[data-act]` elements.\n\n## Drawing: `r` in `draw(r)`\n- Background: `r.sky(['#top', '#mid', '#bottom'])`, `r.starfield({ count, speed, dir: 'down' | 'left' })`.\n- Depth sorted: `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view), { flash: '#ffffff', flashMix: .75, alpha, xray, outline, ghost, emissive, every, key })` (the flash tints over the sprite so shading shows: `flashMix` .5 by default, 1 solid; `every: 2, key: npc` redraws a background character every other frame; actors up to about 250 px tall), `r.sprite(x, y, z, sprite, { flip, anchor: 'bottom' | 'center', glow, flash })`, `r.queue(x, y, z, g => {...}, { bias })` (sorted by that one point: a long wall by `r.queueSpan(a, b, z, h, fn)`), `r.actorNow(...)` (as `r.actor`, drawn at once over what is drawn so far), `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)` (inside a queue function). `{ pick: unit }` on any of them (and `r.shape`), then `r.pick(sx, sy)` → what the last frame drew under a screen point, front to back.\n- Floor marks: `r.shadow(x, y, radius, alpha, color, surfaceZ)`, `r.decal(g => r.groundArc(...))`, `r.groundDisc`, `r.groundRing`, `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n, z)`.\n- On top, unlit, screen pixels: `r.text(str, x, y, color, { align, scale, outline })`, `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color)`, `r.overlay(g => {...})`.\n- `r.w(x, y, z)` = buffer pixel of a world point. `r.W`, `r.H`, `r.ix`, `r.iy` (pass to dither functions), `r.view`, `r.visible(x, y, z)`.\n- X-ray: `r.actor(..., { xray: true })` shows the actor's hidden part, a translucent tint with a 1-px outline (a checker in `E.style.trans = 'dither'`), wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high (22; `hides: true | false` says otherwise) or anything queued with `{ hides: true }` is drawn in front of it; not by one rising less than 8 above its feet (the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall). `xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors: true }`: with `actors` other bodies in front hide it too (a hero stays findable in a crowd). Afterimages: `ghost: { color, life, alpha, owner }`; `owner` (the actor's `{x, y, z}`) keeps a trail left nearer the camera sorted behind the live body.\n- Glowing things you draw yourself: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { emissive: true })` is drawn again after lighting so darkness never dims it (flames, lasers, magic, neon). It still hides behind whatever stands in front of it: walls and `r.actor` bodies do this on their own, and any other queued draw can join them with `{ solid: true }`. (Canvas lighting builds a depth-ordered mask for this; `game.r.glowMask = false` skips it on slow machines.)\n\n## Pixels, sprites, text, UI\n- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`, `dot`, `line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, width)`, `disc(g, x, y, r, c)`, `ell`, `poly(g, pts, c)`, `polyDither(g, pts, c, 0..1, r.ix, r.iy)`, `ddisc`, `sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip)` (or `{ flip, rot, scale }`), `glow(g, 0..1)` (GPU glow for what follows).\n| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n| Real-time strategy (Warcraft II, StarCraft, Dune II) | `iso` | `E.Strategy` (the strategy module): kinds as data, selection by click, box and group, orders, gold, building, training, fog, an AI's build order and waves; `cam.free`, `{ pick: unit }` and `r.pick`, buildings as `E.SHAPES`, E.Shell's minimap; bodies drawn `every: 3` | RTS |\n| Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity) | `iso` | paths as floor tags walked on an `E.Grid`, agents with `E.needs` picking by `E.choose`, `E.Clock` days and light, `game.timeScale` 0 to 4, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`; rides as shapes stepped through angles (`params`, `facing`) | SIM |\n| 3D platformer or adventure remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash) | `iso` / `threequarter` / `brawler` | TileMap blocks of different heights + Body `jump()` (z is real height); in real 3D: `E.World3D` (the 3D engine's manual), `camera.lock` | ADVENTURE; 3D ACTION, 3D PLATFORMER |\n| Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark) | the 3D engine's `'zones'` camera | `camera.zones` (fixed cameras by room), `camera.steer` (tank controls), `camera.lock` to aim, `world.through` doors, `world.flashlight`, fog; `E.RPG` inventory, `game.saves` | 3D HORROR |\n| First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) | the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes | cells of 2 x 2 tiles stepped and turned a quarter at a time (tweened), `E.Grid` paths for the monsters, `E.Turns` `'atb'` (real time) or `'speed'` (turns), `E.Fog` for the automap, `world.thing('door')` gates and `world.unlock`, `rig.drawPortrait` for the party | 3D CRAWLER |\n| Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania, Space Channel 5) | any (a highway on the overlay) | `E.Rhythm` (the rhythm module): a chart of lane strings in parts on a song's steps, judged in ms of the music clock, combo, life, grade, holds, calls a teacher plays and you answer, `rh.draw` (`arrows: true`); the songbook's songs (`E.Songbook`); lanes as `input.zones` on a phone; `audio.onBeat` moves the dancer | RHYTHM |\n| Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam) | `threequarter` / `topdown` | the ball an `E.Body` (`bounce`; curl: turn its velocity across its path), passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` for a lob, a shot's meter, formations by where the ball is, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` | SPORTS |\n| Third-person stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu) | `iso` / `threequarter` | TileMap roofs, Mover, `E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, FlowField `jump` | STEALTH |\n| Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero) | Mode 7 (the mode7 module, over any view) | `E.Mode7` over a TileMap track (floor tags: grass, sand, boost), `E.Car` karts (`kart: true`: hop, drift, boost) in an `E.Race` round an `E.Path` (laps, checkpoints, drivers, items), rigs in karts as billboards, `game.splitScreen(2)` for two | KART |\n| 3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.track` (banked, walls, pads, `gaps` for a shortcut), `world.vehicle` karts raced by `E.Race`, split screen through `world.cameras` | 3D RACING |\n| Rail shooters (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.flyer` on rails or free in bounds, rolls (`f.rolling`) turn shots aside, `f.seek` for a charged lock, `Dialog`'s radio | 3D FLIGHT |\n| Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Road Rash) | the pseudo-3D road (the road module) | `E.Road` segments (curves, hills, tunnels), scenery and traffic placed on it, `curveAt` throwing the car outward, a clock and checkpoints | ROAD |\n| Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA) | `topdown` / `iso` | `E.Car` on a TileMap (its floor tags slow it, its walls bounce it), `E.Race` for laps and drivers, cars drawn with `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points | KART |\n| First-person shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007, Apex Legends) | the 3D engine's first person (or third) | a level of text rows or a terrain, `world.ray` hitscan, recoil and reloads, doors (`world.thing('door')`), raiders on `world.flow()` | 3D SHOOTER |\n| Bomberman, Frogger, Sokoban, Gauntlet, Robotron; Sonic, Prince of Persia; light guns | as the recipe says | `AI_GUIDE.md`'s Genre recipes: each on the kit closest to it | (a recipe) |\n\n**Sizes:** heroes 40-60 px tall at 320x240 (`size: 1.2-1.4`), bosses 1.8-2.4. Small things (coins, bullets, ships, pickups) are string sprites designed at 8-16 px, drawn at scale 2 if they are the player's ship. Keep hitboxes smaller than the art.\n\n**Feel numbers** (world units per second, tile = 16): walk 70-90, run 95-140, jump 250-320 with gravity 800, fall cap 330, dash 200-260, bullets 250-320 (player) and 70-120 (enemy), enemy walk 25-45. Hit feedback: `game.hitFx` (Effects).\n\n## More: animation and examples outside this card\nThe repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; any file at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`) holds more to take from. Its files are large: search them and take only what the game needs. Real 3D (`E.World3D`: first person, racing, flight) is there: `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`.\n- **Motion clips**: 325 curated and 2,548 motion-capture takes that play on any Humanoid: find one in `src/mocap/catalogs/`, cut a small set with `tools/anim-set.mjs`, then `Mocap.drive(rig, lib)` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Where to find more; `docs/MOCAP.md`).\n- **Objects and materials** (full engine): `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a still object from any view and facing, `map.solid(...)` makes it solid; `E.MATERIALS` dresses floors (`floorMat`), walls (`face`, `roof`) and platform tiles (`mat`). `docs/ASSETS.md` is the card: the format, the library, writing one and checking it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`.\n- **Worked examples**: Emberdeep (`src/emberdeep/`), a whole game on this engine: 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies, 3 heroes and 30 skills, registered with `def(kind, id, spec)` (one search lists them); adapt them, they lean on the game's own helpers. `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body.\n- **Modules** (`engine/modules/<name>.js`; `modules/` in the handoff package): general systems in files of their own, each a `<script>` after the engine, its header its manual (read it there, or its section of the index). A kit carries the modules its slice names (`@modules` in its header); the full engine (`dist/my-3d2dge.js`; `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) carries them all.\n  - `grid`: square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges over heights and past allies, fields of view, `E.Fog`, groups, shapes of effect, threat maps, `E.GridMover`, `E.Turns` (speed, charge time, phases, ATB). ROGUELIKE, PUZZLE, TACTICS, ARCADE, ARTILLERY, RTS, SIM, 3D CRAWLER.\n  - `gen`: generated maps from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a TileMap reads. ROGUELIKE, ARCADE.\n  - `fight` (the full engine only): one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter` (frame data, hurt boxes from the rig's joints, guards, throws, cancels, juggles, specials by motion or charge, a meter, projectiles), `E.Match` (rounds, KO, the HUD, training), `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`. FIGHTING.\n  - `rooms`: a world of rooms on a grid of screens (`E.World`): walking between them at open edges, the explored map (`drawMap`, or `shellMap()` for `E.Shell`), gates that abilities or keys open, saves in `E.store`. METROIDVANIA.\n  - `mode7`: the SNES's Mode 7 (`E.Mode7`): a camera over a flat floor (a TileMap's, a function's or a canvas), a turning sky, billboards far to near (rigs, sprites, shapes, your drawing), fog, `project` and `toFloor`, split screens. KART.\n  - `road`: the pseudo-3D road (`E.Road`): segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance and hidden past a crest, the sky's parallax on curves. ROAD.\n  - `drive`: cars on the ground plane (`E.Car`: grip and slide, a kart's hop and drift boost, floor tags, walls and cars it bounces off) and races (`E.Race`: checkpoints along an `E.Path`, laps, places, drivers that pass and rubber-band, items, the HUD; the 3D engine's `world.vehicle` too). KART.\n  - `story`: dialogue as data (`E.Story`): lines with speakers and portraits, choices with conditions, flags and values, gotos, the game's events, a quest log, balloons over heads, a backlog, auto and skip; its state for a save. RPG, POINT-AND-CLICK, TACTICS.\n  - `rpg`: a role-playing game's rules (`E.RPG`): items, bags (slots or a grid of sizes), members with stats, levels and gear, status effects, a party (items used, rewards shared), shops, damage, random encounters by zone, list and card windows. RPG, 3D HORROR.\n  - `strategy` (the full engine only; needs `grid`): `E.Strategy` for real-time strategy (selection by click, box and group; queued orders; formations; production, resources, placement; fog, the minimap; an AI's build order and waves) and for sims `E.Clock` (days, the light), `E.needs` and `E.choose`, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`. RTS, SIM.\n  - `click` (the full engine only): point-and-click adventures (`E.Click`): painted rooms, walk areas with holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots with verbs, SCUMM's nine verbs, Sierra's icons, a verb coin or two buttons, an inventory, rules as data, exits, `story`'s talk; taps on a phone. POINT-AND-CLICK.\n  - `physics`: 2D rigid bodies (`E.Physics`): circles, boxes, polygons, segments, chains and arcs; bounce, friction, sensors, layers, sleep; fast balls swept, never passing through; the same world at any refresh rate; flippers, bumpers, slingshots, kickers. PINBALL, ARTILLERY.\n  - `terrain`: destructible ground in the side plane (`E.Terrain`): carved and added by circles and polygons, the ground below a point and its normal, rays for shots, walkers on slopes, a collider for `physics`. ARTILLERY.\n  - `songs`: the songbook (`E.Songbook`): songs by mood (field, town, battle, race, horror, chase, dance ...) and jingles (stage clear, game over, level up); `get(id)`, `find(mood)`. RHYTHM.\n  - `rhythm` (the full engine only): `E.Rhythm`: charts on a song's steps, judgments in ms of the music clock, combo, score, a life gauge, grades, holds, call and response, a note highway in any direction. RHYTHM.\n  - `arcade`: the coin-op's frame: scores that float up, extra lives, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode (a demo, the table, the title). ARCADE, SHOOTER, PINBALL.\n- **Everything running**: https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs\n\n## Before you hand the game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge` (between the markers in a single file; `game/game.js` in the handoff package). Only your game: no leftover starter menu or unused slices. No network, no libraries.\n- Title screen and game-over screen as scenes; `pausable: true` on gameplay scenes. No pause, settings, rebinding or full-screen code of your own (they're standard); prompts from `game.input.label(action)`.\n- The camera follows the player (`game.focus` every update) and shows the level; the HUD uses screen pixels, or `E.Shell`'s HTML for a modern game (the STEALTH kit; the handoff package's template).\n- Sound effects on jump, hit, pickup and death; music per scene.\n- The look target list above: backdrop, textured tiles, props, sized and dressed HD characters, effects, title font, lights. Compare your screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug, not with NES games.\n- If you can run commands: `node tools/check.mjs game/index.html` (your page; add `#scene` to jump to a scene). It presses start, plays, cycles the views, prints how much of the screen is filled and how many colors it uses, adds look notes (flat areas, checkerboard dithering, low contrast, a thin palette), and fails on any error. Act on every look note. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html#scene --steps \"wait:1500 press:Enter wait:800 rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records an animation frame by frame into one contact sheet: check every attack with it (a swing must wind up, strike, and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps).\n- Animation variety: every character has its own moves from `E.MOVES` (not one shared swing), enemies telegraph with a visible wind-up, and reactions play out (hurt, knockback, `down`, victory `cheer`).\n## 21d. Shell\n\n- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n- `new E.Shell({ theme })`: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font: a family, titleFont), and scale: the whole interface that many times (a number, or a function of the window) for a TV across a room; tv: true picks one by the window's width (14 px text at 875 px, up to 30 at 1,875)\n- `new E.Shell({ screens })`: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n- `new E.Shell({ store, meters })`: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n- `new E.Shell({ standard })`: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the settings' groups)\n- `shell.back()` †: what cancel does: the screen's own back(), or close it (back to the screen under it; closing the pause screen resumes)\n- `shell.dispose()` †: take its elements away and let go of the game (game.dispose() does it)\n- `shell.hide()` †: close every screen (and resume, when the menu paused the game)\n- `shell.hud(st = {})` †: the HUD, from this step's state (call it every step; a piece left out is hidden, and only what changed is redrawn)\n- `shell.hud({ objective })` †: { step: '01 / 04', title, text, at: { x, y, z }, from: hero } (the distance in meters, and above or below)\n- `shell.hud({ status, place, tip })` †: status: 'UNSEEN' or { text, color }; place: 'The Old Harbour'; tip: 'text' or { title, text }\n- `shell.hud({ bars, counts })` †: bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'bar' | 'pips', color }] (health, stamina, a meter); counts: [[label, value]]\n- `shell.hud({ prompt })` †: { key: 'E' (or act: 'interact': its key on the player's device), label, hint, progress: 0..1 }\n- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n- `shell.hud({ minimap })` †: { map, at (with facing), markers: [{ x, y, kind: 'dot' | 'diamond' | 'ring' | 'cross', color }], onClick(x, y) (the world point clicked), fog (an E.Fog: unseen cells black, seen ones dim), camera: true (the ground the screen shows) }\n- `shell.screen` †: the name of the open screen ('pause', 'settings', 'controls', ...), or null while the game plays\n- `shell.settings` †: the player's settings: game.settings (the standard ones and the game's own, by key)\n- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act, primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel), rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY', '#A heading'] (settings' rows, changed as on the settings screen) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates (the new spec over the old). 'settings' takes its items\n- `shell.show('title')` †: { name, subtitle, meta: [...], footer }\n- `shell.show('help')` †: { keys: [[key, what]], sections: [[heading, text]] }\n- `shell.show('results')` †: { emblem, stats: [[value, label]], medals: [[label, earned]] }\n- `shell.show('map')` †: { map, at, markers (a list or a function), labels: [[text, x, y]], onPick(x, y) }\n- `shell.toast(title, text = '', seconds = 4)` †: a message that slides in at the top for a few seconds\n- `shell.tutorial(title, text = '', seconds = 15)` †: a tutorial card (bottom left, dismissable) for some seconds; tutorial(null) takes it away\n- `shell.root = null` †: game: the game it is over; o: its options; root: its DOM element (null in a headless game)\n\n## 22. Gpu lighting (webgpu)\n\n- `game.gpu.active(view)` †: true when it lights frames in this view (ready, enabled and a ground view)\n- `game.gpu.snapshot()` †: offscreen mode only: read the last frame back as a PNG data URL\n- `game.gpu.status()` †: 'loading' | 'on' | 'off' | 'unavailable'\n- `game.gpu.adapterInfo` †: the GPU's description from the browser (vendor, architecture), or null\n- `game.gpu.ambient` †: the unlit color, [r, g, b] 0..1\n- `game.gpu.bands = 6` †: how many light bands (fewer is more retro)\n53:- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adapta [... omitted end of long line]\n69:- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n83:Every `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and th [... omitted end of long line]\n84:- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (wh [... omitted end of long line]\n110:- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n160:- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggl [... omitted end of long line]\n164:- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `p [... omitted end of long line]\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n312:A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or f [... omitted end of long line]\n313:- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} },  [... omitted end of long line]\n316:- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch [... omitted end of long line]\n364:- **The modules:** `grid` (square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges with climb and drop past allies, lines, symmetric fields of view, `E.Fog`, flood fills and groups, tactics' shapes of effect, fac [... omitted end of long line]\n441:- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })` and `inputs: [game.input, game.players[1]]` (a pad each, or half a keyboard each); the select and the menus read every player.\n452:- `new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [10000] })` keeps the score, lives, two players' turns (each one's stage in `p.save`), the continue, the tables with initials and the attract mode; `cab.up [... omitted end of long line]\n459:- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })`, `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'rows' })`, and in `draw` each viewport its own camera: `m7.draw(r, cams[r.viewport.i])`, `race.hud(r, players[r.viewport.i] [... omitted end of long line]\n564:**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with heights (banked bends, a hill, a hump to jump,  [... omitted end of long line]\n568:**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n569:- The field is a `TileMap` cage in `threequarter` (boards that cut low before the camera, nets); the ball and the players are `E.Body`s. The ball bounces off the ground and the boards (`bounce`), and  [... omitted end of long line]\n571:- The players a person doesn't run play by formation, by where the ball is: with it, the other one runs ahead on the far side; without it, the nearer presses and the other covers the goal. `game.allow [... omitted end of long line]\n580:- The arena is an `E.Grid` of hard blocks on even cells and soft blocks to break; players and foes are `E.GridMover`s (a turn asked early is kept).\n582:- Two to four players on one machine: `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, a mover and an input each (`game.players[i]`).\n660:- The camera follows the player and the level is visible. The HUD uses screen pixels (`r.text`, `r.overlay`), anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`, or for a modern game `E.Shell`'s HTML pieces (`ui.hud` every  [... omitted end of long line]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '83,123p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md && sed -n '311,325p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md && sed -n '1,130p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/platformer.js && cat vendor/ai-hub/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '83,123p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md && sed -n '311,325p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md && sed -n '1,130p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/platformer.js && cat vendor/ai-hub/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"Every `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n\nAdjust them:\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n  menu: {\n    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n  } });\nconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\nr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n```\n- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n\nWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n\n## Frame order\n\n`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n\n### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\nA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n\n### Lighting\n- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views, and on screen in side views and for PlatformMaps. The template game and the STEALTH slice show it. Its pass costs a few milliseconds a frame at 320x240 (more on a phone): a heavy scene can keep it for a computer, `matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches`, as the STEALTH slice does.\n- GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`) needs WebGPU and a TileMap. It adds:\n  - colored lights, and soft shadows cast by walls and `lights.caster(...)`;\n  - light that wraps around characters;\n  - glow from `px.glow`, emissive decals and actors, and `[r, g, b, glow]` floor texels;\n  - bloom and heat shimmer (`lights.heat`).\n// The PLATFORMER kit's game (a side-scroller: a night approach to a castle in five zones, a sword, a dash, candles, a 2.5D view; Castlevania, Mega Man X, Klonoa), readable: the starter's shell (src/starter/00-shell.js), then\n// the slice from line 159 (src/starter/20-platformer.js: its header says what it plays and how), then the start (90-start.js), as the\n// single-file kit holds them between GAME START and GAME END. platformer.html plays it; node kits/test.mjs platformer runs it headless.\n/* ============================== GAME START ==============================\n * MY-3D2DGE STARTER: a title menu and vertical slices, one per genre (a genre kit file holds one; the file with every\n * slice adds an animation lab). Each slice is a complete small game, written to be copied and changed: its own header\n * (below this shell) says what it plays, its keys and touch controls, its deep links and how it works.\n * FULL SCREEN, ANY SHAPE: res: 'ps1', expand: true. 320 x 240 is the least the screen shows; a wider or taller window\n * shows more world (r.W x r.H grows), so every HUD anchors to r.W and r.H, never to 320 and 240, and every level fills\n * what a wide screen or an upright phone shows. Prompts name the player's own keys: prompt('attack', 'Sword').\n * To make your own game: change the slice closest to your genre into yours, with its own title, play and game-over\n * scenes, and delete this menu and the slices you do not use; this shell (the game, its scenes, prompt, best and the\n * proof) stays unless you replace it. Deep links: #<slice> starts a slice, #<slice>/<part> jumps into a part.\n * ======================================================================== */\n(() => {\n'use strict';\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E;\n\n/* ---- SHELL: one game, several scenes. Each slice switches view, keys and resolution on entry ----\n * expand: the picture fills the window, any shape, with no bars: at least 320 x 240 (a slice's res), more on the long side */\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d' });\nconst P = game.particles, L = game.lights, A = game.audio;\nconst scenes = {};\nlet gpu = null;                               // WebGPU lighting, used by the adventure slice only\nconst best = (k, v) => { const old = E.store.get('best:' + k, 0); if (v > old) E.store.set('best:' + k, v); return Math.max(old, v); };\n// a prompt in the player's own controls, after rebinding: 'J Sword' on a keyboard, 'X Sword' on a pad, 'ATTACK Sword' (the\n// button's name) on a touch screen, where the buttons sit over the bottom corner: touch() says so, to move a prompt off it\nconst prompt = (a, word) => game.input.label(a) + ' ' + word, touch = () => game.input.lastDevice === 'touch';\n// each slice: its scene's id, its title entry, a line about it, and its family (the title groups them under it)\nconst SLICES = [\n  { id: 'adventure', label: 'ADVENTURE', about: 'TOP-DOWN ACTION ADVENTURE', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'pointclick', label: 'POINT-AND-CLICK', about: 'RELIGHT THE LIGHTHOUSE ON GULL ROCK', family: 'ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'platformer', label: 'PLATFORMER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING PLATFORMER', family: 'PLATFORM' },\n  { id: 'metroidvania', label: 'METROIDVANIA', about: 'A WORLD OF ROOMS, ITS MAP, ITS GATES', family: 'PLATFORM' },\n  { id: 'brawler', label: 'BRAWLER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING BEAT-EM-UP', family: 'FIGHTING' },\n  { id: 'fighting', label: 'FIGHTING', about: 'ONE ON ONE, BEST OF THREE', family: 'FIGHTING' },\n  { id: 'shooter', label: 'SHOOTER', about: 'VERTICAL SHOOT-EM-UP', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'arcade', label: 'ARCADE', about: 'A CABINET OF THREE CLASSICS', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'pinball', label: 'PINBALL', about: 'ONE TABLE: RAMP, MULTIBALL, JACKPOT', family: 'ARCADE' },\n  { id: 'kart', label: 'KART', about: 'MODE 7 KARTS, THREE LAPS', family: 'RACING' },\n  { id: 'road', label: 'ROAD', about: 'A COAST ROAD AGAINST THE CLOCK', family: 'RACING' },\n  { id: 'puzzle', label: 'PUZZLE', about: 'FALLING BLOCKS AND FALLING PAIRS', family: 'PUZZLE' },\n  { id: 'rhythm', label: 'RHYTHM', about: 'STEP UP! FOUR LANES, TWO SONGS', family: 'MUSIC' },\n  { id: 'rpg', label: 'RPG', about: 'A JOURNEY: TOWN, WORLD, CAVE, BATTLES', family: 'RPG' },\n  { id: 'roguelike', label: 'ROGUELIKE', about: 'A CRYPT OF TEN GENERATED FLOORS', family: 'RPG' },\n  { id: 'tactics', label: 'TACTICS', about: 'TAKE THE HILL FORT, TURN BY TURN', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'rts', label: 'RTS', about: 'GATHER GOLD, RAISE AN ARMY, BURN THEIR CAMP', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'artillery', label: 'ARTILLERY', about: 'TWO TEAMS OF THREE ON AN ISLAND THAT BLOWS APART', family: 'STRATEGY' },\n  { id: 'sim', label: 'SIM', about: 'BUILD A THEME PARK, KEEP THEM SMILING', family: 'SIMULATION' },\n  { id: 'stealth', label: 'STEALTH', about: 'ROOFTOP STEALTH AND PARKOUR', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\n  { id: 'sports', label: 'SPORTS', about: 'TWO AGAINST TWO IN THE CAGE', family: 'SPORTS' },\n  { id: 'animlab', label: 'ANIMATION LAB', about: 'EVERY MOVE, SKIN AND VIEW', family: 'TOOLS' }\n];\n/* the camera: each slice's scene says how the player may zoom it, camera: { zoom: [min, max] | false, start, tall }: the\n   engine zooms it with the mouse wheel, - and =, a pinch on a phone and Settings, Camera zoom (the player's own, kept, on\n   top of the slice's start: tall on a phone held upright). Keys every slice shares besides: V changes the camera view,\n   [ and ] turn it (camera.rotate), 0 resets it (the player's zoom too), G toggles GPU lighting, M mutes */\nconst camOpt = () => (game.scene && game.scene.camera) || {};\naddEventListener('keydown', e => {\n  if (e.repeat) return;\n  if (e.code === 'KeyV') { game.nextView(); game.note(game.view.label.toUpperCase() + ' VIEW'); }\n  else if (e.code === 'Digit0') { game.settings.set('zoom', 1); game.resetCamera(); game.note('CAMERA RESET'); }   // (resetCamera draws it at once)\n  else if ((e.code === 'BracketLeft' || e.code === 'BracketRight') && camOpt().rotate) { game.rotateView(e.code === 'BracketLeft' ? -45 : 45); game.note('TURN ' + game.yaw); }\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyM') A.mute();\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyG' && gpu) gpu.enabled = !gpu.enabled;\n});\n\n/* ---- TITLE: a lineup of HD heroes on a torch-lit castle ledge under a dusk sky. The ledge is wide enough for a wide\n * screen and sits on the screen's bottom edge whatever its height; the logo and menu hang from the top ---- */\nconst MID = 480, stage = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ['S'.repeat(60), 'S'.repeat(60)], legend: { S: 1 },\n  types: { 1: { style: 'stone', side: '#6a5a78', moss: '#4a6a3a' } }, edges: 'open' });\nconst titleBg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night');\nconst HEROES = [   // six heroes, each dressed with the HD rig options\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', armor: true, hair: 'short', cape: { len: 6, width: 6, seg: 2.6 }, colors: { cloth: '#3a5ab0', cape: '#b02a3a', capeIn: '#5a1020', hair: '#e0b050', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, outfit: 'tunic', hair: 'spiky', hat: { style: 'pointed', color: '#3aa050' }, colors: { cloth: '#3aa050', pants: '#e8dcc0', hair: '#e0b040', trim: '#8a5a2a' } }) },\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'bulky', weapon: null, hat: { style: 'band', color: '#e03a3a' }, colors: { cloth: '#f0f0f0', pants: '#2f5fd0' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#d83a2a', pants: '#2f5fd0', glove: '#f0f0f0' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'staff', outfit: 'robe', hair: 'ponytail', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#6a3ab0', hair: '#c8402a', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', hood: true, outfit: 'coat', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#e8e4dc', coat: '#d8d2c8', trim: '#b02a2a', belt: '#b02a2a', pants: '#4a4a5a', boot: '#3a2e2a' } }) }   // the stealth slice's hooded assassin\n];\nHEROES.forEach((h, i) => { h.x = MID + (i - 2.5) * 35; });\n// the corner's buttons (menu, full screen, the page's ? API) in game pixels: about 170 x 60 CSS pixels at the top right\nconst corner = () => { const S = game.screen, k = S.dpr / S.S; return [170 * k, 60 * k]; };\n// where a HUD's top-right corner ends: left of the corner's menu button while it stays there (a touch screen), else the edge\nconst cornerX = () => { const ca = touch() && game.cornerArea(); return ca && ca.y0 < 40 ? Math.floor(ca.x0) - 4 : game.W; };\n/* ---- THE PROOF (tools/kits-test.mjs runs each slice's twice and compares): proof(id, n, script, state, fresh) enters the\n * slice afresh on a game clock at 0, with Math.random seeded, runs n fixed steps of 1/120 s with script(i, key) pressing\n * keys (key.hold('right'), key.up('right'), key.tap('attack')), and gives a hash of what state() returns: the numbers that\n * say how the run went. fresh(), when given, runs first (seeded): it puts back what the slice keeps between visits. A\n * slice's run(n) is one (__game.scenes.adventure.run(600)): the same number every time ---- */\nconst proof = (id, n, script, state, fresh) => {\n  const was = Math.random, I = game.input, held = new Set();\n  const key = { hold: a => { held.add(a); I.hold(a); }, up: a => { held.delete(a); I.hold(a, false); }, tap: a => I.tap(a) };\n  Math.random = E.rng(7);\n  try {\n    if (fresh) fresh();\n    game.time = 0; game.timeScale = 1; game.go(id, null, { fade: 0 }); game.advance(1 / 120);\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { script(i, key); game.advance(1 / 120); }\n    const s = JSON.stringify(state()); let h = 2166136261;\n    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) h = Math.imul(h ^ s.charCodeAt(i), 16777619);\n    return h >>> 0;\n  } finally { for (const a of held) I.hold(a, false); Math.random = was; }\n};\n// a proof's walk: every `every` steps it lets go of the directions and holds the next of `dirs`\nconst walk = (i, key, dirs, every) => { if (i % every) return; for (const d of dirs) key.up(d); key.hold(dirs[(i / every) % dirs.length]); };\n// START picks too (the touch START, a pad's Start), as Enter does: any player's\nconst pickOnStart = m => { const P = game.players; if (P.some(I => I.pressed('start'))) { P.forEach(I => I.consumeAll()); m.onPick(m.i, m.items[m.i]); } };\n/** the title's menu for the slices in this file: grouped by family when there are several (FamilyMenu, in 05-families.js:\n *  the reference editions carry it, a genre kit of one slice doesn't), else a plain E.Menu */\nconst menuOf = (slices, o) => new Set(slices.map(s => s.family)).size > 1 ? new FamilyMenu(slices, o) : new E.Menu(game, slices.map(s => s.label), o);\nlet titleMenu = null;   // (made at the start, once the file's slices are known)\nscenes.title = {\n  view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'DEFAULT', res: 'ps1',\n  enter() { A.music('title'); game.resetCamera(); game.follow(null); game.cam.room = null; game.cam.bounds = null; if (gpu) gpu.enabled = false; L.enabled = false; },\n  update(dt) {\n    if (!titleMenu.update(dt)) pickOnStart(titleMenu);\n    game.focus(MID, 0, 32 + (game.H / 2 - 42) / -game.view.bz);   // the ledge's top 42 px above the screen's bottom edge\n    const t = game.time;\n    HEROES.forEach((h, i) => {\n      const u = (t * .45 + i * .37) % 3, swing = u < .5 ? { spec: { a0: 1.4, a1: -1.6, z0: 12, z1: 8, reach: 7.5 }, phase: u < .15 ? 'wind' : 'active', u: u < .15 ? u / .15 : (u - .15) / .35 } : null;\n      h.rig.update(dt, { x: h.x, y: 0, z: 32, facing: h.face, vx: 0, vy: 0, attack: swing, point: i === 3 && u < .6 });\n    });\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    const [cw, ch] = corner(), top = r.W / 2 + 132 > r.W - cw ? Math.round(ch) : 0;   // the logo drops below the corner's buttons where it would reach them (an upright phone)\n    titleMenu.y = 76 + top;\n    titleBg.draw(r);\n    stage.draw(r);\n    for (const x of [MID - 100, MID + 100]) r.prop('torch', x, 0, 32, { size: 1.3, light: false });\n    for (const x of [MID - 53, MID + 53]) r.prop('banner', x, -8, 64, { size: 1.1, color: '#8a1a2a' });\n# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card, longer if one asks for more time: what\n   the game is, how to win, the controls. The card is the hub's, from the\n   game's tags, or the game's own (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. A player can ask for more time, which adds\nten seconds; while more than half of them are asking, the countdown holds.\nBy default the hub shows its own card over the game. A game can make the\ncard its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"The title draw selected **Beauty and the Tall Tale**. I’ve pinned the engine and Hub client, and work is underway on the climbing rules, facade and character animation, and co-op networking. The game will have three four-floor sections followed by the rooftop rescue and plane escape.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'class Shell|class Settings|const SHELL|function shell|shell-title|shell-panel|className.*shell|\\\\.m3|\\\\.e-shell' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js | head -60 && sed -n '157,181p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md && sed -n '895,922p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && sed -n '956,975p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/package.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'class Shell|class Settings|const SHELL|function shell|shell-title|shell-panel|className.*shell|\\\\.m3|\\\\.e-shell' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js | head -60 && sed -n '157,181p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md && sed -n '895,922p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && sed -n '956,975p' vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/package.json\"","aggregated_output":"3087:class Settings {\n9363:const SHELL_CSS = `.esh{position:fixed;inset:0;pointer-events:none;z-index:20;font:14px/1.45 var(--font);color:var(--ink);--sat:env(safe-area-inset-top,0px);\n9410:const SHELL_THEME = { ink: '#f1e8d6', paper: 'rgba(14,18,22,.86)', accent: '#d9b77c', muted: '#a49d8c', danger: '#e8836a', line: 'rgba(217,183,124,.3)', font: 'system-ui, sans-serif', titleFont: 'Geor [... omitted end of long line]\n9412:const SHELL_GROUPS = ['Display', 'Sound', 'Comfort'];\n9415:class Shell {\n- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n\n### Players (two to four on one machine)\n- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n\n### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\nThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n- **Tweens** (Phaser's tweens, Unity's DOTween): `game.tween(target, { x: 10, y: 4 }, seconds, { ease, delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases a target's numbers to new values, from where they are when its delay ends; a door sliding, a title dropping in, a coin flying to the HUD, a camera move. `ease` is a name in `E.ease` (Penner's: `linear`, `inSine`, `outBack`, `outElastic`, `outBounce`, ...; `outQuad` by default) or a function of 0..1. It replaces a timer, a start value and a lerp in every update.\n- **Scripts** (Unity's coroutines): `game.script(function* (game) { ... })` runs a generator in the update step and waits at each `yield`: a number of seconds, a condition (`E.until(fn)` or a function), a tween, another script or a generator (run and waited for), `E.all([...])`, `E.any([...])`, or nothing (one step). A wave timeline is a loop (`for (const w of WAVES) { spawn(w); yield E.any([w.time, () => !enemies.length]); }`); a cutscene is its lines in order (`talk.say(...)`, `yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`, `yield game.tween(cam, ...)`), instead of a state machine with a timer per state. It runs at once to its first `yield`; `.cancel()` stops it and runs its `finally`.\n- **Paths:** `new E.Path(points, { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })` measures a line by distance along it: `at(d)` is a point with its heading (a moving platform's place, an enemy's dive, a rail, a camera track: `d = speed * time`), `nearest(x, y)` the distance along of the nearest point (a racer's progress round the lap, for places), `draw(r)` shows it while you build.\n- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind, amount, wind, lightning }` (rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand): drops fall through the world's height in ground views and in three layers of parallax in side and overhead views, splash or settle on the floor (`particles.ground`, the platforms' height in a side view), and lightning flashes (the player's Screen flashes setting) and thunders. It is cosmetic: its own seeded random stream, never `Math.random`, so replays and gameplay are untouched. `E.WEATHER` holds the kinds; add your own.\n- **Sprites** (frames, turns, swaps): `E.anim([...frames], fps, { loop, pingpong })` (or one sheet cut into `frames`) is played by `r.sprite(x, y, z, anim, { t })`; `rot` turns a sprite in whole pixels, cached in 16 or 32 steps as the era's games pre-rotated theirs (a ship facing its heading, a spinning blade); `scale` grows it nearest-neighbour; `E.recolor(spr, map)` is a palette swap (the second player, a stronger enemy).\n- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n\n### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n- ASCII `rows` and a `legend`: a number is a wall type, a string is a spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn }` combines them.\n- Wall `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH }`. `cut` lowers a wall when it stands between the camera and the floor.\n- `floorTex(x, y, floorTag)` colors the floor per pixel; it is baked once per view. Ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`.\n## 16b. Platformer\n\n- `new E.Platformer(o = {}) → hero`: a side-scroller character body with modern, forgiving controls (coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, optional double jump, wall jump, dash, ladders, knockback). const hero = new E.Platformer({ x, z, w: 8, h: 22 }); update: hero.update(dt, level, game.input) (or an intent object for enemies: { x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash }) then read hero.onGround, hero.jumped, hero.landed, hero.facing (1 or -1), hero.climbing, hero.air draw a Humanoid with rig.update(dt, hero.rigState()) Units: world units per second. One tile = 16. A 285 jump with gravity 800 clears about 3 tiles.\n- `hero.knock(dir, speed = 130, up = 170, stun = .3)`: push the body (getting hit): dir = -1 / 1\n- `hero.release(up = 0)` †: let go of a swing, keeping its speed (up: an extra upward speed)\n- `hero.rigState(extra)`: state for Humanoid.update: rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack }))\n- `hero.swingFrom(x, z, len)` †: hang from a point (a grapple's hook, a whip's ring): a pendulum from (x, z) to its hands, len long (the distance now by default); left and right pump it, jump lets go with its speed (release() does too)\n- `hero.update(dt, level, ctl = {}, solids)`: move it one step: ctl is game.input or an intent { x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash } (enemies, bots); solids: moving platforms [{ x, z, w, h, vx, vz }] to stand on\n- `hero.accel = 900`, `hero.airAccel = 650`, `hero.decel = 1100`: how fast it speeds up and slows down on the ground and in the air\n- `hero.air = false`: in the air, not climbing\n- `hero.airJumps = 0`: extra jumps in the air (1 = double jump)\n- `hero.buffer = .13`: seconds a jump pressed before landing is kept\n- `hero.climb = 70`: ladder speed\n- `hero.climbing = false`: on a ladder\n- `hero.coyote = .09`: seconds a jump still works after walking off a ledge\n- `hero.cut = .45`: the share of upward speed kept when jump is let go early (short hops)\n- `hero.dash = 0`: dash speed (0 = no dash)\n- `hero.dashTime = .22`: seconds a dash lasts\n- `hero.dashing = 0`: seconds of dash left\n- `hero.drop = 0`: seconds left of dropping through a one-way platform\n- `hero.facing = 1`: 1 right, -1 left\n- `hero.fallGravity = 1150`: while falling (snappier landings)\n- `hero.gravity = 800`: while rising\n- `hero.h = 22`, `hero.w = 8`: its box (x is its center, z its feet)\n- `hero.hanging` †: the ledge it hangs from ({ dir, top, cx }), or null\n- `hero.hitWall`: keep wall contact while holding toward it\n- `hero.jump = 285`: the jump's starting speed\n- `hero.jumped = false`, `hero.landed = false`: it jumped or landed this step\n## 17b. Actions\n\n- `E.ACTIONS: climbUp, climbJump, vault, hopDown, roll, getUp, takedown` †: the actions E.Action plays. Add your own: E.ACTIONS.name = { beats, setup, at }; setup(A) and at(A, beat, u) get the action: its options A.o, A.start (where the body began), A.body; setup may change A.beats. Play one: mover.play('takedown', { other })\n- `new E.Action(name, o = {}) → action`: new E.Action(name, { body, to, other, dir, dist, top, aerial, reverse, speed: 1 }): play E.ACTIONS[name] on a body (and on o.other for a paired action). It moves them itself: run update(dt) every step instead of their physics, and feed rigState() to the rig. Each body ends standing where the action put it\n- `action.canCancel` †: true during a beat marked as one the action may be cut short in (spec.cancel)\n- `action.cancel()` †: stop now: the bodies stay where they are\n- `action.invulnerable` †: true while hits should miss (spec.safe: [from, to] of the action's progress)\n- `action.otherState(extra)` †: the rig state for the other body of a paired action (the victim of a takedown)\n- `action.rigState(extra)` †: the rig state for the body (merge extra over it): x, y, z, its speed, facing and the beat's pose\n- `action.u` †: 0..1 through the whole action\n- `action.update(dt)` †: move the bodies one step; returns true while the action runs (false once it has ended)\n- `action.beat` †: the name of the beat playing now\n- `action.beats` †: [[name, seconds], ...]\n- `action.body`, `action.other` †: the bodies it moves\n- `action.done = false` †: it has ended (or was cancelled)\n- `action.duration` †: the action's length in seconds (o.speed shortens it)\n- `action.name` †: the action's name\n- `action.o` †: the options it was given\n- `action.spec` †: its E.ACTIONS entry\n- `action.start` †: where the body was when it began\n{\n  \"name\": \"my-3d2dge-handoff\",\n  \"version\": \"0.89.1\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"description\": \"my-3D2dge for coding agents: the engine, its card, index and guide, the tools and a template game\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"node game/test.mjs\",\n    \"check\": \"node tools/check.mjs game/index.html\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\"\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
